<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="theme-color" content="#142d4c"><meta name="description" content="Browse 596 federal, state, and local assistance program guides."><title>Assistance Program Guides — American Aid Network</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/site.css"></head><body><?php require __DIR__ . "/../includes/site-header.php"; ?><main><section class="guide-index"><div class="wrap narrow"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a><span>›</span><b>Program Guides</b></div><h1>Assistance Program Guides</h1><p>One guide per program, compiled from official sources: who qualifies, how to apply, and where to get answers.</p><div class="state-search"><span>⌕</span><input data-filter="programs" aria-label="Search program guides" placeholder="Search 596 program guides"></div><div data-list="programs"><section class="index-group" data-search="federal 211 — community resource helpline free, confidential helpline that connects you with local assistance programs of every kind — rent, utilities, food, shelter, healthcare, and more. available 24/7 in most areas, in many languages. 988 suicide &amp; crisis lifeline free, confidential support 24/7 for anyone in emotional distress or crisis. call or text 988. feeding america food bank locator find your nearest food bank and free food distribution. no paperwork needed at most pantries. hud housing counseling free or low-cost hud-approved counselors who help with eviction prevention, foreclosure, renting, and homelessness — and know every local housing program. health insurance marketplace (healthcare.gov) subsidized health insurance if you don&#39;t qualify for medicaid. losing a job or income counts as a special enrollment event. liheap (energy bill assistance) federal program that helps pay heating and cooling bills, and can help in a shutoff emergency. run by state and local agencies. lawhelp.org (free legal aid) find free legal aid in your state for evictions, debt collection, benefits denials, and family issues. lifeline (phone &amp; internet discount) monthly discount on phone or internet service for low-income households. medicaid &amp; chip free or very low-cost health coverage for people with limited income, including children (chip), pregnant women, seniors, and people with disabilities. missingmoney (unclaimed property search) official state-endorsed search for money owed to you: old deposits, uncashed checks, forgotten accounts. free — never pay anyone to claim it. nfcc nonprofit credit counseling free or low-cost counseling from nonprofit agencies: budgeting, debt management plans, and negotiating with creditors. a safe alternative to for-profit &quot;debt relief&quot; companies. snap (food stamps) monthly benefits on an ebt card to buy groceries. the largest food assistance program in the country — millions of eligible people never apply. social security (ssi / ssdi) ssi provides monthly payments to people 65+ or with disabilities who have very limited income. ssdi covers workers who become disabled. tanf (cash assistance for families) monthly cash assistance and work support for very-low-income families with children. run by states under different names. usagov benefit finder official government screening tool that checks 1,000+ federal and state programs you may qualify for in about 10 minutes. va benefits (veterans) disability compensation, pensions, healthcare, housing help, and more for veterans and eligible family members. wic (women, infants &amp; children) food benefits, nutrition support, and healthcare referrals for pregnant women, new mothers, and children under 5."><h2>Federal programs (17)</h2><article><a href="/programs/211-community-resource-helpline/">211 — Community Resource Helpline</a><p>Free, confidential helpline that connects you with local assistance programs of every kind — rent, utilities, food, shelter, healthcare, and more. Available 24/7 in most areas, in many languages.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/988-suicide-and-crisis-lifeline/">988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline</a><p>Free, confidential support 24/7 for anyone in emotional distress or crisis. Call or text 988.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/feeding-america-food-bank-locator/">Feeding America Food Bank Locator</a><p>Find your nearest food bank and free food distribution. No paperwork needed at most pantries.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/hud-housing-counseling/">HUD Housing Counseling</a><p>Free or low-cost HUD-approved counselors who help with eviction prevention, foreclosure, renting, and homelessness — and know every local housing program.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/health-insurance-marketplace-healthcare-gov/">Health Insurance Marketplace (Healthcare.gov)</a><p>Subsidized health insurance if you don&#39;t qualify for Medicaid. Losing a job or income counts as a special enrollment event.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/liheap-energy-bill-assistance/">LIHEAP (Energy Bill Assistance)</a><p>Federal program that helps pay heating and cooling bills, and can help in a shutoff emergency. Run by state and local agencies.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/lawhelp-org-free-legal-aid/">LawHelp.org (Free Legal Aid)</a><p>Find free legal aid in your state for evictions, debt collection, benefits denials, and family issues.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/lifeline-phone-and-internet-discount/">Lifeline (Phone &amp; Internet Discount)</a><p>Monthly discount on phone or internet service for low-income households.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/medicaid-and-chip/">Medicaid &amp; CHIP</a><p>Free or very low-cost health coverage for people with limited income, including children (CHIP), pregnant women, seniors, and people with disabilities.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/missingmoney-unclaimed-property-search/">MissingMoney (Unclaimed Property Search)</a><p>Official state-endorsed search for money owed to you: old deposits, uncashed checks, forgotten accounts. Free — never pay anyone to claim it.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/nfcc-nonprofit-credit-counseling/">NFCC Nonprofit Credit Counseling</a><p>Free or low-cost counseling from nonprofit agencies: budgeting, debt management plans, and negotiating with creditors. A safe alternative to for-profit &quot;debt relief&quot; companies.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/snap-food-stamps/">SNAP (Food Stamps)</a><p>Monthly benefits on an EBT card to buy groceries. The largest food assistance program in the country — millions of eligible people never apply.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/social-security-ssi-ssdi/">Social Security (SSI / SSDI)</a><p>SSI provides monthly payments to people 65+ or with disabilities who have very limited income. SSDI covers workers who become disabled.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/tanf-cash-assistance-for-families/">TANF (Cash Assistance for Families)</a><p>Monthly cash assistance and work support for very-low-income families with children. Run by states under different names.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/usagov-benefit-finder/">USAGov Benefit Finder</a><p>Official government screening tool that checks 1,000+ federal and state programs you may qualify for in about 10 minutes.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/va-benefits-veterans/">VA Benefits (Veterans)</a><p>Disability compensation, pensions, healthcare, housing help, and more for veterans and eligible family members.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/wic-women-infants-and-children/">WIC (Women, Infants &amp; Children)</a><p>Food benefits, nutrition support, and healthcare referrals for pregnant women, new mothers, and children under 5.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="alabama 211 connects alabama dial 2-1-1 from any alabama phone, or call 1-888-421-1266, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. 211 connects alabama answers 24 hours a day, every day of the year. you can also text your zip code, or the keyword 211know, to 898211. all kids (alabama chip) all kids is alabama&#39;s low-cost health coverage for children under 19 in families that earn too much for medicaid but still need affordable coverage, reaching up to 312 percent of the federal poverty level. it covers checkups, immunizations, doctor and hospital visits, prescriptions, dental, vision, and mental health care through blue cross blue shield of alabama. apply online or call all kids at 1-888-373-5437. alabama community action agencies community action agencies are the local nonprofits that pull together help for low-income alabama families in one place: utility and energy assistance including liheap, weatherization, and often emergency aid, food, and job support. find the agency serving your county through the community action association of alabama&#39;s agency locator, or dial 2-1-1. alabama family assistance (tanf cash assistance) family assistance is alabama&#39;s tanf cash assistance for low-income families with a child under age 18, or 19 if the child is a full-time student. most parents take part in the jobs program, which helps you find and keep a job. apply online through onealabama at one.alabama.gov or at your county department of human resources office. cash comes on an ebt card. alabama medicaid alabama medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant women, parents with very low income, older adults, and people with disabilities. alabama has not expanded medicaid, so adults without dependent children generally do not qualify no matter how low their income. call the medicaid recipient call center at 1-800-362-1504, monday through friday, 8am to 4:30pm, to ask questions or find the district office that takes applications. alabama snap (food assistance) snap is alabama&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. apply online through mydhr at mydhr.alabama.gov, run by the alabama department of human resources, or at your county dhr office. older adults with no earned income can use a shorter application through the alabama elderly simplified application project. alabama wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum women, infants, and children under age 5. alabama wic runs through county health departments of the alabama department of public health. contact your county health department for an appointment, or call the statewide wic line at 1-888-942-4673. liheap energy assistance (alabama) the low-income home energy assistance program helps low-income alabama households pay home heating and cooling costs, and it can step in during an energy crisis such as a shutoff. the alabama department of economic and community affairs funds liheap but does not take applications; you apply through the community action agency serving your county. find your agency through adeca, or dial 2-1-1. legal services alabama legal services alabama provides free civil legal help to low-income residents on the noncriminal problems that upend a household: eviction and housing, domestic violence, public benefits, consumer and debt issues, and more. apply online at legalservicesalabama.org, or call the statewide intake line at 1-866-456-4995, monday through friday, 8:30am to 4:30pm. unemployment compensation (alabama) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the alabama department of labor. file online through the claimant portal at uiclaimantportal.labor.alabama.gov, which is the fastest way. for questions about a claim&#39;s eligibility, call 1-855-234-2856. weatherization assistance program (alabama) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible alabama households: an energy audit by trained staff, then improvements like air sealing and insulation that lower future bills, along with health and safety checks. the alabama department of economic and community affairs runs the program through local agencies. contact the agency serving your county to apply, since adeca does not take applications directly."><h2>Alabama programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/211-connects-alabama/">211 Connects Alabama</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Alabama phone, or call 1-888-421-1266, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. 211 Connects Alabama answers 24 hours a day, every day of the year. You can also text your zip code, or the keyword 211KNOW, to 898211.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/all-kids-alabama-chip/">ALL Kids (Alabama CHIP)</a><p>ALL Kids is Alabama&#39;s low-cost health coverage for children under 19 in families that earn too much for Medicaid but still need affordable coverage, reaching up to 312 percent of the federal poverty level. It covers checkups, immunizations, doctor and hospital visits, prescriptions, dental, vision, and mental health care through Blue Cross Blue Shield of Alabama. Apply online or call ALL Kids at 1-888-373-5437.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/alabama-community-action-agencies/">Alabama Community Action Agencies</a><p>Community Action Agencies are the local nonprofits that pull together help for low-income Alabama families in one place: utility and energy assistance including LIHEAP, weatherization, and often emergency aid, food, and job support. Find the agency serving your county through the Community Action Association of Alabama&#39;s agency locator, or dial 2-1-1.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/alabama-family-assistance-tanf-cash-assistance/">Alabama Family Assistance (TANF Cash Assistance)</a><p>Family Assistance is Alabama&#39;s TANF cash assistance for low-income families with a child under age 18, or 19 if the child is a full-time student. Most parents take part in the JOBS Program, which helps you find and keep a job. Apply online through OneAlabama at one.alabama.gov or at your county Department of Human Resources office. Cash comes on an EBT card.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/alabama-medicaid/">Alabama Medicaid</a><p>Alabama Medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant women, parents with very low income, older adults, and people with disabilities. Alabama has not expanded Medicaid, so adults without dependent children generally do not qualify no matter how low their income. Call the Medicaid Recipient Call Center at 1-800-362-1504, Monday through Friday, 8am to 4:30pm, to ask questions or find the district office that takes applications.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/alabama-snap-food-assistance/">Alabama SNAP (Food Assistance)</a><p>SNAP is Alabama&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. Apply online through MyDHR at mydhr.alabama.gov, run by the Alabama Department of Human Resources, or at your county DHR office. Older adults with no earned income can use a shorter application through the Alabama Elderly Simplified Application Project.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/alabama-wic/">Alabama WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum women, infants, and children under age 5. Alabama WIC runs through county health departments of the Alabama Department of Public Health. Contact your county health department for an appointment, or call the statewide WIC line at 1-888-942-4673.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/liheap-energy-assistance-alabama/">LIHEAP Energy Assistance (Alabama)</a><p>The Low-Income Home Energy Assistance Program helps low-income Alabama households pay home heating and cooling costs, and it can step in during an energy crisis such as a shutoff. The Alabama Department of Economic and Community Affairs funds LIHEAP but does not take applications; you apply through the community action agency serving your county. Find your agency through ADECA, or dial 2-1-1.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/legal-services-alabama/">Legal Services Alabama</a><p>Legal Services Alabama provides free civil legal help to low-income residents on the noncriminal problems that upend a household: eviction and housing, domestic violence, public benefits, consumer and debt issues, and more. Apply online at legalservicesalabama.org, or call the statewide intake line at 1-866-456-4995, Monday through Friday, 8:30am to 4:30pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-compensation-alabama/">Unemployment Compensation (Alabama)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Alabama Department of Labor. File online through the Claimant Portal at uiclaimantportal.labor.alabama.gov, which is the fastest way. For questions about a claim&#39;s eligibility, call 1-855-234-2856.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-alabama/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Alabama)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible Alabama households: an energy audit by trained staff, then improvements like air sealing and insulation that lower future bills, along with health and safety checks. The Alabama Department of Economic and Community Affairs runs the program through local agencies. Contact the agency serving your county to apply, since ADECA does not take applications directly.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="alaska alaska 2-1-1 dial 2-1-1 from any alaska phone for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, health care, and more. alaska 2-1-1 is a statewide service of united way of anchorage that refers you to the programs and funds near you. you can also search at alaska211.org. alaska child care assistance program the child care assistance program helps low-income working families in alaska pay for child care, funded through the federal child care development fund. it lowers what you pay a licensed or approved provider so you can work, look for work, or go to school. call the child care program office at 907-269-4500 or toll-free 888-268-4632 to start. alaska heating assistance program (hap / liheap) the heating assistance program, hap, is alaska&#39;s liheap: a one-time seasonal payment sent straight to your heating vendor as a credit on your account. the heating season runs october 1 through april 30, and the deadline for regular, non-crisis help is april 30. apply through the alaska connect portal or a local public assistance office, or call 800-478-7778. alaska legal services corporation alaska legal services corporation provides free civil legal help to low-income alaskans on housing, family, consumer, public benefits, health care, and alaska native law matters. call the statewide intake line at 1-888-478-2572 to reach the office serving your area, or apply online at alsc-law.org/intake. offices are open monday through friday, 9am to 5pm. alaska medicaid &amp; denali kidcare alaska medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, families, seniors, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. alaska expanded medicaid in 2015 to cover adults ages 19 to 64 without dependent children, and denali kidcare covers children and pregnant people. apply through healthcare.gov or the alaska connect portal, or call the virtual contact center at 800-478-7778. alaska snap (food assistance) snap is alaska&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money loaded onto an alaska quest ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. the fastest way to apply is online through the alaska connect portal, linked from the division of public assistance snap page. you can also apply by phone through the virtual contact center at 800-478-7778 (tdd or alaska relay 7-1-1) or at a local public assistance office. alaska senior benefits program the alaska senior benefits program pays a monthly cash benefit of $125, $175, or $250 to low-income alaskans age 65 and older, with the amount set by your other income. savings and resources do not count. apply online through the alaska connect portal or by phone at 800-478-7778, and the benefit arrives by direct deposit or an alaska quest ebt card. alaska temporary assistance program (cash assistance) the alaska temporary assistance program, atap, is the state&#39;s tanf program: monthly cash assistance and work services for low-income families with children. most adults follow a work first plan of job search, training, or work experience while the cash steadies the household. apply online through the alaska connect portal or by phone through the virtual contact center at 800-478-7778. alaska wic wic gives free healthy foods, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children under age 5. alaska wic runs through local clinics across the state. call the state wic office at 907-465-3100 to find your local clinic and start, or look up a clinic by region online. unemployment insurance (alaska) unemployment insurance pays temporary weekly benefits if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the alaska department of labor and workforce development. the fastest way to file is online through myalaska at my.alaska.gov. you can also reach a ui claim center by phone: anchorage 907-269-4700, fairbanks 907-451-2871, or juneau 907-465-5552, open 10am to 3pm, monday through friday. weatherization assistance (alaska housing finance corporation) alaska&#39;s weatherization program makes energy-efficiency repairs at no cost to income-eligible households: air sealing, insulation, and heating-system work that lowers future bills. the alaska housing finance corporation, ahfc, runs it through regional providers, and both homeowners and renters can qualify, including condos. contact your regional weatherization provider to apply, or call ahfc at 907-338-6100 or 800-478-2432 in alaska."><h2>Alaska programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/alaska-2-1-1/">Alaska 2-1-1</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Alaska phone for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, health care, and more. Alaska 2-1-1 is a statewide service of United Way of Anchorage that refers you to the programs and funds near you. You can also search at alaska211.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/alaska-child-care-assistance-program/">Alaska Child Care Assistance Program</a><p>The Child Care Assistance Program helps low-income working families in Alaska pay for child care, funded through the federal Child Care Development Fund. It lowers what you pay a licensed or approved provider so you can work, look for work, or go to school. Call the Child Care Program Office at 907-269-4500 or toll-free 888-268-4632 to start.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/alaska-heating-assistance-program-hap-liheap/">Alaska Heating Assistance Program (HAP / LIHEAP)</a><p>The Heating Assistance Program, HAP, is Alaska&#39;s LIHEAP: a one-time seasonal payment sent straight to your heating vendor as a credit on your account. The heating season runs October 1 through April 30, and the deadline for regular, non-crisis help is April 30. Apply through the Alaska Connect portal or a local Public Assistance office, or call 800-478-7778.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/alaska-legal-services-corporation/">Alaska Legal Services Corporation</a><p>Alaska Legal Services Corporation provides free civil legal help to low-income Alaskans on housing, family, consumer, public benefits, health care, and Alaska Native law matters. Call the statewide intake line at 1-888-478-2572 to reach the office serving your area, or apply online at alsc-law.org/intake. Offices are open Monday through Friday, 9am to 5pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/alaska-medicaid-and-denali-kidcare/">Alaska Medicaid &amp; Denali KidCare</a><p>Alaska Medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, families, seniors, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. Alaska expanded Medicaid in 2015 to cover adults ages 19 to 64 without dependent children, and Denali KidCare covers children and pregnant people. Apply through HealthCare.gov or the Alaska Connect portal, or call the Virtual Contact Center at 800-478-7778.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/alaska-snap-food-assistance/">Alaska SNAP (Food Assistance)</a><p>SNAP is Alaska&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money loaded onto an Alaska Quest EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. The fastest way to apply is online through the Alaska Connect portal, linked from the Division of Public Assistance SNAP page. You can also apply by phone through the Virtual Contact Center at 800-478-7778 (TDD or Alaska Relay 7-1-1) or at a local Public Assistance office.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/alaska-senior-benefits-program/">Alaska Senior Benefits Program</a><p>The Alaska Senior Benefits Program pays a monthly cash benefit of $125, $175, or $250 to low-income Alaskans age 65 and older, with the amount set by your other income. Savings and resources do not count. Apply online through the Alaska Connect portal or by phone at 800-478-7778, and the benefit arrives by direct deposit or an Alaska Quest EBT card.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/alaska-temporary-assistance-program-cash-assistance/">Alaska Temporary Assistance Program (Cash Assistance)</a><p>The Alaska Temporary Assistance Program, ATAP, is the state&#39;s TANF program: monthly cash assistance and work services for low-income families with children. Most adults follow a Work First plan of job search, training, or work experience while the cash steadies the household. Apply online through the Alaska Connect portal or by phone through the Virtual Contact Center at 800-478-7778.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/alaska-wic/">Alaska WIC</a><p>WIC gives free healthy foods, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children under age 5. Alaska WIC runs through local clinics across the state. Call the state WIC office at 907-465-3100 to find your local clinic and start, or look up a clinic by region online.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-alaska/">Unemployment Insurance (Alaska)</a><p>Unemployment Insurance pays temporary weekly benefits if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Alaska Department of Labor and Workforce Development. The fastest way to file is online through myAlaska at my.alaska.gov. You can also reach a UI Claim Center by phone: Anchorage 907-269-4700, Fairbanks 907-451-2871, or Juneau 907-465-5552, open 10am to 3pm, Monday through Friday.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-alaska-housing-finance-corporation/">Weatherization Assistance (Alaska Housing Finance Corporation)</a><p>Alaska&#39;s Weatherization program makes energy-efficiency repairs at no cost to income-eligible households: air sealing, insulation, and heating-system work that lowers future bills. The Alaska Housing Finance Corporation, AHFC, runs it through regional providers, and both homeowners and renters can qualify, including condos. Contact your regional weatherization provider to apply, or call AHFC at 907-338-6100 or 800-478-2432 in Alaska.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="arizona 2-1-1 arizona dial 2-1-1 from any arizona phone, or call 1-877-211-8661 from anywhere in the state, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, health, and more. 2-1-1 arizona, a program of solari, answers from 9am to 7pm, seven days a week, in english and spanish. you can also search at 211arizona.org. arizona cash assistance (tanf) cash assistance is arizona&#39;s tanf program: a modest monthly cash payment for low-income families with children, run by des. arizona holds adults to a lifetime limit of 12 months of cash assistance, one of the shortest in the country, and most adults take part in the jobs program. apply online through health-e-arizona plus at healthearizonaplus.gov or by phone at 1-855-432-7587. arizona liheap &amp; power az (utility assistance) liheap helps low-income arizona households pay home energy bills, run by des through local community action agencies. standard help is up to $640 toward your utility bill, and a household facing a shutoff can qualify for an added one-time crisis payment of up to $500 per 12-month period. apply through the des online portal, or contact your local community action agency, which takes applications and can help you complete them. arizona legal aid (community legal services &amp; azlawhelp) arizona&#39;s legal aid programs give free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing, eviction, family, public benefits, and consumer problems. community legal services covers maricopa, la paz, mohave, yavapai, and yuma counties at 1-800-852-9075. for anywhere in the state, apply through azlawhelp.org or call 1-866-637-5341 to be routed to the program serving your area. arizona medicaid (ahcccs) ahcccs, the arizona health care cost containment system, is the state&#39;s medicaid program: free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. apply online through health-e-arizona plus at healthearizonaplus.gov, or call 1-855-432-7587 to apply by phone. arizona nutrition assistance (snap) nutrition assistance is what arizona calls snap: monthly food money on an ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. the department of economic security (des) runs it. apply online through health-e-arizona plus at healthearizonaplus.gov, which also covers ahcccs health coverage and cash assistance on one application, or call 1-855-432-7587. arizona wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. the arizona department of health services runs it through local clinics. call 1-800-252-5942 to find the clinic nearest you, or start online at azdhs.gov. kidscare (arizona chip) kidscare is arizona&#39;s children&#39;s health insurance program (chip), run by ahcccs: low-cost health coverage for children under 19 whose family earns too much for regular ahcccs but still needs affordable coverage. kidscare monthly premiums are paused until further notice. apply online through health-e-arizona plus at healthearizonaplus.gov. summer utility shutoff protection (arizona) arizona bars its regulated electric utilities from disconnecting your power during the hottest months. under arizona corporation commission rules, companies like arizona public service, tucson electric power, and uns electric cannot shut off service from june 1 through october 15, or when high heat is forecast. your bill still adds up during the moratorium, so call your utility about a payment plan and apply for liheap. to report a wrongful disconnection, call acc consumer services at 1-800-222-7000. unemployment insurance (arizona) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the arizona department of economic security. you file online, since des has no walk-in unemployment offices, then file a weekly claim for each week you want to be paid. the ui call center is 1-877-600-2722, monday through friday, 8am to 5pm mountain standard time. weatherization assistance program (arizona) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible arizona households: insulation, air sealing, and heating and cooling improvements that lower future bills and make the home safer in extreme heat. the arizona department of housing runs it through local providers. apply by contacting the weatherization provider for your county, listed on the department of housing program page."><h2>Arizona programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/2-1-1-arizona/">2-1-1 Arizona</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Arizona phone, or call 1-877-211-8661 from anywhere in the state, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, health, and more. 2-1-1 Arizona, a program of Solari, answers from 9am to 7pm, seven days a week, in English and Spanish. You can also search at 211arizona.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/arizona-cash-assistance-tanf/">Arizona Cash Assistance (TANF)</a><p>Cash Assistance is Arizona&#39;s TANF program: a modest monthly cash payment for low-income families with children, run by DES. Arizona holds adults to a lifetime limit of 12 months of Cash Assistance, one of the shortest in the country, and most adults take part in the Jobs Program. Apply online through Health-e-Arizona Plus at healthearizonaplus.gov or by phone at 1-855-432-7587.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/arizona-liheap-and-power-az-utility-assistance/">Arizona LIHEAP &amp; Power AZ (Utility Assistance)</a><p>LIHEAP helps low-income Arizona households pay home energy bills, run by DES through local Community Action Agencies. Standard help is up to $640 toward your utility bill, and a household facing a shutoff can qualify for an added one-time crisis payment of up to $500 per 12-month period. Apply through the DES online portal, or contact your local Community Action Agency, which takes applications and can help you complete them.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/arizona-legal-aid-community-legal-services-and-azlawhelp/">Arizona Legal Aid (Community Legal Services &amp; AZLawHelp)</a><p>Arizona&#39;s legal aid programs give free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing, eviction, family, public benefits, and consumer problems. Community Legal Services covers Maricopa, La Paz, Mohave, Yavapai, and Yuma counties at 1-800-852-9075. For anywhere in the state, apply through AZLawHelp.org or call 1-866-637-5341 to be routed to the program serving your area.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/arizona-medicaid-ahcccs/">Arizona Medicaid (AHCCCS)</a><p>AHCCCS, the Arizona Health Care Cost Containment System, is the state&#39;s Medicaid program: free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. Apply online through Health-e-Arizona Plus at healthearizonaplus.gov, or call 1-855-432-7587 to apply by phone.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/arizona-nutrition-assistance-snap/">Arizona Nutrition Assistance (SNAP)</a><p>Nutrition Assistance is what Arizona calls SNAP: monthly food money on an EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. The Department of Economic Security (DES) runs it. Apply online through Health-e-Arizona Plus at healthearizonaplus.gov, which also covers AHCCCS health coverage and Cash Assistance on one application, or call 1-855-432-7587.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/arizona-wic/">Arizona WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. The Arizona Department of Health Services runs it through local clinics. Call 1-800-252-5942 to find the clinic nearest you, or start online at azdhs.gov.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/kidscare-arizona-chip/">KidsCare (Arizona CHIP)</a><p>KidsCare is Arizona&#39;s Children&#39;s Health Insurance Program (CHIP), run by AHCCCS: low-cost health coverage for children under 19 whose family earns too much for regular AHCCCS but still needs affordable coverage. KidsCare monthly premiums are paused until further notice. Apply online through Health-e-Arizona Plus at healthearizonaplus.gov.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/summer-utility-shutoff-protection-arizona/">Summer Utility Shutoff Protection (Arizona)</a><p>Arizona bars its regulated electric utilities from disconnecting your power during the hottest months. Under Arizona Corporation Commission rules, companies like Arizona Public Service, Tucson Electric Power, and UNS Electric cannot shut off service from June 1 through October 15, or when high heat is forecast. Your bill still adds up during the moratorium, so call your utility about a payment plan and apply for LIHEAP. To report a wrongful disconnection, call ACC Consumer Services at 1-800-222-7000.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-arizona/">Unemployment Insurance (Arizona)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Arizona Department of Economic Security. You file online, since DES has no walk-in unemployment offices, then file a weekly claim for each week you want to be paid. The UI Call Center is 1-877-600-2722, Monday through Friday, 8am to 5pm Mountain Standard Time.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-arizona/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Arizona)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible Arizona households: insulation, air sealing, and heating and cooling improvements that lower future bills and make the home safer in extreme heat. The Arizona Department of Housing runs it through local providers. Apply by contacting the weatherization provider for your county, listed on the Department of Housing program page.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="arkansas arhome (arkansas medicaid expansion) arhome, arkansas health and opportunity for me, is arkansas&#39;s expanded medicaid for low-income adults ages 19 to 64. it uses medicaid dollars to enroll most members in a private qualified health plan that covers doctor visits, prescriptions, and hospital care. apply online through access arkansas at access.arkansas.gov, or call 1-855-372-1084. arkids first (children&#39;s health coverage) arkids first is arkansas&#39;s health coverage for children, combining medicaid as arkids a and chip as arkids b so kids get checkups, shots, dental, vision, and hospital care. apply online through access arkansas at access.arkansas.gov, or call 1-855-372-1084. children can qualify even when a parent does not. arkansas 211 dial 2-1-1 for free, confidential help finding local assistance with food, housing, rent, utilities, and childcare. arkansas 211 is run by hark at the excellerate foundation and currently answers calls from benton, madison, and washington counties in northwest arkansas, with service expanding. you can also call 1-866-489-6983 around the clock, or text your zip code to 898211. arkansas liheap (energy assistance) the low income home energy assistance program, liheap, helps arkansas households pay heating and cooling bills, with extra crisis help when your power or heat is about to be shut off. applications are taken by local community action agencies, not by a state office. find the agency serving your county through the arkansas community action agencies association at acaaa.org, or call 501-372-0807. arkansas snap (food assistance) snap is arkansas&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. apply online through access arkansas at access.arkansas.gov, the state&#39;s one application for snap, medicaid, and tea cash assistance, or at your local department of human services county office. you can also get help by phone at 1-855-372-1084. arkansas tea (cash assistance) transitional employment assistance, tea, is arkansas&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children, paired with the work pays employment program. apply online through access arkansas at access.arkansas.gov or at your local dhs county office. tea carries a work requirement and a time limit on how long you can receive cash, so ask your caseworker where you stand. arkansas wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. arkansas wic runs through local health units of the arkansas department of health. apply through your local health unit, or call the state wic office at 501-661-2508 for the clinic nearest you. legal aid of arkansas legal aid of arkansas is a nonprofit law firm that gives free civil legal help to low-income arkansans on eviction and housing, family and domestic abuse, consumer and debt, disability, and public benefits problems. as of 2026 it serves the whole state as the sole federally funded legal aid program. call the free help line at 1-800-952-9243, or apply online at arlegalaid.org. unemployment insurance (arkansas) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the arkansas division of workforce services. file your first claim online through ezarc at ezarc.adws.arkansas.gov, then file weekly certifications through arkansas launch at launch.arkansas.gov. for help, call the ui service center at 1-844-908-2178, monday through friday, 8am to 4pm. weatherization assistance program (arkansas) the weatherization assistance program makes free energy-efficiency repairs, insulation, air sealing, and heating and cooling fixes, so your utility bills drop over time. income-eligible homeowners and renters both qualify. arkansas delivers it through local community action agencies, so contact the agency serving your county through the arkansas community action agencies association at acaaa.org, or call 501-372-0807."><h2>Arkansas programs (10)</h2><article><a href="/programs/arhome-arkansas-medicaid-expansion/">ARHOME (Arkansas Medicaid Expansion)</a><p>ARHOME, Arkansas Health and Opportunity for Me, is Arkansas&#39;s expanded Medicaid for low-income adults ages 19 to 64. It uses Medicaid dollars to enroll most members in a private qualified health plan that covers doctor visits, prescriptions, and hospital care. Apply online through Access Arkansas at access.arkansas.gov, or call 1-855-372-1084.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/arkids-first-childrens-health-coverage/">ARKids First (Children&#39;s Health Coverage)</a><p>ARKids First is Arkansas&#39;s health coverage for children, combining Medicaid as ARKids A and CHIP as ARKids B so kids get checkups, shots, dental, vision, and hospital care. Apply online through Access Arkansas at access.arkansas.gov, or call 1-855-372-1084. Children can qualify even when a parent does not.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/arkansas-211/">Arkansas 211</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 for free, confidential help finding local assistance with food, housing, rent, utilities, and childcare. Arkansas 211 is run by Hark at the Excellerate Foundation and currently answers calls from Benton, Madison, and Washington counties in Northwest Arkansas, with service expanding. You can also call 1-866-489-6983 around the clock, or text your zip code to 898211.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/arkansas-liheap-energy-assistance/">Arkansas LIHEAP (Energy Assistance)</a><p>The Low Income Home Energy Assistance Program, LIHEAP, helps Arkansas households pay heating and cooling bills, with extra crisis help when your power or heat is about to be shut off. Applications are taken by local community action agencies, not by a state office. Find the agency serving your county through the Arkansas Community Action Agencies Association at acaaa.org, or call 501-372-0807.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/arkansas-snap-food-assistance/">Arkansas SNAP (Food Assistance)</a><p>SNAP is Arkansas&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. Apply online through Access Arkansas at access.arkansas.gov, the state&#39;s one application for SNAP, Medicaid, and TEA cash assistance, or at your local Department of Human Services county office. You can also get help by phone at 1-855-372-1084.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/arkansas-tea-cash-assistance/">Arkansas TEA (Cash Assistance)</a><p>Transitional Employment Assistance, TEA, is Arkansas&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children, paired with the Work Pays employment program. Apply online through Access Arkansas at access.arkansas.gov or at your local DHS county office. TEA carries a work requirement and a time limit on how long you can receive cash, so ask your caseworker where you stand.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/arkansas-wic/">Arkansas WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. Arkansas WIC runs through local health units of the Arkansas Department of Health. Apply through your local health unit, or call the state WIC office at 501-661-2508 for the clinic nearest you.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/legal-aid-of-arkansas/">Legal Aid of Arkansas</a><p>Legal Aid of Arkansas is a nonprofit law firm that gives free civil legal help to low-income Arkansans on eviction and housing, family and domestic abuse, consumer and debt, disability, and public benefits problems. As of 2026 it serves the whole state as the sole federally funded legal aid program. Call the free HELP Line at 1-800-952-9243, or apply online at arlegalaid.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-arkansas/">Unemployment Insurance (Arkansas)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Arkansas Division of Workforce Services. File your first claim online through EZARC at ezarc.adws.arkansas.gov, then file weekly certifications through Arkansas LAUNCH at launch.arkansas.gov. For help, call the UI Service Center at 1-844-908-2178, Monday through Friday, 8am to 4pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-arkansas/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Arkansas)</a><p>The Weatherization Assistance Program makes free energy-efficiency repairs, insulation, air sealing, and heating and cooling fixes, so your utility bills drop over time. Income-eligible homeowners and renters both qualify. Arkansas delivers it through local community action agencies, so contact the agency serving your county through the Arkansas Community Action Agencies Association at acaaa.org, or call 501-372-0807.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="bakersfield / kern county, ca 211 kern county kern county&#39;s local 211 line, run by capk. free, confidential referrals to rent help, shelters, food, utility assistance, and more, 24 hours a day, 7 days a week. america&#39;s job center of kern free job search help, resume workshops, training programs, and computer access at 1600 e. belle terrace in bakersfield. a youth center serves job seekers ages 18 to 24. capk energy program — help with utility bills capk (community action partnership of kern) helps income-qualified households pay electric, gas, and propane bills, and can weatherize your home to lower future bills. apply by email, mail, or in person at 300 19th street in bakersfield. capk food bank free food through more than 200 emergency food distribution sites across kern county, plus a monthly senior food box program. call 211 to find the food distribution closest to you. capk vita — free tax preparation free tax preparation and e-filing for low- and moderate-income families, year-round. they can help you claim tax credits like the eitc that put real money back in your pocket. capk veterans &amp; supportive services housing help for veterans and their families: rental assistance to prevent homelessness (ssvf), short-term and transitional housing, and permanent supportive housing. case managers walk with you through the whole process. capk wic (women, infants &amp; children) free healthy food, nutrition help, and breastfeeding support for pregnant women, new moms, and kids under 5. dads, grandparents, and foster parents caring for young children can also apply. calfresh (food stamps) — kern county dhs monthly money on an ebt card to buy groceries. if you have very little income or cash on hand, you may qualify for expedited benefits within 3 days. calworks — kern county dhs monthly cash aid and job help for families with children and pregnant women. can also help pay for childcare, diapers, transportation, and even housing assistance. catholic charities — bakersfield community services emergency help with rent or mortgage payments, plus a food pantry and clothing for families in crisis. you do not need to be catholic to get help. clinica sierra vista community health centers across kern county offering medical, dental, and behavioral health care whether or not you have insurance. staff can also help you sign up for medi-cal or covered california. credit.org — free nonprofit credit counseling a nonprofit, hud-approved agency offering free credit counseling, debt management plans, and housing counseling (including foreclosure prevention) by phone. your first session is free with no products to sell. general assistance — kern county dhs cash help with rent, utilities, transportation, and personal needs for adults with no other means of support. a disability advocate can also help you apply for ssi if you have a long-term disability. golden empire gleaners a bakersfield food bank working with over 125 community agencies to get groceries to kern county families in need. call or stop by the warehouse on 30th street to get connected to food help. greater bakersfield legal assistance (gbla) free legal help for low-income kern county residents: eviction defense, denied or cut-off benefits (calworks, calfresh, ssi), restraining orders for domestic violence survivors, health coverage problems, and a seniors law center for people 60 and older. housing authority of the county of kern runs section 8 housing choice vouchers, project-based vouchers, and affordable housing communities across kern county. some waiting lists open and close during the year, so check which programs are accepting applications. kern behavioral health 24-hour crisis hotline free, confidential 24-hour support for mental health or substance use crises, or just to talk to a trained counselor without judgment. they can also send a mobile crisis response team and connect you to ongoing care. kern county aging &amp; adult services — senior information &amp; referral one phone call connects seniors and their families to meals on wheels, in-home care, transportation, utility rebate help, and legal services. staff can also help you fill out forms and understand letters from other agencies. kern county veterans service department free help for veterans and their families filing va claims: disability compensation, pensions, health care, education, and survivor benefits. accredited officers handle the paperwork with you at no charge. kern medical kern county&#39;s public teaching hospital in bakersfield, home to the region&#39;s only level ii trauma center. covered california counselors are on site weekdays to help patients get insured. m street navigation center (capk) a 24-hour shelter in bakersfield with beds, meals, laundry, medical check-ups, and secure storage. pets welcome. staff help connect guests to longer-term housing. medi-cal — kern county dhs free or low-cost health insurance covering medical, dental, and vision care. you may qualify even if you work, own a home or car, or are not a u.s. citizen. omni family health a network of health centers in kern county providing medical, dental, and behavioral health care with a sliding fee scale if you are uninsured or money is tight. call or text to make an appointment. the mission at kern county overnight shelter, three hot meals a day, showers, and laundry at 816 east 21st street in bakersfield, every day of the year. also offers recovery programs and support for women with children. the open door network shelter and support for people facing homelessness, domestic violence, sexual assault, or human trafficking in kern county. their crisis hotline answers 24 hours a day. the salvation army — bakersfield corps free food distribution monday through thursday afternoons, a tuesday senior lunch, and social services help during weekday business hours at 4417 wilson road. unemployment insurance (edd) weekly payments of $40 to $450 if you lost work through no fault of your own. apply online through myedd; first payment usually takes about three weeks."><h2>Bakersfield / Kern County, CA programs (27)</h2><article><a href="/programs/211-kern-county/">211 Kern County</a><p>Kern County&#39;s local 211 line, run by CAPK. Free, confidential referrals to rent help, shelters, food, utility assistance, and more, 24 hours a day, 7 days a week.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/americas-job-center-of-kern/">America&#39;s Job Center of Kern</a><p>Free job search help, resume workshops, training programs, and computer access at 1600 E. Belle Terrace in Bakersfield. A youth center serves job seekers ages 18 to 24.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/capk-energy-program-help-with-utility-bills/">CAPK Energy Program — Help with Utility Bills</a><p>CAPK (Community Action Partnership of Kern) helps income-qualified households pay electric, gas, and propane bills, and can weatherize your home to lower future bills. Apply by email, mail, or in person at 300 19th Street in Bakersfield.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/capk-food-bank/">CAPK Food Bank</a><p>Free food through more than 200 emergency food distribution sites across Kern County, plus a monthly Senior Food Box program. Call 211 to find the food distribution closest to you.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/capk-vita-free-tax-preparation/">CAPK VITA — Free Tax Preparation</a><p>Free tax preparation and e-filing for low- and moderate-income families, year-round. They can help you claim tax credits like the EITC that put real money back in your pocket.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/capk-veterans-and-supportive-services/">CAPK Veterans &amp; Supportive Services</a><p>Housing help for veterans and their families: rental assistance to prevent homelessness (SSVF), short-term and transitional housing, and permanent supportive housing. Case managers walk with you through the whole process.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/capk-wic-women-infants-and-children/">CAPK WIC (Women, Infants &amp; Children)</a><p>Free healthy food, nutrition help, and breastfeeding support for pregnant women, new moms, and kids under 5. Dads, grandparents, and foster parents caring for young children can also apply.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/calfresh-food-stamps-kern-county-dhs/">CalFresh (Food Stamps) — Kern County DHS</a><p>Monthly money on an EBT card to buy groceries. If you have very little income or cash on hand, you may qualify for expedited benefits within 3 days.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/calworks-kern-county-dhs/">CalWORKs — Kern County DHS</a><p>Monthly cash aid and job help for families with children and pregnant women. Can also help pay for childcare, diapers, transportation, and even housing assistance.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/catholic-charities-bakersfield-community-services/">Catholic Charities — Bakersfield Community Services</a><p>Emergency help with rent or mortgage payments, plus a food pantry and clothing for families in crisis. You do not need to be Catholic to get help.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/clinica-sierra-vista/">Clinica Sierra Vista</a><p>Community health centers across Kern County offering medical, dental, and behavioral health care whether or not you have insurance. Staff can also help you sign up for Medi-Cal or Covered California.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/credit-org-free-nonprofit-credit-counseling/">Credit.org — Free Nonprofit Credit Counseling</a><p>A nonprofit, HUD-approved agency offering free credit counseling, debt management plans, and housing counseling (including foreclosure prevention) by phone. Your first session is free with no products to sell.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/general-assistance-kern-county-dhs/">General Assistance — Kern County DHS</a><p>Cash help with rent, utilities, transportation, and personal needs for adults with no other means of support. A Disability Advocate can also help you apply for SSI if you have a long-term disability.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/golden-empire-gleaners/">Golden Empire Gleaners</a><p>A Bakersfield food bank working with over 125 community agencies to get groceries to Kern County families in need. Call or stop by the warehouse on 30th Street to get connected to food help.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/greater-bakersfield-legal-assistance-gbla/">Greater Bakersfield Legal Assistance (GBLA)</a><p>Free legal help for low-income Kern County residents: eviction defense, denied or cut-off benefits (CalWORKs, CalFresh, SSI), restraining orders for domestic violence survivors, health coverage problems, and a Seniors Law Center for people 60 and older.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/housing-authority-of-the-county-of-kern/">Housing Authority of the County of Kern</a><p>Runs Section 8 Housing Choice Vouchers, Project-Based Vouchers, and affordable housing communities across Kern County. Some waiting lists open and close during the year, so check which programs are accepting applications.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/kern-behavioral-health-24-hour-crisis-hotline/">Kern Behavioral Health 24-Hour Crisis Hotline</a><p>Free, confidential 24-hour support for mental health or substance use crises, or just to talk to a trained counselor without judgment. They can also send a Mobile Crisis Response team and connect you to ongoing care.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/kern-county-aging-and-adult-services-senior-information-and-referral/">Kern County Aging &amp; Adult Services — Senior Information &amp; Referral</a><p>One phone call connects seniors and their families to meals on wheels, in-home care, transportation, utility rebate help, and legal services. Staff can also help you fill out forms and understand letters from other agencies.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/kern-county-veterans-service-department/">Kern County Veterans Service Department</a><p>Free help for veterans and their families filing VA claims: disability compensation, pensions, health care, education, and survivor benefits. Accredited officers handle the paperwork with you at no charge.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/kern-medical/">Kern Medical</a><p>Kern County&#39;s public teaching hospital in Bakersfield, home to the region&#39;s only Level II trauma center. Covered California counselors are on site weekdays to help patients get insured.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/m-street-navigation-center-capk/">M Street Navigation Center (CAPK)</a><p>A 24-hour shelter in Bakersfield with beds, meals, laundry, medical check-ups, and secure storage. Pets welcome. Staff help connect guests to longer-term housing.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/medi-cal-kern-county-dhs/">Medi-Cal — Kern County DHS</a><p>Free or low-cost health insurance covering medical, dental, and vision care. You may qualify even if you work, own a home or car, or are not a U.S. citizen.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/omni-family-health/">Omni Family Health</a><p>A network of health centers in Kern County providing medical, dental, and behavioral health care with a sliding fee scale if you are uninsured or money is tight. Call or text to make an appointment.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/the-mission-at-kern-county/">The Mission at Kern County</a><p>Overnight shelter, three hot meals a day, showers, and laundry at 816 East 21st Street in Bakersfield, every day of the year. Also offers recovery programs and support for women with children.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/the-open-door-network/">The Open Door Network</a><p>Shelter and support for people facing homelessness, domestic violence, sexual assault, or human trafficking in Kern County. Their crisis hotline answers 24 hours a day.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/the-salvation-army-bakersfield-corps/">The Salvation Army — Bakersfield Corps</a><p>Free food distribution Monday through Thursday afternoons, a Tuesday senior lunch, and social services help during weekday business hours at 4417 Wilson Road.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-edd/">Unemployment Insurance (EDD)</a><p>Weekly payments of $40 to $450 if you lost work through no fault of your own. Apply online through myEDD; first payment usually takes about three weeks.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="california care / fera (utility bill discounts) 30-35% discount on electricity and 20% on gas bills from pg&amp;e, sce, socalgas, and sdg&amp;e for income-qualified households. enrollment is a short form with your utility. caleitc + young child tax credit cash-back state tax credits worth hundreds to thousands of dollars for low-income workers — even if you owe no taxes. free tax prep available. calfresh (california snap) california&#39;s food benefits program — monthly money on an ebt card for groceries. apply through benefitscal, the state&#39;s official benefits site, or call 1-877-847-3663. (getcalfresh no longer takes applications.) calworks (cash aid for families) monthly cash assistance plus job services and child care for california families with children in need. california courts self-help (eviction) official guides on responding to an eviction notice in california — you typically have only 5 business days to respond to a court summons. free self-help centers at every courthouse. california liheap (energy assistance) help paying energy bills and shutoff emergencies, delivered through local community action agencies. covered california california&#39;s health insurance marketplace with income-based subsidies if you earn too much for medi-cal. edd unemployment &amp; disability insurance unemployment benefits if you lost work, state disability insurance if you can&#39;t work due to illness/injury, and paid family leave. medi-cal (california medicaid) free or low-cost health coverage for low-income californians — doctor visits, prescriptions, mental health, and dental for most members."><h2>California programs (9)</h2><article><a href="/programs/care-fera-utility-bill-discounts/">CARE / FERA (Utility Bill Discounts)</a><p>30-35% discount on electricity and 20% on gas bills from PG&amp;E, SCE, SoCalGas, and SDG&amp;E for income-qualified households. Enrollment is a short form with your utility.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/caleitc-young-child-tax-credit/">CalEITC + Young Child Tax Credit</a><p>Cash-back state tax credits worth hundreds to thousands of dollars for low-income workers — even if you owe no taxes. Free tax prep available.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/calfresh-california-snap/">CalFresh (California SNAP)</a><p>California&#39;s food benefits program — monthly money on an EBT card for groceries. Apply through BenefitsCal, the state&#39;s official benefits site, or call 1-877-847-3663. (GetCalFresh no longer takes applications.)</p></article><article><a href="/programs/calworks-cash-aid-for-families/">CalWORKs (Cash Aid for Families)</a><p>Monthly cash assistance plus job services and child care for California families with children in need.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/california-courts-self-help-eviction/">California Courts Self-Help (Eviction)</a><p>Official guides on responding to an eviction notice in California — you typically have only 5 business days to respond to a court summons. Free self-help centers at every courthouse.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/california-liheap-energy-assistance/">California LIHEAP (Energy Assistance)</a><p>Help paying energy bills and shutoff emergencies, delivered through local community action agencies.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/covered-california/">Covered California</a><p>California&#39;s health insurance marketplace with income-based subsidies if you earn too much for Medi-Cal.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/edd-unemployment-and-disability-insurance/">EDD Unemployment &amp; Disability Insurance</a><p>Unemployment benefits if you lost work, State Disability Insurance if you can&#39;t work due to illness/injury, and Paid Family Leave.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/medi-cal-california-medicaid/">Medi-Cal (California Medicaid)</a><p>Free or low-cost health coverage for low-income Californians — doctor visits, prescriptions, mental health, and dental for most members.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="colorado 211 colorado dial 2-1-1 from any colorado phone, or call 1-866-760-6489, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. 211 colorado covers all 64 counties and updates its resource database daily. you can also text your zip code to 898-211 or search at 211colorado.org. colorado child tax credit the colorado child tax credit is a refundable credit for lower-income families with young children, so it can come back as cash even if you owe no state tax. it is available on tax year 2025 returns for families with a child under 17 who meet the income limits. because of state revenue rules, the credit is not offered for tax year 2026. claim it on your colorado return. colorado earned income tax credit colorado&#39;s earned income tax credit is a refundable credit worth a percentage of your federal eitc, so it can pay out as cash even if you owe no state tax. for tax year 2025 it equals 50 percent of your federal eitc. for tax year 2026 the rate drops to 25 percent. claim it on your colorado income tax return. colorado leap (low-income energy assistance program) the low-income energy assistance program, leap, helps low-income colorado households pay a portion of winter home heating costs, whether you heat with gas, electric, propane, or wood. leap runs one season a year, november 1 through april 30. apply online through colorado peak at colorado.gov/peak during the season, or call 1-866-heat-help, that is 1-866-432-8435, to request a paper application or apply by phone. colorado legal services colorado legal services provides free civil legal help to low-income and older colorado residents on housing, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. call the intake line at 303-837-1313 to reach the office serving your area, or apply online at coloradolegalservices.org. free plain-language legal information and self-help forms are at lawhelp.colorado.gov. colorado snap (food assistance) snap is colorado&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money loaded onto a colorado quest ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. apply online through colorado peak at colorado.gov/peak, which also handles health first colorado, colorado works cash, and other benefits, or at your county human services office. the state snap customer service line at 1-800-816-4451 answers questions about an existing case. colorado wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. colorado wic runs through local clinics. start by filling out the online referral form at coloradowic.gov and a staff member contacts you within 10 days, or call your local wic clinic directly. the state wic office line is 303-692-2400. colorado works (tanf cash assistance) colorado works is the state&#39;s temporary assistance for needy families, tanf, program: monthly cash assistance for low-income families with a child in the home, paired with job training and employment support. apply online through colorado peak at colorado.gov/peak or at your county human services office. counties run the program and have up to 45 days to process a completed application. health first colorado (medicaid) &amp; chp+ health first colorado is the state&#39;s medicaid program: free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, adults, older adults, and people with disabilities. child health plan plus, chp+, covers children and pregnant people at slightly higher incomes. apply online through colorado peak at colorado.gov/peak or at your county human services office. the member contact center is 1-800-221-3943, monday through friday, 8am to 4:30pm. unemployment insurance (colorado) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the colorado department of labor and employment. file your claim online through myui+ at cdle.colorado.gov, which is the fastest way, or call the customer support center at 1-800-388-5515 or 303-318-9000, monday through friday, 8am to 4pm. weatherization assistance program (colorado) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible colorado households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system repairs that lower future bills. the colorado energy office runs the weatherization assistance program through local providers across the state. because the state office does not take applications directly, contact the weatherization provider serving your county to apply."><h2>Colorado programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/211-colorado/">211 Colorado</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Colorado phone, or call 1-866-760-6489, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. 211 Colorado covers all 64 counties and updates its resource database daily. You can also text your zip code to 898-211 or search at 211colorado.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/colorado-child-tax-credit/">Colorado Child Tax Credit</a><p>The Colorado Child Tax Credit is a refundable credit for lower-income families with young children, so it can come back as cash even if you owe no state tax. It is available on tax year 2025 returns for families with a child under 17 who meet the income limits. Because of state revenue rules, the credit is not offered for tax year 2026. Claim it on your Colorado return.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/colorado-earned-income-tax-credit/">Colorado Earned Income Tax Credit</a><p>Colorado&#39;s Earned Income Tax Credit is a refundable credit worth a percentage of your federal EITC, so it can pay out as cash even if you owe no state tax. For tax year 2025 it equals 50 percent of your federal EITC. For tax year 2026 the rate drops to 25 percent. Claim it on your Colorado income tax return.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/colorado-leap-low-income-energy-assistance-program/">Colorado LEAP (Low-income Energy Assistance Program)</a><p>The Low-income Energy Assistance Program, LEAP, helps low-income Colorado households pay a portion of winter home heating costs, whether you heat with gas, electric, propane, or wood. LEAP runs one season a year, November 1 through April 30. Apply online through Colorado PEAK at colorado.gov/PEAK during the season, or call 1-866-HEAT-HELP, that is 1-866-432-8435, to request a paper application or apply by phone.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/colorado-legal-services/">Colorado Legal Services</a><p>Colorado Legal Services provides free civil legal help to low-income and older Colorado residents on housing, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. Call the intake line at 303-837-1313 to reach the office serving your area, or apply online at coloradolegalservices.org. Free plain-language legal information and self-help forms are at lawhelp.colorado.gov.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/colorado-snap-food-assistance/">Colorado SNAP (Food Assistance)</a><p>SNAP is Colorado&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money loaded onto a Colorado Quest EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. Apply online through Colorado PEAK at colorado.gov/PEAK, which also handles Health First Colorado, Colorado Works cash, and other benefits, or at your county human services office. The state SNAP customer service line at 1-800-816-4451 answers questions about an existing case.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/colorado-wic/">Colorado WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. Colorado WIC runs through local clinics. Start by filling out the online referral form at coloradowic.gov and a staff member contacts you within 10 days, or call your local WIC clinic directly. The state WIC office line is 303-692-2400.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/colorado-works-tanf-cash-assistance/">Colorado Works (TANF Cash Assistance)</a><p>Colorado Works is the state&#39;s Temporary Assistance for Needy Families, TANF, program: monthly cash assistance for low-income families with a child in the home, paired with job training and employment support. Apply online through Colorado PEAK at colorado.gov/PEAK or at your county human services office. Counties run the program and have up to 45 days to process a completed application.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/health-first-colorado-medicaid-and-chp/">Health First Colorado (Medicaid) &amp; CHP+</a><p>Health First Colorado is the state&#39;s Medicaid program: free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, adults, older adults, and people with disabilities. Child Health Plan Plus, CHP+, covers children and pregnant people at slightly higher incomes. Apply online through Colorado PEAK at colorado.gov/PEAK or at your county human services office. The Member Contact Center is 1-800-221-3943, Monday through Friday, 8am to 4:30pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-colorado/">Unemployment Insurance (Colorado)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Colorado Department of Labor and Employment. File your claim online through MyUI+ at cdle.colorado.gov, which is the fastest way, or call the customer support center at 1-800-388-5515 or 303-318-9000, Monday through Friday, 8am to 4pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-colorado/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Colorado)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible Colorado households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system repairs that lower future bills. The Colorado Energy Office runs the Weatherization Assistance Program through local providers across the state. Because the state office does not take applications directly, contact the weatherization provider serving your county to apply.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="connecticut 211 connecticut dial 2-1-1 from any connecticut phone, or call 1-800-203-1234, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. 211 connecticut is a program of united way of connecticut and answers around the clock, every day. you can also chat or search at 211ct.org. care 4 kids (child care assistance) care 4 kids helps low-to-moderate-income connecticut families pay for child care, so a parent can work, train, or attend school. run by the office of early childhood, it pays part of the cost directly to an approved provider. apply online through the care 4 kids portal, or call 1-888-214-5437 with questions. connecticut earned income tax credit connecticut offers a refundable state earned income tax credit worth 40 percent of your federal eitc. because it is refundable, it pays out as a refund even when it is larger than the state tax you owe. claim it on your connecticut income tax return using schedule ct-eitc. connecticut energy assistance program / ceap (winter heating) the connecticut energy assistance program, ceap, is the state&#39;s liheap: help with winter heating bills for income-eligible households, with payments usually made straight to your utility or fuel dealer. the department of social services runs it through nine regional community action agencies. to apply, dial 2-1-1 to be connected to the agency serving your town. for the 2025-2026 heating season, applications opened september 1, 2025 and run through may 29, 2026. connecticut legal aid (statewide legal services) connecticut&#39;s legal aid programs give free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. call statewide legal services of connecticut at 1-800-453-3320 to reach the legal aid program serving your area, or apply online any time at apply.slsct.org. free self-help legal information is at ctlawhelp.org. connecticut snap (food assistance) snap is connecticut&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. apply online through connect at connect.ct.gov or the mydss mobile portal, by mail, or in person at a dss office. you can also call the dss benefits center at 1-855-626-6632, monday through friday, 8am to 4pm. connecticut wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. connecticut wic runs through local agencies of the state department of public health. start online with the wic interest form, or call the state wic office at 1-800-741-2142 to find your local clinic. husky health (connecticut medicaid &amp; chip) husky health is connecticut&#39;s medicaid and chip program: free or low-cost coverage. husky a covers children, parents, caretakers, and pregnant people; husky b is chip for children; husky c covers adults 65 and older and people with disabilities; husky d covers low-income adults under 65. enroll through access health ct at 1-855-805-4325, monday through friday, 8am to 4pm, or apply through connect. temporary family assistance / tfa (cash assistance) temporary family assistance, tfa, is connecticut&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children, the state&#39;s part of the federal tanf program. most adults take part in jobs first, which pairs the cash with employment services. apply online through connect at connect.ct.gov or at your local department of social services office. unemployment insurance (connecticut dol) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the connecticut department of labor. file online through reemployct, the state&#39;s unemployment system, which is the fastest way. if you need help, the consumer contact center is 1-800-956-3294, monday through friday, 7:30am to 4:30pm. weatherization assistance program (connecticut) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible connecticut households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system improvements that lower future bills. the department of energy and environmental protection runs the weatherization assistance program through local community action agencies. contact the community action agency serving your area to apply, and note that applying for energy assistance generally screens you for weatherization too."><h2>Connecticut programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/211-connecticut/">211 Connecticut</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Connecticut phone, or call 1-800-203-1234, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. 211 Connecticut is a program of United Way of Connecticut and answers around the clock, every day. You can also chat or search at 211ct.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/care-4-kids-child-care-assistance/">Care 4 Kids (Child Care Assistance)</a><p>Care 4 Kids helps low-to-moderate-income Connecticut families pay for child care, so a parent can work, train, or attend school. Run by the Office of Early Childhood, it pays part of the cost directly to an approved provider. Apply online through the Care 4 Kids portal, or call 1-888-214-5437 with questions.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/connecticut-earned-income-tax-credit/">Connecticut Earned Income Tax Credit</a><p>Connecticut offers a refundable state Earned Income Tax Credit worth 40 percent of your federal EITC. Because it is refundable, it pays out as a refund even when it is larger than the state tax you owe. Claim it on your Connecticut income tax return using Schedule CT-EITC.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/connecticut-energy-assistance-program-ceap-winter-heating/">Connecticut Energy Assistance Program / CEAP (Winter Heating)</a><p>The Connecticut Energy Assistance Program, CEAP, is the state&#39;s LIHEAP: help with winter heating bills for income-eligible households, with payments usually made straight to your utility or fuel dealer. The Department of Social Services runs it through nine regional Community Action Agencies. To apply, dial 2-1-1 to be connected to the agency serving your town. For the 2025-2026 heating season, applications opened September 1, 2025 and run through May 29, 2026.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/connecticut-legal-aid-statewide-legal-services/">Connecticut Legal Aid (Statewide Legal Services)</a><p>Connecticut&#39;s legal aid programs give free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. Call Statewide Legal Services of Connecticut at 1-800-453-3320 to reach the legal aid program serving your area, or apply online any time at apply.slsct.org. Free self-help legal information is at ctlawhelp.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/connecticut-snap-food-assistance/">Connecticut SNAP (Food Assistance)</a><p>SNAP is Connecticut&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. Apply online through ConneCT at connect.ct.gov or the MyDSS mobile portal, by mail, or in person at a DSS office. You can also call the DSS Benefits Center at 1-855-626-6632, Monday through Friday, 8am to 4pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/connecticut-wic/">Connecticut WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. Connecticut WIC runs through local agencies of the state Department of Public Health. Start online with the WIC interest form, or call the state WIC office at 1-800-741-2142 to find your local clinic.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/husky-health-connecticut-medicaid-and-chip/">HUSKY Health (Connecticut Medicaid &amp; CHIP)</a><p>HUSKY Health is Connecticut&#39;s Medicaid and CHIP program: free or low-cost coverage. HUSKY A covers children, parents, caretakers, and pregnant people; HUSKY B is CHIP for children; HUSKY C covers adults 65 and older and people with disabilities; HUSKY D covers low-income adults under 65. Enroll through Access Health CT at 1-855-805-4325, Monday through Friday, 8am to 4pm, or apply through ConneCT.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/temporary-family-assistance-tfa-cash-assistance/">Temporary Family Assistance / TFA (Cash Assistance)</a><p>Temporary Family Assistance, TFA, is Connecticut&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children, the state&#39;s part of the federal TANF program. Most adults take part in Jobs First, which pairs the cash with employment services. Apply online through ConneCT at connect.ct.gov or at your local Department of Social Services office.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-connecticut-dol/">Unemployment Insurance (Connecticut DOL)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Connecticut Department of Labor. File online through ReEmployCT, the state&#39;s unemployment system, which is the fastest way. If you need help, the Consumer Contact Center is 1-800-956-3294, Monday through Friday, 7:30am to 4:30pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-connecticut/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Connecticut)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible Connecticut households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system improvements that lower future bills. The Department of Energy and Environmental Protection runs the Weatherization Assistance Program through local Community Action Agencies. Contact the Community Action Agency serving your area to apply, and note that applying for energy assistance generally screens you for weatherization too.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="delaware community legal aid society (clasi) the community legal aid society, clasi, provides free civil legal help to low-income delawareans on housing, public benefits, family, immigration, disability, and elder-law matters, with offices in wilmington, dover, and georgetown. call the new castle county office at 302-575-0660, the kent county office in dover at 302-674-8500, or the sussex county office in georgetown at 302-856-0038. for consumer, unemployment, and housing cases, legal services corporation of delaware also helps at lscd.com. delaware 211 dial 2-1-1 from any delaware phone, or call 1-800-560-3372, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. you can also text your zip code to 898-211. delaware 211 is staffed monday through friday, 8am to 7pm, and it refers you to local programs rather than paying bills itself. delaware earned income tax credit delaware offers two versions of the earned income tax credit, and you pick the one that helps most. one is refundable and equal to 4.5 percent of your federal eitc, so it can pay out even if you owe no tax. the other is nonrefundable and equal to 20 percent of your federal eitc, which can wipe out more tax but cannot drop below zero. claim it on your delaware return using de schedule ii. delaware energy assistance program (liheap) the delaware energy assistance program helps low-income households with home energy costs in three parts: winter heating assistance from october 1 through march 31, year-round crisis assistance for a shutoff or empty fuel tank, and summer cooling assistance that can include a free window air conditioner. community partners run intake. apply through delaware assist at assist.dhss.delaware.gov, call the division of social services at 302-255-9875, or call catholic charities in your county. delaware healthy children program (chip) the delaware healthy children program is the state&#39;s chip coverage for uninsured children under age 19 whose family earns a little too much for medicaid. as of july 1, 2024, there are no monthly premiums, and there are no copays for medical care or prescriptions except for non-emergency use of the emergency room. apply through delaware assist at assist.dhss.delaware.gov, or call 1-800-996-9969 to request an application. delaware medicaid delaware medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. delaware expanded medicaid, so low-income adults ages 19 to 64 can qualify on income alone. apply online through delaware assist at assist.dhss.delaware.gov, or call the division of medicaid and medical assistance at 302-255-9040. delaware snap (food supplement program) snap, called the food supplement program in delaware, loads monthly food money onto an ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. apply online through delaware assist at assist.dhss.delaware.gov, which handles snap, medicaid, cash assistance, energy help, and wic on one application, or at any state service center. for help, call the division of social services at 302-255-9040. delaware tanf (cash assistance) temporary assistance for needy families, tanf, is delaware&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children. most adults must take part in work or approved work activities for at least 30 hours a week. apply online through delaware assist at assist.dhss.delaware.gov or at any state service center, then attend an interview with a dss social worker. call 302-255-9040 for help. delaware wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. delaware wic runs through the division of public health. start your application through delaware assist at assist.dhss.delaware.gov, then call your county wic office to set up an appointment. in new castle county, call 302-605-4066. unemployment insurance (delaware) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the delaware department of labor, division of unemployment insurance. file your claim online through the division&#39;s claim system, linked from labor.delaware.gov, which is the fastest way. for help, call the statewide unemployment insurance line at 302-761-8446. weatherization assistance program (delaware) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible delaware households: air sealing, insulation, duct and pipe sealing, and a heating-system safety check that together typically cut energy bills by 30 to 50 percent. the dnrec state energy office runs the program through a statewide administrator, the energy coordinating agency. to apply, call the energy coordinating agency at 302-504-6111; for other questions, call dnrec at 302-735-3480."><h2>Delaware programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/community-legal-aid-society-clasi/">Community Legal Aid Society (CLASI)</a><p>The Community Legal Aid Society, CLASI, provides free civil legal help to low-income Delawareans on housing, public benefits, family, immigration, disability, and elder-law matters, with offices in Wilmington, Dover, and Georgetown. Call the New Castle County office at 302-575-0660, the Kent County office in Dover at 302-674-8500, or the Sussex County office in Georgetown at 302-856-0038. For consumer, unemployment, and housing cases, Legal Services Corporation of Delaware also helps at lscd.com.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/delaware-211/">Delaware 211</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Delaware phone, or call 1-800-560-3372, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. You can also text your ZIP code to 898-211. Delaware 211 is staffed Monday through Friday, 8am to 7pm, and it refers you to local programs rather than paying bills itself.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/delaware-earned-income-tax-credit/">Delaware Earned Income Tax Credit</a><p>Delaware offers two versions of the Earned Income Tax Credit, and you pick the one that helps most. One is refundable and equal to 4.5 percent of your federal EITC, so it can pay out even if you owe no tax. The other is nonrefundable and equal to 20 percent of your federal EITC, which can wipe out more tax but cannot drop below zero. Claim it on your Delaware return using DE Schedule II.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/delaware-energy-assistance-program-liheap/">Delaware Energy Assistance Program (LIHEAP)</a><p>The Delaware Energy Assistance Program helps low-income households with home energy costs in three parts: winter heating assistance from October 1 through March 31, year-round crisis assistance for a shutoff or empty fuel tank, and summer cooling assistance that can include a free window air conditioner. Community partners run intake. Apply through Delaware ASSIST at assist.dhss.delaware.gov, call the Division of Social Services at 302-255-9875, or call Catholic Charities in your county.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/delaware-healthy-children-program-chip/">Delaware Healthy Children Program (CHIP)</a><p>The Delaware Healthy Children Program is the state&#39;s CHIP coverage for uninsured children under age 19 whose family earns a little too much for Medicaid. As of July 1, 2024, there are no monthly premiums, and there are no copays for medical care or prescriptions except for non-emergency use of the emergency room. Apply through Delaware ASSIST at assist.dhss.delaware.gov, or call 1-800-996-9969 to request an application.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/delaware-medicaid/">Delaware Medicaid</a><p>Delaware Medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. Delaware expanded Medicaid, so low-income adults ages 19 to 64 can qualify on income alone. Apply online through Delaware ASSIST at assist.dhss.delaware.gov, or call the Division of Medicaid and Medical Assistance at 302-255-9040.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/delaware-snap-food-supplement-program/">Delaware SNAP (Food Supplement Program)</a><p>SNAP, called the Food Supplement Program in Delaware, loads monthly food money onto an EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. Apply online through Delaware ASSIST at assist.dhss.delaware.gov, which handles SNAP, Medicaid, cash assistance, energy help, and WIC on one application, or at any State Service Center. For help, call the Division of Social Services at 302-255-9040.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/delaware-tanf-cash-assistance/">Delaware TANF (Cash Assistance)</a><p>Temporary Assistance for Needy Families, TANF, is Delaware&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children. Most adults must take part in work or approved work activities for at least 30 hours a week. Apply online through Delaware ASSIST at assist.dhss.delaware.gov or at any State Service Center, then attend an interview with a DSS social worker. Call 302-255-9040 for help.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/delaware-wic/">Delaware WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. Delaware WIC runs through the Division of Public Health. Start your application through Delaware ASSIST at assist.dhss.delaware.gov, then call your county WIC office to set up an appointment. In New Castle County, call 302-605-4066.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-delaware/">Unemployment Insurance (Delaware)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Delaware Department of Labor, Division of Unemployment Insurance. File your claim online through the Division&#39;s claim system, linked from labor.delaware.gov, which is the fastest way. For help, call the statewide Unemployment Insurance line at 302-761-8446.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-delaware/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Delaware)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible Delaware households: air sealing, insulation, duct and pipe sealing, and a heating-system safety check that together typically cut energy bills by 30 to 50 percent. The DNREC State Energy Office runs the program through a statewide administrator, the Energy Coordinating Agency. To apply, call the Energy Coordinating Agency at 302-504-6111; for other questions, call DNREC at 302-735-3480.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="district of columbia 211 warmline (district of columbia) dial 2-1-1 from any district phone, or call (202) 463-6211 from out of state, to reach the 211 warmline, the district&#39;s centralized hub for social service resources and referrals. staff help you find local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. the warmline answers monday through friday, 9am to 5pm; after hours, leave a detailed voicemail and a call agent returns your call within 1 to 2 business days. dc earned income tax credit the district&#39;s earned income tax credit is one of the most generous in the country. for tax year 2025, the dc eitc equals 100 percent of your federal eitc, and it is refundable, so you may receive a refund even if you owe no dc income tax. claim it on your dc individual income tax return, the d-40. questions go to the office of tax and revenue at (202) 727-4829, which spells (202) 727-4tax. dc energy assistance (liheap &amp; utility discount program) the department of energy and environment, doee, runs the district&#39;s home energy help. as of the last check, doee has stopped taking new liheap applications and is directing residents to the utility discount program, udp. the regular liheap benefit ranges from $200 to $1,800 based on household size, income, heating source, and dwelling type, with a separate crisis benefit for households facing disconnection. call 311 or (202) 737-4404 to check an application status or ask what help is open right now. dc health care alliance the dc health care alliance is a locally funded program that provides medical coverage to district residents who are not eligible for medicaid, have no other health insurance, and are not eligible for medicare. because federal medicaid requires a qualifying immigration status, alliance is often the coverage route for residents who cannot get medicaid for that reason. alliance and the immigrant children&#39;s program combined into one program, health care alliance for adults and children. apply the same way as medicaid, online at districtdirect.dc.gov or by phone at (202) 727-5355. dc medicaid dc medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. the district expanded medicaid to cover low-income adults, so apply even if you were turned down years ago. apply online at districtdirect.dc.gov or by phone at (202) 727-5355. there is no in-person interview requirement, and it can take up to 45 days to hear back, or 60 days if you are disabled. dc snap (food assistance) snap is the district&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. apply online at districtdirect.dc.gov, by phone through the department of human services public benefits call center at (202) 727-5355, or in person at an economic security administration service center. the district also adds a local temporary local benefit, give snap a raise, of up to 10 percent of a household&#39;s federal maximum benefit. dc tanf (cash assistance) temporary assistance for needy families, tanf, is the district&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children. most adults take part in the tanf employment and education program, tep, which coaches you toward work and education goals. apply online at districtdirect.dc.gov or by phone through the public benefits call center at (202) 727-5355. effective october 2025, the maximum monthly benefit runs from $504 for a household of one up to larger amounts for bigger families. dc wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. dc health runs it through local wic clinics. to start, call your local wic site, or reach the dc wic team at (202) 442-9397. you can find site locations at dcwic.org/wic-locations. legal aid dc legal aid dc, the legal aid society of the district of columbia, provides free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing and eviction, family and domestic violence, public benefits, and consumer and debt matters. call the intake line at (202) 628-1161, or apply online at legalaiddc.org/help. intake hours are limited to certain days, so check the site before you call. unemployment insurance (dc does) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the dc department of employment services, does. file your claim online through dcnetworks at dcnetworks.org, which is the fastest way, or get help by phone at (202) 724-7000, toll-free at 1-877-319-7346, monday through friday, 8:30am to 4:30pm. weatherization assistance program (dc) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible district households: energy audits, insulation, duct sealing, and heating and cooling repairs that lower future bills. doee runs the program through local nonprofit contractors. openings are delayed this fiscal year, and doee contacts waitlisted applicants first. apply online, or email your application to energyefficiency.conservation@dc.gov, and reach the residential service line at (202) 299-3316, monday through friday, 8:30am to 2:30pm."><h2>District of Columbia programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/211-warmline-district-of-columbia/">211 Warmline (District of Columbia)</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any District phone, or call (202) 463-6211 from out of state, to reach the 211 Warmline, the District&#39;s centralized hub for social service resources and referrals. Staff help you find local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. The Warmline answers Monday through Friday, 9am to 5pm; after hours, leave a detailed voicemail and a call agent returns your call within 1 to 2 business days.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/dc-earned-income-tax-credit/">DC Earned Income Tax Credit</a><p>The District&#39;s Earned Income Tax Credit is one of the most generous in the country. For Tax Year 2025, the DC EITC equals 100 percent of your federal EITC, and it is refundable, so you may receive a refund even if you owe no DC income tax. Claim it on your DC individual income tax return, the D-40. Questions go to the Office of Tax and Revenue at (202) 727-4829, which spells (202) 727-4TAX.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/dc-energy-assistance-liheap-and-utility-discount-program/">DC Energy Assistance (LIHEAP &amp; Utility Discount Program)</a><p>The Department of Energy and Environment, DOEE, runs the District&#39;s home energy help. As of the last check, DOEE has stopped taking new LIHEAP applications and is directing residents to the Utility Discount Program, UDP. The regular LIHEAP benefit ranges from $200 to $1,800 based on household size, income, heating source, and dwelling type, with a separate crisis benefit for households facing disconnection. Call 311 or (202) 737-4404 to check an application status or ask what help is open right now.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/dc-health-care-alliance/">DC Health Care Alliance</a><p>The DC Health Care Alliance is a locally funded program that provides medical coverage to District residents who are not eligible for Medicaid, have no other health insurance, and are not eligible for Medicare. Because federal Medicaid requires a qualifying immigration status, Alliance is often the coverage route for residents who cannot get Medicaid for that reason. Alliance and the Immigrant Children&#39;s Program combined into one program, Health Care Alliance for Adults and Children. Apply the same way as Medicaid, online at districtdirect.dc.gov or by phone at (202) 727-5355.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/dc-medicaid/">DC Medicaid</a><p>DC Medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. The District expanded Medicaid to cover low-income adults, so apply even if you were turned down years ago. Apply online at districtdirect.dc.gov or by phone at (202) 727-5355. There is no in-person interview requirement, and it can take up to 45 days to hear back, or 60 days if you are disabled.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/dc-snap-food-assistance/">DC SNAP (Food Assistance)</a><p>SNAP is the District&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. Apply online at districtdirect.dc.gov, by phone through the Department of Human Services Public Benefits Call Center at (202) 727-5355, or in person at an Economic Security Administration service center. The District also adds a local Temporary Local Benefit, Give SNAP a Raise, of up to 10 percent of a household&#39;s federal maximum benefit.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/dc-tanf-cash-assistance/">DC TANF (Cash Assistance)</a><p>Temporary Assistance for Needy Families, TANF, is the District&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children. Most adults take part in the TANF Employment and Education Program, TEP, which coaches you toward work and education goals. Apply online at districtdirect.dc.gov or by phone through the Public Benefits Call Center at (202) 727-5355. Effective October 2025, the maximum monthly benefit runs from $504 for a household of one up to larger amounts for bigger families.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/dc-wic/">DC WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. DC Health runs it through local WIC clinics. To start, call your local WIC site, or reach the DC WIC team at (202) 442-9397. You can find site locations at dcwic.org/wic-locations.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/legal-aid-dc/">Legal Aid DC</a><p>Legal Aid DC, the Legal Aid Society of the District of Columbia, provides free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing and eviction, family and domestic violence, public benefits, and consumer and debt matters. Call the intake line at (202) 628-1161, or apply online at legalaiddc.org/help. Intake hours are limited to certain days, so check the site before you call.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-dc-does/">Unemployment Insurance (DC DOES)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the DC Department of Employment Services, DOES. File your claim online through DCNetworks at dcnetworks.org, which is the fastest way, or get help by phone at (202) 724-7000, toll-free at 1-877-319-7346, Monday through Friday, 8:30am to 4:30pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-dc/">Weatherization Assistance Program (DC)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible District households: energy audits, insulation, duct sealing, and heating and cooling repairs that lower future bills. DOEE runs the program through local nonprofit contractors. Openings are delayed this fiscal year, and DOEE contacts waitlisted applicants first. Apply online, or email your application to energyefficiency.conservation@dc.gov, and reach the Residential Service Line at (202) 299-3316, Monday through Friday, 8:30am to 2:30pm.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="florida florida 211 network dial 2-1-1 anywhere in florida for free, confidential, 24/7 help finding local assistance: food, housing, utility bill help, health care, and crisis support. eleven accredited centers cover all 67 counties. florida kidcare health and dental insurance for florida children from birth through age 18, combining medicaid for children, medikids, florida healthy kids, and the children&#39;s medical services plan. most families pay $15 or $20 a month; many pay nothing. florida medicaid free or low-cost health coverage for low-income florida children, pregnant women, parents, seniors, and people with disabilities. ssi recipients are enrolled automatically; everyone else applies through myaccess. florida wic free healthy foods, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and referrals for pregnant and postpartum women, infants, and children under 5 in florida. apply through your county health department; being on medicaid, snap, or tanf qualifies you automatically on income. floridalawhelp.org (eviction &amp; legal help) free legal information for floridians on evictions, foreclosures, family law, public benefits, and debt, with a search tool that connects you to legal aid providers by location and topic. available in english, spanish, and haitian creole. liheap (home energy assistance) florida&#39;s liheap helps income-qualified households pay home cooling and heating bills, including year-round crisis help when service is about to be shut off. floridacommerce funds local agencies across the state that take applications. reemployment assistance (unemployment) florida&#39;s unemployment benefits: temporary, partial income replacement if you lost work through no fault of your own. floridacommerce runs the program; apply online through the reconnect system at floridajobs.org. snap food assistance (access florida) florida snap puts monthly grocery money on an ebt card that swipes like a debit card. one myaccess application at myaccess.myflfamilies.com can start snap, cash assistance, and medicaid together. temporary cash assistance (tca) monthly cash for very-low-income florida families with children under 18, plus pregnant women in some circumstances. the relative caregiver program pays monthly amounts to relatives raising a court-ordered dependent child. apply through myaccess. weatherization assistance program (floridacommerce) free home energy improvements for income-qualified florida households so future utility bills shrink. floridacommerce funds local weatherization agencies statewide; apply through the office that serves your county."><h2>Florida programs (10)</h2><article><a href="/programs/florida-211-network/">Florida 211 Network</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 anywhere in Florida for free, confidential, 24/7 help finding local assistance: food, housing, utility bill help, health care, and crisis support. Eleven accredited centers cover all 67 counties.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/florida-kidcare/">Florida KidCare</a><p>Health and dental insurance for Florida children from birth through age 18, combining Medicaid for children, MediKids, Florida Healthy Kids, and the Children&#39;s Medical Services plan. Most families pay $15 or $20 a month; many pay nothing.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/florida-medicaid/">Florida Medicaid</a><p>Free or low-cost health coverage for low-income Florida children, pregnant women, parents, seniors, and people with disabilities. SSI recipients are enrolled automatically; everyone else applies through MyACCESS.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/florida-wic/">Florida WIC</a><p>Free healthy foods, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and referrals for pregnant and postpartum women, infants, and children under 5 in Florida. Apply through your county health department; being on Medicaid, SNAP, or TANF qualifies you automatically on income.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/floridalawhelp-org-eviction-and-legal-help/">FloridaLawHelp.org (Eviction &amp; Legal Help)</a><p>Free legal information for Floridians on evictions, foreclosures, family law, public benefits, and debt, with a search tool that connects you to legal aid providers by location and topic. Available in English, Spanish, and Haitian Creole.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/liheap-home-energy-assistance/">LIHEAP (Home Energy Assistance)</a><p>Florida&#39;s LIHEAP helps income-qualified households pay home cooling and heating bills, including year-round crisis help when service is about to be shut off. FloridaCommerce funds local agencies across the state that take applications.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/reemployment-assistance-unemployment/">Reemployment Assistance (Unemployment)</a><p>Florida&#39;s unemployment benefits: temporary, partial income replacement if you lost work through no fault of your own. FloridaCommerce runs the program; apply online through the Reconnect system at FloridaJobs.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/snap-food-assistance-access-florida/">SNAP Food Assistance (ACCESS Florida)</a><p>Florida SNAP puts monthly grocery money on an EBT card that swipes like a debit card. One MyACCESS application at myaccess.myflfamilies.com can start SNAP, cash assistance, and Medicaid together.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/temporary-cash-assistance-tca/">Temporary Cash Assistance (TCA)</a><p>Monthly cash for very-low-income Florida families with children under 18, plus pregnant women in some circumstances. The Relative Caregiver Program pays monthly amounts to relatives raising a court-ordered dependent child. Apply through MyACCESS.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-floridacommerce/">Weatherization Assistance Program (FloridaCommerce)</a><p>Free home energy improvements for income-qualified Florida households so future utility bills shrink. FloridaCommerce funds local weatherization agencies statewide; apply through the office that serves your county.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="georgia 2-1-1 in georgia (united way network) dial 2-1-1 from any georgia phone for free, confidential help finding local assistance: food, housing, utility bills, health care, and more. regional united way centers answer for their own service areas across the state. georgia medicaid free or low-cost health coverage for eligible georgia children, pregnant women, seniors, people who are blind or disabled, and some low-income parents. one georgia gateway application screens every medicaid category and peachcare for kids together. georgia wic nutritious foods on an ewic card, breastfeeding support, health education, and referrals for pregnant women, new mothers, infants, and young children in georgia. apply through georgia gateway or your county health department; call 800-228-9173 with questions. georgialegalaid.org (eviction &amp; legal help) free plain-language legal information for georgians on eviction, foreclosure, public benefits, family law, and debt, run by atlanta legal aid and the georgia legal services program, with self-help forms and a virtual assistant. liheap (energy assistance) georgia&#39;s liheap helps income-qualified households pay heating and cooling bills, with crisis help for life-threatening energy emergencies. dfcs funds community action agencies that take applications; seniors 65+ and the medically homebound get an early application window. peachcare for kids georgia&#39;s chip: affordable health coverage for uninsured children up to age 18 whose families earn too much for medicaid. dch now charges no premiums for peachcare; small copays of $0.50 to $12.50 apply for some services. snap food stamps (georgia gateway) georgia snap puts monthly grocery money on an ebt card. apply through georgia gateway at gateway.ga.gov, where one account also handles medicaid, tanf, wic, and child care assistance, or call dfcs at 877-423-4746. tanf cash assistance monthly cash for very-low-income georgia families with children under 18, and pregnant women. apply at gateway.ga.gov or by paper form 297 to your county dfcs office; adults are limited to 48 months in a lifetime. unemployment insurance (gdol) georgia&#39;s unemployment benefits: temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own. file online through the georgia department of labor&#39;s myui claimant portal or at a local career center; the maximum duration adjusts between 14 and 26 weeks with the state unemployment rate. weatherization assistance program (gefa) free whole-house energy efficiency improvements for income-qualified georgia households: air and duct sealing, insulation, and hvac upgrades that shrink future bills. the georgia environmental finance authority runs it through local community action agencies."><h2>Georgia programs (10)</h2><article><a href="/programs/2-1-1-in-georgia-united-way-network/">2-1-1 in Georgia (United Way Network)</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Georgia phone for free, confidential help finding local assistance: food, housing, utility bills, health care, and more. Regional United Way centers answer for their own service areas across the state.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/georgia-medicaid/">Georgia Medicaid</a><p>Free or low-cost health coverage for eligible Georgia children, pregnant women, seniors, people who are blind or disabled, and some low-income parents. One Georgia Gateway application screens every Medicaid category and PeachCare for Kids together.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/georgia-wic/">Georgia WIC</a><p>Nutritious foods on an eWIC card, breastfeeding support, health education, and referrals for pregnant women, new mothers, infants, and young children in Georgia. Apply through Georgia Gateway or your county health department; call 800-228-9173 with questions.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/georgialegalaid-org-eviction-and-legal-help/">GeorgiaLegalAid.org (Eviction &amp; Legal Help)</a><p>Free plain-language legal information for Georgians on eviction, foreclosure, public benefits, family law, and debt, run by Atlanta Legal Aid and the Georgia Legal Services Program, with self-help forms and a virtual assistant.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/liheap-energy-assistance/">LIHEAP (Energy Assistance)</a><p>Georgia&#39;s LIHEAP helps income-qualified households pay heating and cooling bills, with crisis help for life-threatening energy emergencies. DFCS funds community action agencies that take applications; seniors 65+ and the medically homebound get an early application window.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/peachcare-for-kids/">PeachCare for Kids</a><p>Georgia&#39;s CHIP: affordable health coverage for uninsured children up to age 18 whose families earn too much for Medicaid. DCH now charges no premiums for PeachCare; small copays of $0.50 to $12.50 apply for some services.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/snap-food-stamps-georgia-gateway/">SNAP Food Stamps (Georgia Gateway)</a><p>Georgia SNAP puts monthly grocery money on an EBT card. Apply through Georgia Gateway at gateway.ga.gov, where one account also handles Medicaid, TANF, WIC, and child care assistance, or call DFCS at 877-423-4746.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/tanf-cash-assistance/">TANF Cash Assistance</a><p>Monthly cash for very-low-income Georgia families with children under 18, and pregnant women. Apply at gateway.ga.gov or by paper Form 297 to your county DFCS office; adults are limited to 48 months in a lifetime.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-gdol/">Unemployment Insurance (GDOL)</a><p>Georgia&#39;s unemployment benefits: temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own. File online through the Georgia Department of Labor&#39;s MyUI Claimant Portal or at a local career center; the maximum duration adjusts between 14 and 26 weeks with the state unemployment rate.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-gefa/">Weatherization Assistance Program (GEFA)</a><p>Free whole-house energy efficiency improvements for income-qualified Georgia households: air and duct sealing, insulation, and HVAC upgrades that shrink future bills. The Georgia Environmental Finance Authority runs it through local community action agencies.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="hawaii aloha united way 211 hawaii dial 2-1-1 anywhere in hawaii to reach the aloha united way 211 helpline, a free and confidential service that connects you to local help with food, rent, utilities, healthcare, and more, drawn from a database of over 1,400 agencies and 4,000 programs. specialists answer from 7am to 10pm, seven days a week. from outside hawaii, or to text, use 1-877-275-6569, and you can search anytime at search.auw211.org. hawaii earned income tax credit hawaii&#39;s earned income tax credit is refundable and equal to 40 percent of the federal earned income credit you claim on your federal return. because it is refundable, it can pay out as a refund even when it is larger than the tax you owe. claim it on your hawaii return using form n-356; you must qualify for the federal eitc first. hawaii general assistance (ga) general assistance (ga) is hawaii&#39;s state cash program for adults ages 18 through 64 who have little or no income, do not have minor children, are temporarily disabled, and do not qualify for federal aid like social security. a dhs medical board must certify that you cannot work at least 30 hours a week for at least 60 days. apply through the department of human services, and call the public assistance information line at 1-855-643-1643 for information. hawaii home energy assistance program (h-heap) the hawaii home energy assistance program (h-heap), formerly liheap, gives income-eligible households a one-time payment toward an electric or gas bill for heating or cooling. it has two parts: energy crisis intervention (eci) for households with a utility disconnect notice, accepted year-round with a limited number of approvals each month, and energy credit (ec) for bill help without a crisis, accepted only june 1 through june 30 each year. applications are processed only by the community action agency serving your island, not by dhs offices. hawaii snap (food assistance) snap is hawaii&#39;s food assistance program, run by the department of human services (dhs): monthly food money loaded onto an ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. the fastest way to apply is online through the dhs public assistance information system (pais) at pais.dhs.hawaii.gov, which handles snap and financial assistance on one application. you can also mail or drop off a paper form at a processing center, or call the public assistance information line at 1-855-643-1643, open 24 hours a day, 7 days a week. hawaii tanf (cash assistance) temporary assistance for needy families (tanf) is hawaii&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families that include a minor child and a caretaker relative. families with mixed or non-citizen immigration status may instead qualify for the state-funded temporary assistance for other needy families (taonf), which provides the same benefits. apply online through the dhs public assistance information system at pais.dhs.hawaii.gov or by paper form. as a condition of eligibility, most families take part in the first-to-work program. hawaii wic wic gives healthy supplemental foods on an ewic card, plus nutrition education, breastfeeding support, and health referrals for pregnant, postpartum, and breastfeeding women, and for infants and children under age 5. hawaii wic runs through the department of health. apply online at hiwic.org and find your nearest clinic. for information, call the wic info line at 808-586-4400. legal aid society of hawaii the legal aid society of hawaii provides free civil legal help to low-income residents statewide on housing and eviction, public benefits, family, elder, and consumer matters. it does not handle criminal or personal injury cases. call the intake line at 808-536-4302 on oahu, or 1-800-499-4302 from the neighbor islands, monday through friday from 9:00 to 11:30am and 1:00 to 3:30pm. you can also start an application through online intake. med-quest (hawaii medicaid) med-quest is hawaii&#39;s medicaid program: free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant women, parents, seniors, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. hawaii expanded medicaid, so low-income adults without children can qualify too. apply online at the med-quest online portal, medical.mybenefits.hawaii.gov, or call med-quest customer service at 1-800-316-8005. unemployment insurance (hawaii) unemployment insurance from the hawaii department of labor and industrial relations (dlir) pays weekly benefits if you lost work through no fault of your own. file your claim online through the ui website at labor.hawaii.gov/ui, then file a claim certification for each week. for help, the statewide ui call center is 1-833-901-2272 toll-free, or 808-762-5752. weatherization assistance program (hawaii) the weatherization assistance program (wap) makes free energy-efficiency improvements so your utility bill shrinks, based on a home energy audit. measures can include high-efficiency faucet aerators and shower heads, led lighting, and an energy-efficient solar water heater, all at no cost. on oahu the program is run by the honolulu community action program (hcap), funded by the u.s. department of energy and the hawaii state office of community services. neighbor-island households apply through their county community action agency."><h2>Hawaii programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/aloha-united-way-211-hawaii/">Aloha United Way 211 Hawaii</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 anywhere in Hawaii to reach the Aloha United Way 211 helpline, a free and confidential service that connects you to local help with food, rent, utilities, healthcare, and more, drawn from a database of over 1,400 agencies and 4,000 programs. Specialists answer from 7am to 10pm, seven days a week. From outside Hawaii, or to text, use 1-877-275-6569, and you can search anytime at search.auw211.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/hawaii-earned-income-tax-credit/">Hawaii Earned Income Tax Credit</a><p>Hawaii&#39;s Earned Income Tax Credit is refundable and equal to 40 percent of the federal earned income credit you claim on your federal return. Because it is refundable, it can pay out as a refund even when it is larger than the tax you owe. Claim it on your Hawaii return using Form N-356; you must qualify for the federal EITC first.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/hawaii-general-assistance-ga/">Hawaii General Assistance (GA)</a><p>General Assistance (GA) is Hawaii&#39;s state cash program for adults ages 18 through 64 who have little or no income, do not have minor children, are temporarily disabled, and do not qualify for federal aid like Social Security. A DHS medical board must certify that you cannot work at least 30 hours a week for at least 60 days. Apply through the Department of Human Services, and call the Public Assistance Information Line at 1-855-643-1643 for information.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/hawaii-home-energy-assistance-program-h-heap/">Hawaii Home Energy Assistance Program (H-HEAP)</a><p>The Hawaii Home Energy Assistance Program (H-HEAP), formerly LIHEAP, gives income-eligible households a one-time payment toward an electric or gas bill for heating or cooling. It has two parts: Energy Crisis Intervention (ECI) for households with a utility disconnect notice, accepted year-round with a limited number of approvals each month, and Energy Credit (EC) for bill help without a crisis, accepted only June 1 through June 30 each year. Applications are processed only by the Community Action Agency serving your island, not by DHS offices.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/hawaii-snap-food-assistance/">Hawaii SNAP (Food Assistance)</a><p>SNAP is Hawaii&#39;s food assistance program, run by the Department of Human Services (DHS): monthly food money loaded onto an EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. The fastest way to apply is online through the DHS Public Assistance Information System (PAIS) at pais.dhs.hawaii.gov, which handles SNAP and financial assistance on one application. You can also mail or drop off a paper form at a processing center, or call the Public Assistance Information Line at 1-855-643-1643, open 24 hours a day, 7 days a week.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/hawaii-tanf-cash-assistance/">Hawaii TANF (Cash Assistance)</a><p>Temporary Assistance for Needy Families (TANF) is Hawaii&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families that include a minor child and a caretaker relative. Families with mixed or non-citizen immigration status may instead qualify for the state-funded Temporary Assistance for Other Needy Families (TAONF), which provides the same benefits. Apply online through the DHS Public Assistance Information System at pais.dhs.hawaii.gov or by paper form. As a condition of eligibility, most families take part in the First-To-Work program.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/hawaii-wic/">Hawaii WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy supplemental foods on an eWIC card, plus nutrition education, breastfeeding support, and health referrals for pregnant, postpartum, and breastfeeding women, and for infants and children under age 5. Hawaii WIC runs through the Department of Health. Apply online at hiwic.org and find your nearest clinic. For information, call the WIC Info Line at 808-586-4400.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/legal-aid-society-of-hawaii/">Legal Aid Society of Hawaii</a><p>The Legal Aid Society of Hawaii provides free civil legal help to low-income residents statewide on housing and eviction, public benefits, family, elder, and consumer matters. It does not handle criminal or personal injury cases. Call the intake line at 808-536-4302 on Oahu, or 1-800-499-4302 from the neighbor islands, Monday through Friday from 9:00 to 11:30am and 1:00 to 3:30pm. You can also start an application through online intake.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/med-quest-hawaii-medicaid/">Med-QUEST (Hawaii Medicaid)</a><p>Med-QUEST is Hawaii&#39;s Medicaid program: free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant women, parents, seniors, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. Hawaii expanded Medicaid, so low-income adults without children can qualify too. Apply online at the Med-QUEST Online Portal, medical.mybenefits.hawaii.gov, or call Med-QUEST Customer Service at 1-800-316-8005.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-hawaii/">Unemployment Insurance (Hawaii)</a><p>Unemployment Insurance from the Hawaii Department of Labor and Industrial Relations (DLIR) pays weekly benefits if you lost work through no fault of your own. File your claim online through the UI website at labor.hawaii.gov/ui, then file a claim certification for each week. For help, the statewide UI Call Center is 1-833-901-2272 toll-free, or 808-762-5752.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-hawaii/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Hawaii)</a><p>The Weatherization Assistance Program (WAP) makes free energy-efficiency improvements so your utility bill shrinks, based on a home energy audit. Measures can include high-efficiency faucet aerators and shower heads, LED lighting, and an energy-efficient solar water heater, all at no cost. On Oahu the program is run by the Honolulu Community Action Program (HCAP), funded by the U.S. Department of Energy and the Hawaii State Office of Community Services. Neighbor-island households apply through their county Community Action Agency.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="idaho idaho careline 2-1-1 dial 2-1-1 or call 1-800-926-2588 for free, confidential help finding local idaho assistance with food, housing, utilities, health care, and more. the idaho careline is run by the department of health and welfare and connects you to programs and community resources near you. idaho child care program (iccp) the idaho child care program helps eligible parents pay part of their child care costs while they work, go to school, or attend job training. it covers children under 13, or older children who have a disability. apply online at idalink.idaho.gov or by phone at 1-877-456-1233. idaho energy assistance (liheap heating assistance) liheap helps low-income idaho households pay winter heating bills, with a crisis part that works year-round to stop a shutoff or restore disconnected service. the program runs through community action partnership agencies that serve every idaho county. find your local agency by dialing 2-1-1, and watch the seasonal application dates. idaho grocery credit idaho&#39;s grocery credit refunds part of the sales tax you pay on groceries, generally $155 per person for the year, or up to $250 per person if you save your food receipts. you claim it on your idaho income tax return, and residents who are not required to file can still claim the refund using form 24. idaho has no state earned income tax credit, so this is the main state tax refund working families should not miss. idaho legal aid services idaho legal aid services gives free civil legal help to low-income idahoans on housing, family safety, public benefits, and consumer problems. call the legal advice line at 208-746-7541 to start; intake is open monday through wednesday only. you can also apply online and use the free self-help forms at idaholegalaid.org. idaho medicaid (expanded coverage) idaho medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, seniors, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. idaho voters approved medicaid expansion in 2018, and coverage for low-income adults began in 2020. apply online at idalink.idaho.gov or by phone at 1-877-456-1233. idaho snap (food stamps) snap, still called food stamps, puts monthly food money on an idaho ebt card you use like a debit card at grocery stores. apply online at idalink.idaho.gov, the state benefits portal that also handles medicaid, cash assistance, and child care help, or by phone at 1-877-456-1233. as of february 15, 2026, candy and soda are no longer snap-eligible purchases in idaho. idaho wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. idaho wic runs through local public health districts and loads benefits onto a wic card you use at approved grocery stores. find your nearest clinic through the wic clinic finder on the department of health and welfare site. tafi (temporary assistance for families in idaho) tafi is idaho&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children, worth up to $309 a month, with a 24-month lifetime limit for adults. most parents take part in the employment and training program while they receive it. apply online at idalink.idaho.gov or by phone at 1-877-456-1233. unemployment insurance (idaho) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the idaho department of labor. file online through the claimant portal at labor.idaho.gov, which is the fastest way, or call the claims line at 208-332-8942. you must file a weekly certification to keep payments coming. weatherization assistance program (idaho) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible idaho households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system repair or replacement that lower future bills. community action partnership agencies run the program locally, so contact the agency serving your area to apply. renters can qualify with written landlord permission."><h2>Idaho programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/idaho-careline-2-1-1/">Idaho CareLine 2-1-1</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 or call 1-800-926-2588 for free, confidential help finding local Idaho assistance with food, housing, utilities, health care, and more. The Idaho CareLine is run by the Department of Health and Welfare and connects you to programs and community resources near you.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/idaho-child-care-program-iccp/">Idaho Child Care Program (ICCP)</a><p>The Idaho Child Care Program helps eligible parents pay part of their child care costs while they work, go to school, or attend job training. It covers children under 13, or older children who have a disability. Apply online at idalink.idaho.gov or by phone at 1-877-456-1233.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/idaho-energy-assistance-liheap-heating-assistance/">Idaho Energy Assistance (LIHEAP Heating Assistance)</a><p>LIHEAP helps low-income Idaho households pay winter heating bills, with a Crisis part that works year-round to stop a shutoff or restore disconnected service. The program runs through Community Action Partnership agencies that serve every Idaho county. Find your local agency by dialing 2-1-1, and watch the seasonal application dates.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/idaho-grocery-credit/">Idaho Grocery Credit</a><p>Idaho&#39;s grocery credit refunds part of the sales tax you pay on groceries, generally $155 per person for the year, or up to $250 per person if you save your food receipts. You claim it on your Idaho income tax return, and residents who are not required to file can still claim the refund using Form 24. Idaho has no state Earned Income Tax Credit, so this is the main state tax refund working families should not miss.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/idaho-legal-aid-services/">Idaho Legal Aid Services</a><p>Idaho Legal Aid Services gives free civil legal help to low-income Idahoans on housing, family safety, public benefits, and consumer problems. Call the Legal Advice Line at 208-746-7541 to start; intake is open Monday through Wednesday only. You can also apply online and use the free self-help forms at idaholegalaid.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/idaho-medicaid-expanded-coverage/">Idaho Medicaid (Expanded Coverage)</a><p>Idaho Medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, seniors, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. Idaho voters approved Medicaid expansion in 2018, and coverage for low-income adults began in 2020. Apply online at idalink.idaho.gov or by phone at 1-877-456-1233.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/idaho-snap-food-stamps/">Idaho SNAP (Food Stamps)</a><p>SNAP, still called food stamps, puts monthly food money on an Idaho EBT card you use like a debit card at grocery stores. Apply online at idalink.idaho.gov, the state benefits portal that also handles Medicaid, cash assistance, and child care help, or by phone at 1-877-456-1233. As of February 15, 2026, candy and soda are no longer SNAP-eligible purchases in Idaho.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/idaho-wic/">Idaho WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. Idaho WIC runs through local public health districts and loads benefits onto a WIC card you use at approved grocery stores. Find your nearest clinic through the WIC clinic finder on the Department of Health and Welfare site.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/tafi-temporary-assistance-for-families-in-idaho/">TAFI (Temporary Assistance for Families in Idaho)</a><p>TAFI is Idaho&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children, worth up to $309 a month, with a 24-month lifetime limit for adults. Most parents take part in the Employment and Training Program while they receive it. Apply online at idalink.idaho.gov or by phone at 1-877-456-1233.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-idaho/">Unemployment Insurance (Idaho)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Idaho Department of Labor. File online through the Claimant Portal at labor.idaho.gov, which is the fastest way, or call the claims line at 208-332-8942. You must file a weekly certification to keep payments coming.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-idaho/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Idaho)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible Idaho households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system repair or replacement that lower future bills. Community Action Partnership agencies run the program locally, so contact the agency serving your area to apply. Renters can qualify with written landlord permission.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="illinois 211 illinois dial 2-1-1 from any illinois phone, or text your zip code to 898211, for free, confidential help finding local assistance: housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and mental health. as of 2026, 211 covers all 102 illinois counties around the clock. all kids (illinois children&#39;s health coverage) all kids is illinois&#39; health coverage for children, covering doctor visits, dental, vision, and more. many families pay no premiums or co-payments. apply online, on paper, or with help from a partner; the all kids hotline is 1-866-255-5437. home weatherization assistance program (illinois dceo) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible illinoisans: air sealing, insulation, and heating-system repairs that lower future bills. run through community action agencies and overseen by the department of commerce and economic opportunity. the help illinois families call center is 1-833-711-0374. illinois earned income credit illinois&#39; state earned income credit is a refundable tax credit worth 20 percent of your federal earned income tax credit. if you qualify for the federal eitc, you qualify for the illinois credit. claim it on your illinois state return; free tax-prep sites can file it. illinois legal aid online (eviction &amp; legal help) free, plain-language legal information for illinoisans, guided court-form tools, and a directory to find a local legal aid provider. illinois legal aid online covers eviction, benefits, family law, debt, and more. illinois wic healthy food on a wic ebt card, nutrition education, and breastfeeding support for pregnant and new mothers, infants, and children under 5. find your local office through the dhs office locator and make an appointment; the state wic office is 1-844-901-0962. liheap energy assistance (illinois) illinois liheap helps low-income households pay heating and cooling bills, with priority help for emergencies. apply during your assignment month at helpillinoisfamilies.com or through a local community action agency; the help illinois families hotline is 1-833-711-0374. for the 2025-2026 program year, applications run from fall 2025 through august 15, 2026, or until funding runs out. medicaid (illinois hfs) illinois medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage run by the department of healthcare and family services. adults 19 to 64 can qualify with income up to 138 percent of the federal poverty level under the aca adult category. apply online at abe (abe.illinois.gov), by phone at the dhs help line 1-800-843-6154, or at a family community resource center. snap food stamps (illinois) illinois snap loads monthly food money on the illinois link card, which works like a debit card at grocery stores. apply online at abe (abe.illinois.gov), by phone, or at your local family community resource center. the dhs help line is 1-800-843-6154. tanf cash assistance (illinois) illinois tanf pays monthly cash to pregnant people and families with children, loaded on the illinois link card. adults are limited to 60 months of cash assistance in a lifetime. apply online at abe (abe.illinois.gov) or at your local family community resource center; the dhs help line is 1-800-843-6154. unemployment insurance (illinois ides) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the illinois department of employment security. file your claim online, or call ides claimant services at 1-800-244-5631. certify for each week you claim through the automated tele-serve line at 1-312-338-4337."><h2>Illinois programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/211-illinois/">211 Illinois</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Illinois phone, or text your zip code to 898211, for free, confidential help finding local assistance: housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and mental health. As of 2026, 211 covers all 102 Illinois counties around the clock.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/all-kids-illinois-childrens-health-coverage/">All Kids (Illinois Children&#39;s Health Coverage)</a><p>All Kids is Illinois&#39; health coverage for children, covering doctor visits, dental, vision, and more. Many families pay no premiums or co-payments. Apply online, on paper, or with help from a partner; the All Kids Hotline is 1-866-255-5437.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/home-weatherization-assistance-program-illinois-dceo/">Home Weatherization Assistance Program (Illinois DCEO)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible Illinoisans: air sealing, insulation, and heating-system repairs that lower future bills. Run through Community Action Agencies and overseen by the Department of Commerce and Economic Opportunity. The Help Illinois Families call center is 1-833-711-0374.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/illinois-earned-income-credit/">Illinois Earned Income Credit</a><p>Illinois&#39; state Earned Income Credit is a refundable tax credit worth 20 percent of your federal Earned Income Tax Credit. If you qualify for the federal EITC, you qualify for the Illinois credit. Claim it on your Illinois state return; free tax-prep sites can file it.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/illinois-legal-aid-online-eviction-and-legal-help/">Illinois Legal Aid Online (Eviction &amp; Legal Help)</a><p>Free, plain-language legal information for Illinoisans, guided court-form tools, and a directory to find a local legal aid provider. Illinois Legal Aid Online covers eviction, benefits, family law, debt, and more.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/illinois-wic/">Illinois WIC</a><p>Healthy food on a WIC EBT card, nutrition education, and breastfeeding support for pregnant and new mothers, infants, and children under 5. Find your local office through the DHS Office Locator and make an appointment; the State WIC Office is 1-844-901-0962.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/liheap-energy-assistance-illinois/">LIHEAP Energy Assistance (Illinois)</a><p>Illinois LIHEAP helps low-income households pay heating and cooling bills, with priority help for emergencies. Apply during your assignment month at helpillinoisfamilies.com or through a local Community Action Agency; the Help Illinois Families hotline is 1-833-711-0374. For the 2025-2026 program year, applications run from fall 2025 through August 15, 2026, or until funding runs out.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/medicaid-illinois-hfs/">Medicaid (Illinois HFS)</a><p>Illinois Medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage run by the Department of Healthcare and Family Services. Adults 19 to 64 can qualify with income up to 138 percent of the federal poverty level under the ACA Adult category. Apply online at ABE (abe.illinois.gov), by phone at the DHS Help Line 1-800-843-6154, or at a Family Community Resource Center.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/snap-food-stamps-illinois/">SNAP Food Stamps (Illinois)</a><p>Illinois SNAP loads monthly food money on the Illinois Link card, which works like a debit card at grocery stores. Apply online at ABE (abe.illinois.gov), by phone, or at your local Family Community Resource Center. The DHS Help Line is 1-800-843-6154.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/tanf-cash-assistance-illinois/">TANF Cash Assistance (Illinois)</a><p>Illinois TANF pays monthly cash to pregnant people and families with children, loaded on the Illinois Link card. Adults are limited to 60 months of cash assistance in a lifetime. Apply online at ABE (abe.illinois.gov) or at your local Family Community Resource Center; the DHS Help Line is 1-800-843-6154.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-illinois-ides/">Unemployment Insurance (Illinois IDES)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Illinois Department of Employment Security. File your claim online, or call IDES Claimant Services at 1-800-244-5631. Certify for each week you claim through the automated Tele-Serve line at 1-312-338-4337.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="indiana indiana 211 dial 2-1-1 from any indiana phone, or call 1-866-211-9966, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. indiana 211 is a statewide service with trained navigators answering around the clock, every day. you can also text your zip code to 898-211 or search at in211.org. indiana earned income credit indiana has its own earned income credit worth 10 percent of your federal earned income tax credit, and it is refundable, so it can come back as cash even if you owe no state tax. you claim it by filing an indiana income tax return and attaching schedule in-eic. you generally must qualify for the federal credit first. indiana energy assistance program (eap / liheap) the energy assistance program, indiana&#39;s liheap, helps low-income households with winter heating and summer cooling costs, paid straight to your utility. it runs through the indiana housing and community development authority and local service providers. applications open for the heating season each fall and typically close in mid-april. find the provider serving your county through ihcda or by dialing 2-1-1. indiana legal services (free civil legal aid) indiana legal services is the statewide nonprofit law firm that gives free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing and eviction, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. apply online at indianalegalservices.org, or apply by phone at 1-844-243-8570, monday through thursday, 10am to 2pm eastern. indiana medicaid (hoosier healthwise &amp; healthy indiana plan) indiana medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage. hoosier healthwise covers children and pregnant people, and the healthy indiana plan, hip, covers low-income adults ages 19 to 64. apply online through the fssa benefits portal at fssabenefits.in.gov, or by phone at 1-800-403-0864. for help with hip enrollment, call 877-get-hip-9, which is 1-877-438-4479. indiana snap (food assistance) snap is indiana&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on a hoosier works ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. the division of family resources runs it. apply online through the fssa benefits portal at fssabenefits.in.gov, which also handles medicaid and tanf cash on one application, or at your local dfr office. you can also call 1-800-403-0864, monday through friday, 8am to 4:30pm local time. indiana tanf (cash assistance) temporary assistance for needy families, tanf, is indiana&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with a child under 18. most adults take part in impact, the state&#39;s employment and training program, which includes an applicant job search step before benefits start. apply online through the fssa benefits portal at fssabenefits.in.gov or at your local dfr office. call 1-800-403-0864 for help. indiana wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. indiana wic runs through the indiana department of health and local clinics. find your local clinic through the inwic app or at in.gov/health/wic, or call 1-800-522-0874 for more information. township trustee emergency assistance (poor relief) every indiana township has a trustee who by law provides emergency poor relief to residents in need: help with rent to stop an eviction, a utility bill to prevent a shutoff, food, clothing, and sometimes burial costs. help usually comes as a voucher paid to the landlord or utility, not cash. you apply at the trustee&#39;s office for the township where you live. find yours through your county website or the indiana township association at ita-in.org. unemployment insurance (indiana dwd / uplink) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the indiana department of workforce development. file your claim online through uplink, the claimant self service system, at uplink.in.gov, which is the fastest way. for help, call 1-800-891-6499. you must file a weekly voucher for each week to be paid. weatherization assistance program (indiana) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible indiana households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system improvements that lower future bills. the indiana housing and community development authority runs the program through local service providers. ihcda does not take applications directly, so contact the provider serving your area to apply. to find your provider, dial 2-1-1."><h2>Indiana programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/indiana-211/">Indiana 211</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Indiana phone, or call 1-866-211-9966, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. Indiana 211 is a statewide service with trained navigators answering around the clock, every day. You can also text your zip code to 898-211 or search at in211.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/indiana-earned-income-credit/">Indiana Earned Income Credit</a><p>Indiana has its own Earned Income Credit worth 10 percent of your federal Earned Income Tax Credit, and it is refundable, so it can come back as cash even if you owe no state tax. You claim it by filing an Indiana income tax return and attaching Schedule IN-EIC. You generally must qualify for the federal credit first.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/indiana-energy-assistance-program-eap-liheap/">Indiana Energy Assistance Program (EAP / LIHEAP)</a><p>The Energy Assistance Program, Indiana&#39;s LIHEAP, helps low-income households with winter heating and summer cooling costs, paid straight to your utility. It runs through the Indiana Housing and Community Development Authority and local service providers. Applications open for the heating season each fall and typically close in mid-April. Find the provider serving your county through IHCDA or by dialing 2-1-1.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/indiana-legal-services-free-civil-legal-aid/">Indiana Legal Services (Free Civil Legal Aid)</a><p>Indiana Legal Services is the statewide nonprofit law firm that gives free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing and eviction, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. Apply online at indianalegalservices.org, or apply by phone at 1-844-243-8570, Monday through Thursday, 10am to 2pm Eastern.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/indiana-medicaid-hoosier-healthwise-and-healthy-indiana-plan/">Indiana Medicaid (Hoosier Healthwise &amp; Healthy Indiana Plan)</a><p>Indiana Medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage. Hoosier Healthwise covers children and pregnant people, and the Healthy Indiana Plan, HIP, covers low-income adults ages 19 to 64. Apply online through the FSSA Benefits Portal at fssabenefits.in.gov, or by phone at 1-800-403-0864. For help with HIP enrollment, call 877-GET-HIP-9, which is 1-877-438-4479.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/indiana-snap-food-assistance/">Indiana SNAP (Food Assistance)</a><p>SNAP is Indiana&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on a Hoosier Works EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. The Division of Family Resources runs it. Apply online through the FSSA Benefits Portal at fssabenefits.in.gov, which also handles Medicaid and TANF cash on one application, or at your local DFR office. You can also call 1-800-403-0864, Monday through Friday, 8am to 4:30pm local time.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/indiana-tanf-cash-assistance/">Indiana TANF (Cash Assistance)</a><p>Temporary Assistance for Needy Families, TANF, is Indiana&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with a child under 18. Most adults take part in IMPACT, the state&#39;s employment and training program, which includes an Applicant Job Search step before benefits start. Apply online through the FSSA Benefits Portal at fssabenefits.in.gov or at your local DFR office. Call 1-800-403-0864 for help.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/indiana-wic/">Indiana WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. Indiana WIC runs through the Indiana Department of Health and local clinics. Find your local clinic through the INWIC app or at in.gov/health/wic, or call 1-800-522-0874 for more information.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/township-trustee-emergency-assistance-poor-relief/">Township Trustee Emergency Assistance (Poor Relief)</a><p>Every Indiana township has a trustee who by law provides emergency poor relief to residents in need: help with rent to stop an eviction, a utility bill to prevent a shutoff, food, clothing, and sometimes burial costs. Help usually comes as a voucher paid to the landlord or utility, not cash. You apply at the trustee&#39;s office for the township where you live. Find yours through your county website or the Indiana Township Association at ita-in.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-indiana-dwd-uplink/">Unemployment Insurance (Indiana DWD / Uplink)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Indiana Department of Workforce Development. File your claim online through Uplink, the Claimant Self Service system, at uplink.in.gov, which is the fastest way. For help, call 1-800-891-6499. You must file a weekly voucher for each week to be paid.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-indiana/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Indiana)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible Indiana households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system improvements that lower future bills. The Indiana Housing and Community Development Authority runs the program through local service providers. IHCDA does not take applications directly, so contact the provider serving your area to apply. To find your provider, dial 2-1-1.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="iowa 211 iowa dial 2-1-1 from any iowa phone, or call 1-800-244-7431, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. 211 iowa connects you to thousands of local programs and answers every day. you can also text your zip code to 898211 or search at 211iowa.org. hawki (healthy and well kids in iowa) hawki, short for healthy and well kids in iowa, is low-cost health and dental coverage for children under 19 in working families who earn too much for medicaid. most families pay nothing, and no family pays more than $40 a month for all of its children combined. apply online through the hhs services portal at hhsservices.iowa.gov, or call hawki member services at 1-800-257-8563. iowa earned income tax credit iowa offers a refundable earned income tax credit worth 15 percent of your federal eitc. because it is refundable, it pays out as cash even if you owe no state tax. claim it on your iowa income tax return after you qualify for the federal credit. iowa energy assistance (liheap) iowa&#39;s low-income home energy assistance program, liheap, helps qualifying households pay part of their winter heating costs. you apply at your local community action agency, not online, and each agency serves a set of counties. applications open october 1 for households with a member age 60 or older or with a disability, november 1 for everyone else, and close april 30. iowa family investment program (fip cash assistance) the family investment program, fip, is iowa&#39;s version of tanf: monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children. most adults take part in promise jobs, the work and training side of the program. apply online through iowa&#39;s hhs services portal at hhsservices.iowa.gov or at your local iowa hhs office. iowa legal aid iowa legal aid provides free civil legal help to low-income iowans on housing, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. call the statewide intake line at 1-800-532-1503 if you are under 60, open monday through friday, 9 to 11am and 1:30 to 3:30pm and closed thursday afternoon. iowans 60 and older have a separate line at 1-800-992-8161. iowa medicaid &amp; iowa health and wellness plan iowa medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. the iowa health and wellness plan covers low-income adults ages 19 to 64 under medicaid expansion. apply online through the hhs services portal at hhsservices.iowa.gov, or call 1-800-972-2017. iowa snap (food assistance) snap is iowa&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money loaded onto an ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. apply online through iowa&#39;s hhs services portal at hhsservices.iowa.gov, which handles snap, fip cash, and medicaid on one application, or at your local iowa hhs office. you can also apply by mail or ask questions at 1-800-972-2017. iowa wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. iowa wic runs through local clinics, and you set up benefits at an appointment. find your local clinic on the iowa wic map, or call 1-800-972-2017 to get started. unemployment insurance (iowa workforce development) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from iowa workforce development. file your claim online at iowaworks.gov, which is the fastest way, or get help from the customer service line at 1-866-239-0843, monday through friday, 8am to 4:30pm. weatherization assistance program (iowa) iowa&#39;s weatherization assistance program makes free energy-efficiency improvements, like insulation and air sealing, that lower future utility bills for income-eligible households. both homeowners and renters can qualify, with priority for older adults, people with disabilities, and families with children. apply through your local community action agency, the same offices that run energy assistance."><h2>Iowa programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/211-iowa/">211 Iowa</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Iowa phone, or call 1-800-244-7431, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. 211 Iowa connects you to thousands of local programs and answers every day. You can also text your zip code to 898211 or search at 211iowa.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/hawki-healthy-and-well-kids-in-iowa/">Hawki (Healthy and Well Kids in Iowa)</a><p>Hawki, short for Healthy and Well Kids in Iowa, is low-cost health and dental coverage for children under 19 in working families who earn too much for Medicaid. Most families pay nothing, and no family pays more than $40 a month for all of its children combined. Apply online through the HHS Services Portal at hhsservices.iowa.gov, or call Hawki Member Services at 1-800-257-8563.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/iowa-earned-income-tax-credit/">Iowa Earned Income Tax Credit</a><p>Iowa offers a refundable Earned Income Tax Credit worth 15 percent of your federal EITC. Because it is refundable, it pays out as cash even if you owe no state tax. Claim it on your Iowa income tax return after you qualify for the federal credit.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/iowa-energy-assistance-liheap/">Iowa Energy Assistance (LIHEAP)</a><p>Iowa&#39;s Low-Income Home Energy Assistance Program, LIHEAP, helps qualifying households pay part of their winter heating costs. You apply at your local community action agency, not online, and each agency serves a set of counties. Applications open October 1 for households with a member age 60 or older or with a disability, November 1 for everyone else, and close April 30.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/iowa-family-investment-program-fip-cash-assistance/">Iowa Family Investment Program (FIP Cash Assistance)</a><p>The Family Investment Program, FIP, is Iowa&#39;s version of TANF: monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children. Most adults take part in PROMISE JOBS, the work and training side of the program. Apply online through Iowa&#39;s HHS Services Portal at hhsservices.iowa.gov or at your local Iowa HHS office.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/iowa-legal-aid/">Iowa Legal Aid</a><p>Iowa Legal Aid provides free civil legal help to low-income Iowans on housing, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. Call the statewide intake line at 1-800-532-1503 if you are under 60, open Monday through Friday, 9 to 11am and 1:30 to 3:30pm and closed Thursday afternoon. Iowans 60 and older have a separate line at 1-800-992-8161.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/iowa-medicaid-and-iowa-health-and-wellness-plan/">Iowa Medicaid &amp; Iowa Health and Wellness Plan</a><p>Iowa Medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. The Iowa Health and Wellness Plan covers low-income adults ages 19 to 64 under Medicaid expansion. Apply online through the HHS Services Portal at hhsservices.iowa.gov, or call 1-800-972-2017.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/iowa-snap-food-assistance/">Iowa SNAP (Food Assistance)</a><p>SNAP is Iowa&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money loaded onto an EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. Apply online through Iowa&#39;s HHS Services Portal at hhsservices.iowa.gov, which handles SNAP, FIP cash, and Medicaid on one application, or at your local Iowa HHS office. You can also apply by mail or ask questions at 1-800-972-2017.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/iowa-wic/">Iowa WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. Iowa WIC runs through local clinics, and you set up benefits at an appointment. Find your local clinic on the Iowa WIC map, or call 1-800-972-2017 to get started.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-iowa-workforce-development/">Unemployment Insurance (Iowa Workforce Development)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from Iowa Workforce Development. File your claim online at iowaworks.gov, which is the fastest way, or get help from the customer service line at 1-866-239-0843, Monday through Friday, 8am to 4:30pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-iowa/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Iowa)</a><p>Iowa&#39;s Weatherization Assistance Program makes free energy-efficiency improvements, like insulation and air sealing, that lower future utility bills for income-eligible households. Both homeowners and renters can qualify, with priority for older adults, people with disabilities, and families with children. Apply through your local community action agency, the same offices that run energy assistance.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="kansas kancare (kansas medicaid &amp; chip) kancare is kansas&#39;s medicaid and chip program: free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, low-income parents, older adults, and people with disabilities. kansas has not expanded medicaid, so most adults without children or a disability do not qualify no matter how low their income. apply at applyforkancare.ks.gov, or call the kancare clearinghouse at 1-800-792-4884. kansas 211 dial 2-1-1 from a kansas phone for free, confidential help finding local assistance with rent, utilities, food, health care, and more. kansas 211 is run statewide by united way of the plains and is answered monday through friday, 7am to 7pm, with a recorded message pointing to resources after hours. you can also text your zip code to 898-211 or search anytime at 211kansas.org. kansas child care assistance child care assistance pays part of the cost of child care so parents can work, look for work, or attend school, with the benefit loaded onto the kansas benefits card and paid to dcf-enrolled providers. apply through the dcf self-service portal at cssp.kees.ks.gov, or call benefits assistance at 1-888-369-4777. processing can take up to 30 days. kansas earned income tax credit kansas has a refundable state earned income tax credit worth 17 percent of your federal eitc. because it is refundable, it can pay out as cash even when it is larger than the kansas tax you owe. claim it on your kansas income tax return (form k-40). you generally must qualify for the federal earned income tax credit first. kansas food assistance (snap) food assistance is kansas&#39;s name for snap: monthly food money on a kansas benefits card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. the department for children and families runs it. apply through the dcf self-service portal at cssp.kees.ks.gov, at your local dcf service center, or by phone. call benefits assistance at 1-888-369-4777 or the dcf customer service center at 1-833-765-2003. kansas lieap (low income energy assistance) lieap is kansas&#39;s low income energy assistance program: a once-a-year benefit that helps pay winter heating costs, run by the department for children and families. the application period is a set winter window that has closed for 2026, and dcf posts the next window when it is set. apply through the dcf self-service portal at cssp.kees.ks.gov during the window, or at a local dcf service center. questions: benefits assistance at 1-888-369-4777. kansas legal services kansas legal services provides free civil legal help to low-income kansans on housing and eviction, family, consumer and debt, public benefits, and other noncriminal matters, across all 105 counties. call intake at 316-267-3975, monday through friday, 8am to 4:30pm, or apply online at kansaslegalservices.org (the online application is open 8am monday through 4:30pm thursday). kansas tanf cash assistance (successful families program) kansas calls its tanf cash assistance the successful families program: monthly cash for low-income families with children, paid on the kansas benefits card, paired with work activities. strict time limits apply. apply through the dcf self-service portal at cssp.kees.ks.gov or at your local dcf service center. for help, call benefits assistance at 1-888-369-4777 or dcf customer service at 1-833-765-2003. kansas wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition help, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children under age 5. kansas wic runs through the department of health and environment and local clinics. start the online pre-application at kswic.com/preappportal, find your local clinic at kdhe.ks.gov/wic, or call the state wic office at 785-296-3683. unemployment insurance (kansas) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the kansas department of labor. file your claim and your weekly certifications through the claimant self service portal linked at dol.ks.gov/unemployment. if you need help, the contact center is 1-800-292-6333 (local 785-575-1460), open during weekday business hours (thursday closes earlier). weatherization assistance program (kansas) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible kansas households: air sealing, insulation, and heating and cooling repairs that lower future bills. kansas housing resources corporation runs the program through local provider agencies. contact khrc at 785-217-2001 to be connected to the provider serving your county, since khrc does not weatherize homes directly."><h2>Kansas programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/kancare-kansas-medicaid-and-chip/">KanCare (Kansas Medicaid &amp; CHIP)</a><p>KanCare is Kansas&#39;s Medicaid and CHIP program: free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, low-income parents, older adults, and people with disabilities. Kansas has not expanded Medicaid, so most adults without children or a disability do not qualify no matter how low their income. Apply at ApplyForKanCare.ks.gov, or call the KanCare Clearinghouse at 1-800-792-4884.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/kansas-211/">Kansas 211</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from a Kansas phone for free, confidential help finding local assistance with rent, utilities, food, health care, and more. Kansas 211 is run statewide by United Way of the Plains and is answered Monday through Friday, 7am to 7pm, with a recorded message pointing to resources after hours. You can also text your ZIP code to 898-211 or search anytime at 211Kansas.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/kansas-child-care-assistance/">Kansas Child Care Assistance</a><p>Child Care Assistance pays part of the cost of child care so parents can work, look for work, or attend school, with the benefit loaded onto the Kansas Benefits Card and paid to DCF-enrolled providers. Apply through the DCF Self-Service Portal at cssp.kees.ks.gov, or call Benefits Assistance at 1-888-369-4777. Processing can take up to 30 days.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/kansas-earned-income-tax-credit/">Kansas Earned Income Tax Credit</a><p>Kansas has a refundable state Earned Income Tax Credit worth 17 percent of your federal EITC. Because it is refundable, it can pay out as cash even when it is larger than the Kansas tax you owe. Claim it on your Kansas income tax return (Form K-40). You generally must qualify for the federal Earned Income Tax Credit first.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/kansas-food-assistance-snap/">Kansas Food Assistance (SNAP)</a><p>Food Assistance is Kansas&#39;s name for SNAP: monthly food money on a Kansas Benefits Card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. The Department for Children and Families runs it. Apply through the DCF Self-Service Portal at cssp.kees.ks.gov, at your local DCF service center, or by phone. Call Benefits Assistance at 1-888-369-4777 or the DCF Customer Service Center at 1-833-765-2003.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/kansas-lieap-low-income-energy-assistance/">Kansas LIEAP (Low Income Energy Assistance)</a><p>LIEAP is Kansas&#39;s Low Income Energy Assistance Program: a once-a-year benefit that helps pay winter heating costs, run by the Department for Children and Families. The application period is a set winter window that has closed for 2026, and DCF posts the next window when it is set. Apply through the DCF Self-Service Portal at cssp.kees.ks.gov during the window, or at a local DCF service center. Questions: Benefits Assistance at 1-888-369-4777.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/kansas-legal-services/">Kansas Legal Services</a><p>Kansas Legal Services provides free civil legal help to low-income Kansans on housing and eviction, family, consumer and debt, public benefits, and other noncriminal matters, across all 105 counties. Call intake at 316-267-3975, Monday through Friday, 8am to 4:30pm, or apply online at kansaslegalservices.org (the online application is open 8am Monday through 4:30pm Thursday).</p></article><article><a href="/programs/kansas-tanf-cash-assistance-successful-families-program/">Kansas TANF Cash Assistance (Successful Families Program)</a><p>Kansas calls its TANF cash assistance the Successful Families Program: monthly cash for low-income families with children, paid on the Kansas Benefits Card, paired with work activities. Strict time limits apply. Apply through the DCF Self-Service Portal at cssp.kees.ks.gov or at your local DCF service center. For help, call Benefits Assistance at 1-888-369-4777 or DCF Customer Service at 1-833-765-2003.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/kansas-wic/">Kansas WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition help, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children under age 5. Kansas WIC runs through the Department of Health and Environment and local clinics. Start the online pre-application at kswic.com/PreAppPortal, find your local clinic at kdhe.ks.gov/wic, or call the state WIC office at 785-296-3683.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-kansas/">Unemployment Insurance (Kansas)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Kansas Department of Labor. File your claim and your weekly certifications through the Claimant Self Service Portal linked at dol.ks.gov/unemployment. If you need help, the Contact Center is 1-800-292-6333 (local 785-575-1460), open during weekday business hours (Thursday closes earlier).</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-kansas/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Kansas)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible Kansas households: air sealing, insulation, and heating and cooling repairs that lower future bills. Kansas Housing Resources Corporation runs the program through local provider agencies. Contact KHRC at 785-217-2001 to be connected to the provider serving your county, since KHRC does not weatherize homes directly.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="kentucky kentucky 211 dial 2-1-1 from any kentucky phone for free, confidential help finding local assistance with food, housing, utility bills, healthcare, and more. kentucky 211 is a united way service that answers around the clock, every day. you can also text your zip code to 898211 or search at kentucky211.org. kentucky child care assistance program (ccap) the child care assistance program, ccap, helps low-income kentucky families pay for child care while parents work, train, or go to school. apply online through kynect at kynect.ky.gov or by phone at 1-855-306-8959. a prescreening tool on kynect can tell you if you likely qualify before you apply. kentucky k-tap (cash assistance) k-tap, the kentucky transitional assistance program, is the state&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children. it is kentucky&#39;s version of tanf, and most adults take part in the kentucky works program, which helps you move toward a job. apply online through kynect at kynect.ky.gov, by phone at 1-855-306-8959, or at your local dcbs office. kentucky liheap (home energy assistance) liheap helps low-income kentucky households with home heating and cooling costs. it runs through community action kentucky&#39;s network of local community action agencies, which cover all 120 counties, not through a single state office. contact the community action agency serving your county to apply, or call 1-800-456-3452 to find your local office. applications open in set seasonal windows. kentucky legal aid (civil legal help) kentucky has four regional civil legal aid programs that give free legal help to low-income residents on housing, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal problems. which office serves you depends on where you live: kentucky legal aid in the south and west, legal aid society around louisville, legal aid of the bluegrass in the north, and appalred legal aid in eastern kentucky. find your program and free self-help legal information at kyjustice.org. kentucky medicaid &amp; kchip kentucky medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. kentucky expanded medicaid in 2014 to cover adults ages 19 to 64 with income up to 138 percent of the federal poverty level, and kchip covers children through chip. apply online through kynect at kynect.ky.gov/benefits, or call kynect at 1-855-459-6328. kentucky snap (food assistance) snap is kentucky&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money loaded onto an ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. apply online through kynect at kynect.ky.gov, by phone at 1-855-306-8959, or at your local department for community based services (dcbs) office. the dcbs family support line answers monday through friday, 8am to 4:30pm eastern. kentucky wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant, breastfeeding, and postpartum women, infants, and children up to age 5. kentucky wic runs through local health departments. call the state wic line at 1-877-597-0367 to find your local clinic, or learn more at the cabinet for health and family services wic page. unemployment insurance (kentucky) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the kentucky office of unemployment insurance. file your claim online at kcc.ky.gov, which is the fastest way, or by phone at 502-564-2900 using the automated system. make sure you are on a .ky.gov website when you file, and only trust unemployment emails from an @ky.gov address. weatherization assistance program (kentucky) kentucky&#39;s weatherization assistance program makes free energy-efficiency repairs for income-eligible households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system fixes that lower future bills. kentucky housing corporation runs the program and it is delivered by local community action agencies, so you apply through the agency serving your county. homeowners and renters can both qualify."><h2>Kentucky programs (10)</h2><article><a href="/programs/kentucky-211/">Kentucky 211</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Kentucky phone for free, confidential help finding local assistance with food, housing, utility bills, healthcare, and more. Kentucky 211 is a United Way service that answers around the clock, every day. You can also text your zip code to 898211 or search at kentucky211.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/kentucky-child-care-assistance-program-ccap/">Kentucky Child Care Assistance Program (CCAP)</a><p>The Child Care Assistance Program, CCAP, helps low-income Kentucky families pay for child care while parents work, train, or go to school. Apply online through kynect at kynect.ky.gov or by phone at 1-855-306-8959. A prescreening tool on kynect can tell you if you likely qualify before you apply.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/kentucky-k-tap-cash-assistance/">Kentucky K-TAP (Cash Assistance)</a><p>K-TAP, the Kentucky Transitional Assistance Program, is the state&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children. It is Kentucky&#39;s version of TANF, and most adults take part in the Kentucky Works program, which helps you move toward a job. Apply online through kynect at kynect.ky.gov, by phone at 1-855-306-8959, or at your local DCBS office.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/kentucky-liheap-home-energy-assistance/">Kentucky LIHEAP (Home Energy Assistance)</a><p>LIHEAP helps low-income Kentucky households with home heating and cooling costs. It runs through Community Action Kentucky&#39;s network of local Community Action Agencies, which cover all 120 counties, not through a single state office. Contact the Community Action Agency serving your county to apply, or call 1-800-456-3452 to find your local office. Applications open in set seasonal windows.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/kentucky-legal-aid-civil-legal-help/">Kentucky Legal Aid (Civil Legal Help)</a><p>Kentucky has four regional civil legal aid programs that give free legal help to low-income residents on housing, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal problems. Which office serves you depends on where you live: Kentucky Legal Aid in the south and west, Legal Aid Society around Louisville, Legal Aid of the Bluegrass in the north, and AppalReD Legal Aid in eastern Kentucky. Find your program and free self-help legal information at kyjustice.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/kentucky-medicaid-and-kchip/">Kentucky Medicaid &amp; KCHIP</a><p>Kentucky Medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. Kentucky expanded Medicaid in 2014 to cover adults ages 19 to 64 with income up to 138 percent of the federal poverty level, and KCHIP covers children through CHIP. Apply online through kynect at kynect.ky.gov/benefits, or call kynect at 1-855-459-6328.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/kentucky-snap-food-assistance/">Kentucky SNAP (Food Assistance)</a><p>SNAP is Kentucky&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money loaded onto an EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. Apply online through kynect at kynect.ky.gov, by phone at 1-855-306-8959, or at your local Department for Community Based Services (DCBS) office. The DCBS Family Support line answers Monday through Friday, 8am to 4:30pm Eastern.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/kentucky-wic/">Kentucky WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant, breastfeeding, and postpartum women, infants, and children up to age 5. Kentucky WIC runs through local health departments. Call the state WIC line at 1-877-597-0367 to find your local clinic, or learn more at the Cabinet for Health and Family Services WIC page.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-kentucky/">Unemployment Insurance (Kentucky)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Kentucky Office of Unemployment Insurance. File your claim online at kcc.ky.gov, which is the fastest way, or by phone at 502-564-2900 using the automated system. Make sure you are on a .ky.gov website when you file, and only trust unemployment emails from an @ky.gov address.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-kentucky/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Kentucky)</a><p>Kentucky&#39;s Weatherization Assistance Program makes free energy-efficiency repairs for income-eligible households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system fixes that lower future bills. Kentucky Housing Corporation runs the program and it is delivered by local Community Action Agencies, so you apply through the agency serving your county. Homeowners and renters can both qualify.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="king / stokes county, nc city of king utility billing and collections the city of king bills you for city water and sewer. if you cannot pay your bill on time, call the billing office before the due date. unpaid bills can get late fees and your service can be shut off. you can pay in person at 229 s. main street, online, or by phone at 1-844-778-1892. crisis control ministry crisis control ministry helps stokes and forsyth county residents in a short-term emergency. they can help pay rent or mortgage, utility bills, and heating fuel. they also run a food pantry where you choose your own food and a free licensed pharmacy. call to set up a phone or in-person interview. crisis intervention program (cip) — stokes county dss cip helps if you are in a heating or cooling emergency, like a shut-off notice in winter or summer. it can pay up to $600 per crisis, sent straight to your utility company. you can get help more than once, up to $600 total per state fiscal year. east stokes outreach ministry east stokes outreach ministry serves people in the eastern part of stokes county, near walnut cove. you can get fresh food from their pantry, low-cost clothing and household items from their thrift store, and emergency financial help when times are hard. king outreach ministry king outreach ministry helps families in western stokes county during a crisis. you can get food from their pantry, clothing, help with prescription medicines, and help with energy and heating costs. they can also point you to other agencies that can help. low income energy assistance program (lieap) — stokes county dss lieap pays part of your heating bill once a year. the money goes straight to your heat company, not to you. people age 60 or older and disabled people getting daas services apply in december. everyone else applies starting in january, until money runs out or march 31."><h2>King / Stokes County, NC programs (6)</h2><article><a href="/programs/city-of-king-utility-billing-and-collections/">City of King Utility Billing and Collections</a><p>The City of King bills you for city water and sewer. If you cannot pay your bill on time, call the billing office before the due date. Unpaid bills can get late fees and your service can be shut off. You can pay in person at 229 S. Main Street, online, or by phone at 1-844-778-1892.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/crisis-control-ministry/">Crisis Control Ministry</a><p>Crisis Control Ministry helps Stokes and Forsyth County residents in a short-term emergency. They can help pay rent or mortgage, utility bills, and heating fuel. They also run a food pantry where you choose your own food and a free licensed pharmacy. Call to set up a phone or in-person interview.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/crisis-intervention-program-cip-stokes-county-dss/">Crisis Intervention Program (CIP) — Stokes County DSS</a><p>CIP helps if you are in a heating or cooling emergency, like a shut-off notice in winter or summer. It can pay up to $600 per crisis, sent straight to your utility company. You can get help more than once, up to $600 total per state fiscal year.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/east-stokes-outreach-ministry/">East Stokes Outreach Ministry</a><p>East Stokes Outreach Ministry serves people in the eastern part of Stokes County, near Walnut Cove. You can get fresh food from their pantry, low-cost clothing and household items from their thrift store, and emergency financial help when times are hard.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/king-outreach-ministry/">King Outreach Ministry</a><p>King Outreach Ministry helps families in western Stokes County during a crisis. You can get food from their pantry, clothing, help with prescription medicines, and help with energy and heating costs. They can also point you to other agencies that can help.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/low-income-energy-assistance-program-lieap-stokes-county-dss/">Low Income Energy Assistance Program (LIEAP) — Stokes County DSS</a><p>LIEAP pays part of your heating bill once a year. The money goes straight to your heat company, not to you. People age 60 or older and disabled people getting DAAS services apply in December. Everyone else applies starting in January, until money runs out or March 31.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="louisiana child care assistance program (louisiana ccap) the child care assistance program, ccap, helps eligible louisiana families pay for child care so parents can work, go to school, or train. the louisiana department of education runs it. apply through the cafe portal at cafe-cp.doe.louisiana.gov or by paper application, or call 1-877-453-2721 for help. healthy louisiana (louisiana medicaid) &amp; lachip healthy louisiana is the state&#39;s medicaid program: free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. louisiana expanded medicaid, so adults 19 to 64 with income up to 138 percent of the federal poverty level can qualify. lachip covers children. apply at mymedicaid.la.gov, or call louisiana medicaid at 1-888-342-6207, monday through friday, 8am to 4:30pm. liheap energy assistance (louisiana) liheap helps low-income louisiana households pay home energy bills, through the louisiana housing corporation and local community action agencies. it covers heating help in winter, cooling help in summer, and year-round crisis help when your power is shut off or about to be. apply online through lhc or through the local agency serving your parish. louisiana 211 dial 2-1-1 from any louisiana phone for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. louisiana 211 answers around the clock, every day, with live specialists and interpreters. you can also text your zip code to 898211 or search at louisiana211.org. louisiana earned income tax credit louisiana has a refundable state earned income tax credit worth 5 percent of your federal eitc. because it is refundable, it can pay out as cash even if you owe no state income tax. you must qualify for the federal eitc first, then claim the louisiana credit on line 16 of your louisiana it-540 return. louisiana fitap (cash assistance) the family independence temporary assistance program, fitap, is louisiana&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children. most work-eligible adults take part in the step program (strategies to empower people), which pairs work activities with support like child care and transportation. apply online through the cafe self-service portal or by phone at 1-888-524-3578. federal and state time limits apply. louisiana legal aid (louisianalawhelp / slls / acadiana) louisiana&#39;s legal aid programs give free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing, family, public benefits, and consumer matters. find your local office and free self-help guides at louisianalawhelp.org. southeast louisiana legal services covers 22 southeast parishes and takes intake at 1-844-244-7871. acadiana legal service corporation covers much of the rest of the state. louisiana snap (food assistance) snap is louisiana&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money loaded onto a louisiana purchase ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. snap is now run by the louisiana department of health&#39;s office of economic stability, but you apply the same way as always: online through the cafe self-service portal, or by phone at 1-888-524-3578 (1-888-la-help-u). you can also text getsnap to 898211 for information. louisiana wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. louisiana wic runs through local health units and clinics. find your clinic and start an application at louisianawic.org, or call 1-800-251-2229 (1-800-251-baby). unemployment insurance (louisiana) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the louisiana workforce commission. file online through the hire self-service system at louisianaworks.net, which is the fastest way, or call the ui call center at 1-866-783-5567, monday through friday, 8am to 4:30pm. weatherization assistance program (louisiana) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible louisiana households: insulation, air sealing, and heating and cooling repairs that lower future bills. the louisiana housing corporation runs the weatherization assistance program through regional providers. contact the provider serving your area to apply, since lhc does not take applications directly. both homeowners and renters can qualify."><h2>Louisiana programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/child-care-assistance-program-louisiana-ccap/">Child Care Assistance Program (Louisiana CCAP)</a><p>The Child Care Assistance Program, CCAP, helps eligible Louisiana families pay for child care so parents can work, go to school, or train. The Louisiana Department of Education runs it. Apply through the CAFE portal at cafe-cp.doe.louisiana.gov or by paper application, or call 1-877-453-2721 for help.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/healthy-louisiana-louisiana-medicaid-and-lachip/">Healthy Louisiana (Louisiana Medicaid) &amp; LaCHIP</a><p>Healthy Louisiana is the state&#39;s Medicaid program: free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. Louisiana expanded Medicaid, so adults 19 to 64 with income up to 138 percent of the federal poverty level can qualify. LaCHIP covers children. Apply at MyMedicaid.la.gov, or call Louisiana Medicaid at 1-888-342-6207, Monday through Friday, 8am to 4:30pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/liheap-energy-assistance-louisiana/">LIHEAP Energy Assistance (Louisiana)</a><p>LIHEAP helps low-income Louisiana households pay home energy bills, through the Louisiana Housing Corporation and local Community Action Agencies. It covers heating help in winter, cooling help in summer, and year-round crisis help when your power is shut off or about to be. Apply online through LHC or through the local agency serving your parish.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/louisiana-211/">Louisiana 211</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Louisiana phone for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. Louisiana 211 answers around the clock, every day, with live specialists and interpreters. You can also text your zip code to 898211 or search at louisiana211.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/louisiana-earned-income-tax-credit/">Louisiana Earned Income Tax Credit</a><p>Louisiana has a refundable state Earned Income Tax Credit worth 5 percent of your federal EITC. Because it is refundable, it can pay out as cash even if you owe no state income tax. You must qualify for the federal EITC first, then claim the Louisiana credit on Line 16 of your Louisiana IT-540 return.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/louisiana-fitap-cash-assistance/">Louisiana FITAP (Cash Assistance)</a><p>The Family Independence Temporary Assistance Program, FITAP, is Louisiana&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children. Most work-eligible adults take part in the STEP program (Strategies to Empower People), which pairs work activities with support like child care and transportation. Apply online through the CAFE Self-Service Portal or by phone at 1-888-524-3578. Federal and state time limits apply.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/louisiana-legal-aid-louisianalawhelp-slls-acadiana/">Louisiana Legal Aid (LouisianaLawHelp / SLLS / Acadiana)</a><p>Louisiana&#39;s legal aid programs give free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing, family, public benefits, and consumer matters. Find your local office and free self-help guides at louisianalawhelp.org. Southeast Louisiana Legal Services covers 22 southeast parishes and takes intake at 1-844-244-7871. Acadiana Legal Service Corporation covers much of the rest of the state.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/louisiana-snap-food-assistance/">Louisiana SNAP (Food Assistance)</a><p>SNAP is Louisiana&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money loaded onto a Louisiana Purchase EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. SNAP is now run by the Louisiana Department of Health&#39;s Office of Economic Stability, but you apply the same way as always: online through the CAFE Self-Service Portal, or by phone at 1-888-524-3578 (1-888-LA-HELP-U). You can also text GETSNAP to 898211 for information.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/louisiana-wic/">Louisiana WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. Louisiana WIC runs through local health units and clinics. Find your clinic and start an application at louisianawic.org, or call 1-800-251-2229 (1-800-251-BABY).</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-louisiana/">Unemployment Insurance (Louisiana)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Louisiana Workforce Commission. File online through the HiRE self-service system at louisianaworks.net, which is the fastest way, or call the UI Call Center at 1-866-783-5567, Monday through Friday, 8am to 4:30pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-louisiana/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Louisiana)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible Louisiana households: insulation, air sealing, and heating and cooling repairs that lower future bills. The Louisiana Housing Corporation runs the Weatherization Assistance Program through regional providers. Contact the provider serving your area to apply, since LHC does not take applications directly. Both homeowners and renters can qualify.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="maine 211 maine dial 2-1-1 from any maine phone, or call 1-866-811-5695, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. 211 maine answers around the clock, every day, in all languages. you can also text your zip code to 898-211, or call 1-877-463-6207 from out of state. general assistance (maine) general assistance is maine&#39;s municipal safety-net program that helps people meet immediate basic needs when they cannot afford them, including rent, mortgage, room rent, temporary housing, heating fuel, food, and household supplies. apply in person at your local town or city office, and help is paid as a voucher directly to the vendor. if you cannot reach your local office, call the state hotline at 1-800-442-6003. home energy assistance program (heap / liheap) heap is maine&#39;s liheap heating help for income-eligible homeowners and renters, covering fuel costs, emergency fuel delivery, and some energy-related repairs and utility payments. local community action agencies handle applications, not mainehousing directly. apply online, or find your local agency by zip code. the 2026-2027 season opened august 3, 2026 and runs until funds run out or may 29, 2027. for more information call 1-877-544-3271. maine earned income tax credit maine&#39;s earned income credit is refundable and equal to 25 percent of your federal earned income tax credit, or 50 percent for workers with no qualifying children. because it is refundable for maine residents and part-year residents, it can pay out as cash even if you owe no state tax. claim it on form 1040me, schedule a. questions go to maine revenue services at income.tax@maine.gov. maine snap (food supplement) snap, called the food supplement program in maine, puts monthly food money on an ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. apply online through my maine connection at mymaineconnection.gov, the state&#39;s one application that also covers mainecare and tanf, or by phone through the office for family independence at 1-855-797-4357, monday through friday, 7am to 4pm. maine tanf (cash assistance) temporary assistance for needy families, tanf, is maine&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children and pregnant people. most adults take part in the aspire work program, which helps you move toward a job. apply online through my maine connection at mymaineconnection.gov, or call the office for family independence at 1-855-797-4357, monday through friday, 7am to 4pm, and choose the tanf option. maine wic nutrition program wic gives healthy food, nutrition education, breastfeeding and lactation support, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. maine wic runs through local agencies across the state and is open to anyone living in maine, including migrant workers. contact your local wic office to apply, or call 1-800-437-9300 for more information. mainecare (maine medicaid) mainecare is maine&#39;s medicaid program, free or low-cost health insurance for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. maine expanded mainecare, so low-income adults now qualify on income alone. apply online through my maine connection at mymaineconnection.gov, or call the office for family independence at 1-855-797-4357. pine tree legal assistance pine tree legal assistance gives free civil legal help to low-income maine residents on housing and eviction, public benefits like snap, tanf, and mainecare, consumer and debt problems, and more. call the statewide line at 207-942-8322 during call-in hours: mondays 12pm to 2:30pm, tuesdays and thursdays 9am to 11:30am. interpreters are free. free legal information is at ptla.org. unemployment insurance (maine department of labor) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the maine department of labor. file online through reemployme, which is the fastest way, or call the claim center at 1-800-593-7660, monday through friday, 8am to 3pm. tty users dial maine relay 711. weatherization assistance program (maine) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible maine households: a home energy assessment first, then air sealing, insulation, and heating-system improvements that lower future bills. community action agencies deliver the program across maine. homeowners, renters, and owners of small 2- to 4-unit buildings can qualify. contact your local community action agency to apply, since applying for heap also puts you in line for weatherization."><h2>Maine programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/211-maine/">211 Maine</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Maine phone, or call 1-866-811-5695, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. 211 Maine answers around the clock, every day, in all languages. You can also text your zip code to 898-211, or call 1-877-463-6207 from out of state.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/general-assistance-maine/">General Assistance (Maine)</a><p>General Assistance is Maine&#39;s municipal safety-net program that helps people meet immediate basic needs when they cannot afford them, including rent, mortgage, room rent, temporary housing, heating fuel, food, and household supplies. Apply in person at your local town or city office, and help is paid as a voucher directly to the vendor. If you cannot reach your local office, call the state hotline at 1-800-442-6003.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/home-energy-assistance-program-heap-liheap/">Home Energy Assistance Program (HEAP / LIHEAP)</a><p>HEAP is Maine&#39;s LIHEAP heating help for income-eligible homeowners and renters, covering fuel costs, emergency fuel delivery, and some energy-related repairs and utility payments. Local Community Action Agencies handle applications, not MaineHousing directly. Apply online, or find your local agency by zip code. The 2026-2027 season opened August 3, 2026 and runs until funds run out or May 29, 2027. For more information call 1-877-544-3271.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/maine-earned-income-tax-credit/">Maine Earned Income Tax Credit</a><p>Maine&#39;s Earned Income Credit is refundable and equal to 25 percent of your federal Earned Income Tax Credit, or 50 percent for workers with no qualifying children. Because it is refundable for Maine residents and part-year residents, it can pay out as cash even if you owe no state tax. Claim it on Form 1040ME, Schedule A. Questions go to Maine Revenue Services at income.tax@maine.gov.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/maine-snap-food-supplement/">Maine SNAP (Food Supplement)</a><p>SNAP, called the Food Supplement Program in Maine, puts monthly food money on an EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. Apply online through My Maine Connection at mymaineconnection.gov, the state&#39;s one application that also covers MaineCare and TANF, or by phone through the Office for Family Independence at 1-855-797-4357, Monday through Friday, 7am to 4pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/maine-tanf-cash-assistance/">Maine TANF (Cash Assistance)</a><p>Temporary Assistance for Needy Families, TANF, is Maine&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children and pregnant people. Most adults take part in the ASPIRE work program, which helps you move toward a job. Apply online through My Maine Connection at mymaineconnection.gov, or call the Office for Family Independence at 1-855-797-4357, Monday through Friday, 7am to 4pm, and choose the TANF option.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/maine-wic-nutrition-program/">Maine WIC Nutrition Program</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, nutrition education, breastfeeding and lactation support, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. Maine WIC runs through local agencies across the state and is open to anyone living in Maine, including migrant workers. Contact your local WIC office to apply, or call 1-800-437-9300 for more information.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/mainecare-maine-medicaid/">MaineCare (Maine Medicaid)</a><p>MaineCare is Maine&#39;s Medicaid program, free or low-cost health insurance for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. Maine expanded MaineCare, so low-income adults now qualify on income alone. Apply online through My Maine Connection at mymaineconnection.gov, or call the Office for Family Independence at 1-855-797-4357.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/pine-tree-legal-assistance/">Pine Tree Legal Assistance</a><p>Pine Tree Legal Assistance gives free civil legal help to low-income Maine residents on housing and eviction, public benefits like SNAP, TANF, and MaineCare, consumer and debt problems, and more. Call the statewide line at 207-942-8322 during call-in hours: Mondays 12pm to 2:30pm, Tuesdays and Thursdays 9am to 11:30am. Interpreters are free. Free legal information is at ptla.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-maine-department-of-labor/">Unemployment Insurance (Maine Department of Labor)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Maine Department of Labor. File online through ReEmployME, which is the fastest way, or call the claim center at 1-800-593-7660, Monday through Friday, 8am to 3pm. TTY users dial Maine Relay 711.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-maine/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Maine)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible Maine households: a home energy assessment first, then air sealing, insulation, and heating-system improvements that lower future bills. Community Action Agencies deliver the program across Maine. Homeowners, renters, and owners of small 2- to 4-unit buildings can qualify. Contact your local Community Action Agency to apply, since applying for HEAP also puts you in line for weatherization.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="maryland 211 maryland dial 2-1-1 from any maryland phone for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. 211 maryland is a statewide service that answers around the clock, every day of the year. you can also text your keyword to 898-211 for ongoing support, or search at 211md.org. maryland earned income tax credit maryland offers a state earned income tax credit on top of the federal credit, and part of it is refundable, so it can pay out even if you owe no tax. the nonrefundable state credit equals 50 percent of your federal eitc, and the refundable credit equals 45 percent of your federal eitc. claim it on your maryland return at marylandtaxes.gov. maryland energy assistance / office of home energy programs (ohep) the office of home energy programs, ohep, helps low-income maryland households with home energy costs. it runs the maryland energy assistance program (meap) for heating grants, the electric universal service program (eusp) for electric bills, and arrearage retirement help for past-due balances. apply online through marylandbenefits at marylandbenefits.gov, at your local home energy program office, or by phone at 1-800-332-6347. maryland legal aid maryland legal aid provides free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing and eviction, public benefits, family, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. call the statewide intake line at 1-888-465-2468 to reach the office serving your area, or start an application online and find your local office at marylandlegalaid.org. maryland medicaid (healthchoice) &amp; mchp maryland medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. most members get care through healthchoice, maryland&#39;s medicaid managed care program, and mchp covers children through chip. apply through maryland health connection at marylandhealthconnection.gov, or call 1-855-642-8572, monday through friday, 8am to 6pm. maryland snap (food supplement program) snap is maryland&#39;s food supplement program: monthly food money loaded onto an ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. apply online through marylandbenefits at marylandbenefits.gov, which also handles medicaid, cash assistance, and energy help on one account, or at your local department of social services. you can also call the dhs customer call center at 1-800-332-6347, monday through friday, 8:30am to 5pm. maryland temporary cash assistance (tca) temporary cash assistance, tca, is maryland&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children, and it is the state&#39;s version of the federal tanf program. most adults take part in work activities while they receive it. apply online through marylandbenefits at marylandbenefits.gov or at your local department of social services, or call 1-800-332-6347. maryland temporary disability assistance program (tdap) tdap is maryland&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income adults with a disability who do not have dependent children. a licensed medical practitioner must verify the disability. apply online through marylandbenefits at marylandbenefits.gov or at your local department of social services, or call 1-800-332-6347. maryland wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. maryland wic runs through local county offices of the maryland department of health. call 1-800-242-4942 to find your local clinic, or apply through the wic client portal linked at health.maryland.gov/phpa/wic. unemployment insurance (maryland) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the maryland department of labor. file online through the beacon system at beacon.labor.maryland.gov, which is the fastest way, or call a claims agent at 667-207-6520, monday through friday, 8am to 4pm. you must certify each week to keep payments coming. weatherization assistance program (maryland) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible maryland households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system improvements that lower future bills. the department of housing and community development runs the program through local weatherization agencies. contact the local agency serving your area to apply, or call 1-855-583-8976 for help finding it."><h2>Maryland programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/211-maryland/">211 Maryland</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Maryland phone for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. 211 Maryland is a statewide service that answers around the clock, every day of the year. You can also text your keyword to 898-211 for ongoing support, or search at 211md.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/maryland-earned-income-tax-credit/">Maryland Earned Income Tax Credit</a><p>Maryland offers a state Earned Income Tax Credit on top of the federal credit, and part of it is refundable, so it can pay out even if you owe no tax. The nonrefundable state credit equals 50 percent of your federal EITC, and the refundable credit equals 45 percent of your federal EITC. Claim it on your Maryland return at marylandtaxes.gov.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/maryland-energy-assistance-office-of-home-energy-programs-ohep/">Maryland Energy Assistance / Office of Home Energy Programs (OHEP)</a><p>The Office of Home Energy Programs, OHEP, helps low-income Maryland households with home energy costs. It runs the Maryland Energy Assistance Program (MEAP) for heating grants, the Electric Universal Service Program (EUSP) for electric bills, and arrearage retirement help for past-due balances. Apply online through MarylandBenefits at MarylandBenefits.gov, at your local home energy program office, or by phone at 1-800-332-6347.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/maryland-legal-aid/">Maryland Legal Aid</a><p>Maryland Legal Aid provides free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing and eviction, public benefits, family, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. Call the statewide intake line at 1-888-465-2468 to reach the office serving your area, or start an application online and find your local office at marylandlegalaid.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/maryland-medicaid-healthchoice-and-mchp/">Maryland Medicaid (HealthChoice) &amp; MCHP</a><p>Maryland Medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. Most members get care through HealthChoice, Maryland&#39;s Medicaid managed care program, and MCHP covers children through CHIP. Apply through Maryland Health Connection at marylandhealthconnection.gov, or call 1-855-642-8572, Monday through Friday, 8am to 6pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/maryland-snap-food-supplement-program/">Maryland SNAP (Food Supplement Program)</a><p>SNAP is Maryland&#39;s Food Supplement Program: monthly food money loaded onto an EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. Apply online through MarylandBenefits at MarylandBenefits.gov, which also handles Medicaid, cash assistance, and energy help on one account, or at your local department of social services. You can also call the DHS Customer Call Center at 1-800-332-6347, Monday through Friday, 8:30am to 5pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/maryland-temporary-cash-assistance-tca/">Maryland Temporary Cash Assistance (TCA)</a><p>Temporary Cash Assistance, TCA, is Maryland&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children, and it is the state&#39;s version of the federal TANF program. Most adults take part in work activities while they receive it. Apply online through MarylandBenefits at MarylandBenefits.gov or at your local department of social services, or call 1-800-332-6347.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/maryland-temporary-disability-assistance-program-tdap/">Maryland Temporary Disability Assistance Program (TDAP)</a><p>TDAP is Maryland&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income adults with a disability who do not have dependent children. A licensed medical practitioner must verify the disability. Apply online through MarylandBenefits at MarylandBenefits.gov or at your local department of social services, or call 1-800-332-6347.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/maryland-wic/">Maryland WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. Maryland WIC runs through local county offices of the Maryland Department of Health. Call 1-800-242-4942 to find your local clinic, or apply through the WIC Client Portal linked at health.maryland.gov/phpa/wic.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-maryland/">Unemployment Insurance (Maryland)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Maryland Department of Labor. File online through the BEACON system at beacon.labor.maryland.gov, which is the fastest way, or call a claims agent at 667-207-6520, Monday through Friday, 8am to 4pm. You must certify each week to keep payments coming.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-maryland/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Maryland)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible Maryland households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system improvements that lower future bills. The Department of Housing and Community Development runs the program through local weatherization agencies. Contact the local agency serving your area to apply, or call 1-855-583-8976 for help finding it.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="massachusetts fuel assistance / liheap (massachusetts) fuel assistance, the low income home energy assistance program (liheap), helps income-eligible massachusetts households pay winter heating bills, whether you heat with oil, gas, electricity, propane, or wood. local fuel assistance agencies take applications; the online season opens october 1. find your agency or apply through the heap portal, and call the cold relief heatline at 1-800-632-8175 with questions. mass 211 dial 2-1-1 from any massachusetts phone, or call 1-877-211-6277, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. mass 211 answers around the clock, every day, with information in more than 150 languages. you can also chat or search the resource directory at mass211.org. masshealth (medicaid) &amp; children&#39;s medical security plan masshealth is massachusetts&#39;s medicaid and chip program: free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. the children&#39;s medical security plan covers uninsured children under 19 who do not qualify for masshealth, regardless of immigration status. apply online or call masshealth customer service at 1-800-841-2900, monday through friday, 8am to 5pm. massachusetts earned income tax credit massachusetts has a refundable earned income tax credit worth 40 percent of your federal eitc, raised from 30 percent for tax years starting on or after january 1, 2023. because it is refundable, it pays out as cash even if you owe no tax. you must qualify for the federal eitc and file a massachusetts return to claim it. massachusetts legal aid (masslegalhelp / masslrf) massachusetts legal aid programs provide free civil legal help to low-income residents on eviction, housing, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. find free plain-language legal information at masslegalhelp.org, and use the massachusetts legal resource finder at masslrf.org to see whether you qualify for a legal aid program that serves your city or town. massachusetts snap (food assistance) snap is massachusetts&#39;s food assistance program, run by the department of transitional assistance (dta): monthly food money on an ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. apply online at dtaconnect.com, which takes about 20 minutes, or by phone through the dta assistance line at 1-877-382-2363, press 7, open monday through friday, 8:15am to 4:45pm. massachusetts tafdc (cash assistance) transitional aid to families with dependent children, tafdc, is massachusetts&#39;s monthly cash assistance for families with children and pregnant people in the last 120 days of pregnancy who have little or no income. it is run by the department of transitional assistance. apply online at dtaconnect.com or by phone at 1-877-382-2363. approval also brings masshealth automatically. massachusetts wic nutrition program wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. massachusetts wic runs through local clinics. begin online at mass.gov, or call 1-800-942-1007, monday through friday, 9am to 5pm, to reach the wic clinic nearest you and set up an appointment. raft (residential assistance for families in transition) raft is massachusetts&#39;s active emergency housing assistance: up to $7,000 per household in a 12-month period to help with overdue rent, mortgage payments, utility bills, and moving costs when you face eviction, foreclosure, or loss of utilities. apply online at applyhousinghelp.mass.gov, where your application is handled by a regional administering agency. both a tenant and a landlord application are needed for rental help. unemployment insurance (massachusetts dua) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the department of unemployment assistance (dua). file online through ui online at mass.gov/dua, available daily from 6am to 10pm, or by phone through the teleclaim center at 1-877-626-6800, monday through thursday, 8:30am to 4:30pm. the phone line is closed fridays so staff can process claims. weatherization assistance program (massachusetts) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible massachusetts households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system improvements that lower future bills. your fuel assistance application also serves as your weatherization application, so households eligible for home energy assistance are eligible for weatherization. contact your local agency, or call the cold relief heatline at 1-800-632-8175."><h2>Massachusetts programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/fuel-assistance-liheap-massachusetts/">Fuel Assistance / LIHEAP (Massachusetts)</a><p>Fuel Assistance, the Low Income Home Energy Assistance Program (LIHEAP), helps income-eligible Massachusetts households pay winter heating bills, whether you heat with oil, gas, electricity, propane, or wood. Local Fuel Assistance agencies take applications; the online season opens October 1. Find your agency or apply through the HEAP portal, and call the Cold Relief Heatline at 1-800-632-8175 with questions.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/mass-211/">Mass 211</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Massachusetts phone, or call 1-877-211-6277, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. Mass 211 answers around the clock, every day, with information in more than 150 languages. You can also chat or search the resource directory at mass211.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/masshealth-medicaid-and-childrens-medical-security-plan/">MassHealth (Medicaid) &amp; Children&#39;s Medical Security Plan</a><p>MassHealth is Massachusetts&#39;s Medicaid and CHIP program: free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. The Children&#39;s Medical Security Plan covers uninsured children under 19 who do not qualify for MassHealth, regardless of immigration status. Apply online or call MassHealth Customer Service at 1-800-841-2900, Monday through Friday, 8am to 5pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/massachusetts-earned-income-tax-credit/">Massachusetts Earned Income Tax Credit</a><p>Massachusetts has a refundable Earned Income Tax Credit worth 40 percent of your federal EITC, raised from 30 percent for tax years starting on or after January 1, 2023. Because it is refundable, it pays out as cash even if you owe no tax. You must qualify for the federal EITC and file a Massachusetts return to claim it.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/massachusetts-legal-aid-masslegalhelp-masslrf/">Massachusetts Legal Aid (MassLegalHelp / MassLRF)</a><p>Massachusetts legal aid programs provide free civil legal help to low-income residents on eviction, housing, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. Find free plain-language legal information at masslegalhelp.org, and use the Massachusetts Legal Resource Finder at masslrf.org to see whether you qualify for a legal aid program that serves your city or town.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/massachusetts-snap-food-assistance/">Massachusetts SNAP (Food Assistance)</a><p>SNAP is Massachusetts&#39;s food assistance program, run by the Department of Transitional Assistance (DTA): monthly food money on an EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. Apply online at DTAConnect.com, which takes about 20 minutes, or by phone through the DTA Assistance Line at 1-877-382-2363, press 7, open Monday through Friday, 8:15am to 4:45pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/massachusetts-tafdc-cash-assistance/">Massachusetts TAFDC (Cash Assistance)</a><p>Transitional Aid to Families with Dependent Children, TAFDC, is Massachusetts&#39;s monthly cash assistance for families with children and pregnant people in the last 120 days of pregnancy who have little or no income. It is run by the Department of Transitional Assistance. Apply online at DTAConnect.com or by phone at 1-877-382-2363. Approval also brings MassHealth automatically.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/massachusetts-wic-nutrition-program/">Massachusetts WIC Nutrition Program</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. Massachusetts WIC runs through local clinics. Begin online at mass.gov, or call 1-800-942-1007, Monday through Friday, 9am to 5pm, to reach the WIC clinic nearest you and set up an appointment.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/raft-residential-assistance-for-families-in-transition/">RAFT (Residential Assistance for Families in Transition)</a><p>RAFT is Massachusetts&#39;s active emergency housing assistance: up to $7,000 per household in a 12-month period to help with overdue rent, mortgage payments, utility bills, and moving costs when you face eviction, foreclosure, or loss of utilities. Apply online at ApplyHousingHelp.Mass.Gov, where your application is handled by a Regional Administering Agency. Both a tenant and a landlord application are needed for rental help.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-massachusetts-dua/">Unemployment Insurance (Massachusetts DUA)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Department of Unemployment Assistance (DUA). File online through UI Online at mass.gov/dua, available daily from 6am to 10pm, or by phone through the TeleClaim Center at 1-877-626-6800, Monday through Thursday, 8:30am to 4:30pm. The phone line is closed Fridays so staff can process claims.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-massachusetts/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Massachusetts)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible Massachusetts households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system improvements that lower future bills. Your Fuel Assistance application also serves as your weatherization application, so households eligible for Home Energy Assistance are eligible for weatherization. Contact your local agency, or call the Cold Relief Heatline at 1-800-632-8175.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="michigan family independence program (michigan cash assistance) the family independence program, fip, is michigan&#39;s tanf cash assistance for low-income families with children, paid monthly on the bridge card. adults have a 60-month lifetime limit, and applicants take part in the path work program. apply online through mi bridges at newmibridges.michigan.gov or at your local mdhhs office. food assistance program (michigan snap) the food assistance program, fap, is michigan&#39;s name for snap: monthly food money on a bridge card you use like a debit card at the store. apply online through mi bridges at newmibridges.michigan.gov, or with a paper application at your local mdhhs office. michigan 211 dial 2-1-1 from any michigan phone, or call 844-875-9211, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. michigan 211 runs through seven regional call centers, covers the whole state, and answers around the clock. you can also text your zip code to 898211 or search at mi211.org. michigan earned income tax credit michigan&#39;s earned income tax credit for working families is worth 30 percent of your federal eitc and is refundable, so it can put money in your pocket even if you owe no state tax. public act 4 of 2023 raised it from 6 percent to 30 percent. claim it by filing a michigan mi-1040 return after you qualify for the federal eitc. michigan home heating credit the home heating credit is a refundable michigan tax credit that helps low-income residents pay winter heating costs. you can claim it even if you are not required to file a state income tax return, and you do not have to owe tax to get it. file form mi-1040cr-7 by september 30. michigan legal help michigan legal help is a free website with plain-language legal information and do-it-yourself tools that build court forms for people without a lawyer. it covers eviction, family law, debt, and public benefits, and points you to legal aid and self-help centers across the state. the tools and information are free to everyone. michigan medicaid &amp; healthy michigan plan michigan medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. the healthy michigan plan covers adults 19 to 64 who meet the income rules. apply online through mi bridges at newmibridges.michigan.gov, by phone at 1-855-789-5610, or at your local mdhhs office. michigan wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum women, infants, and children up to age 5. michigan wic is offered through county health departments and local agencies. call 800-942-1636 to find your local clinic, or find one at signupwic.com. state emergency relief (michigan ser) state emergency relief helps michigan residents through a crisis that threatens health or safety: heat and electricity shut-offs, plus rent, past-due rent, security deposits, and moving costs. energy help has no copay for eligible households. apply online through mi bridges at newmibridges.michigan.gov or with form dhs-1514 at your local mdhhs office. unemployment benefits (michigan) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the michigan unemployment insurance agency. file online through miwam at michigan.gov/uia, which is the fastest way, or by phone at 1-866-500-0017. the customer service line is open monday through friday, 8am to 4:30pm. weatherization assistance program (michigan) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible michigan households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system repairs that lower future bills. mdhhs runs the program through local community action agencies. contact the community action agency serving your area to apply."><h2>Michigan programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/family-independence-program-michigan-cash-assistance/">Family Independence Program (Michigan Cash Assistance)</a><p>The Family Independence Program, FIP, is Michigan&#39;s TANF cash assistance for low-income families with children, paid monthly on the Bridge Card. Adults have a 60-month lifetime limit, and applicants take part in the PATH work program. Apply online through MI Bridges at newmibridges.michigan.gov or at your local MDHHS office.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/food-assistance-program-michigan-snap/">Food Assistance Program (Michigan SNAP)</a><p>The Food Assistance Program, FAP, is Michigan&#39;s name for SNAP: monthly food money on a Bridge Card you use like a debit card at the store. Apply online through MI Bridges at newmibridges.michigan.gov, or with a paper application at your local MDHHS office.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/michigan-211/">Michigan 211</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Michigan phone, or call 844-875-9211, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. Michigan 211 runs through seven regional call centers, covers the whole state, and answers around the clock. You can also text your zip code to 898211 or search at mi211.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/michigan-earned-income-tax-credit/">Michigan Earned Income Tax Credit</a><p>Michigan&#39;s Earned Income Tax Credit for Working Families is worth 30 percent of your federal EITC and is refundable, so it can put money in your pocket even if you owe no state tax. Public Act 4 of 2023 raised it from 6 percent to 30 percent. Claim it by filing a Michigan MI-1040 return after you qualify for the federal EITC.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/michigan-home-heating-credit/">Michigan Home Heating Credit</a><p>The Home Heating Credit is a refundable Michigan tax credit that helps low-income residents pay winter heating costs. You can claim it even if you are not required to file a state income tax return, and you do not have to owe tax to get it. File form MI-1040CR-7 by September 30.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/michigan-legal-help/">Michigan Legal Help</a><p>Michigan Legal Help is a free website with plain-language legal information and do-it-yourself tools that build court forms for people without a lawyer. It covers eviction, family law, debt, and public benefits, and points you to legal aid and self-help centers across the state. The tools and information are free to everyone.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/michigan-medicaid-and-healthy-michigan-plan/">Michigan Medicaid &amp; Healthy Michigan Plan</a><p>Michigan Medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. The Healthy Michigan Plan covers adults 19 to 64 who meet the income rules. Apply online through MI Bridges at newmibridges.michigan.gov, by phone at 1-855-789-5610, or at your local MDHHS office.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/michigan-wic/">Michigan WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum women, infants, and children up to age 5. Michigan WIC is offered through county health departments and local agencies. Call 800-942-1636 to find your local clinic, or find one at Signupwic.com.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/state-emergency-relief-michigan-ser/">State Emergency Relief (Michigan SER)</a><p>State Emergency Relief helps Michigan residents through a crisis that threatens health or safety: heat and electricity shut-offs, plus rent, past-due rent, security deposits, and moving costs. Energy help has no copay for eligible households. Apply online through MI Bridges at newmibridges.michigan.gov or with form DHS-1514 at your local MDHHS office.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-benefits-michigan/">Unemployment Benefits (Michigan)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Michigan Unemployment Insurance Agency. File online through MiWAM at michigan.gov/uia, which is the fastest way, or by phone at 1-866-500-0017. The customer service line is open Monday through Friday, 8am to 4:30pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-michigan/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Michigan)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible Michigan households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system repairs that lower future bills. MDHHS runs the program through local Community Action Agencies. Contact the Community Action Agency serving your area to apply.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="minnesota medical assistance (medicaid) &amp; minnesotacare medical assistance is minnesota&#39;s medicaid program, free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, adults, older adults, and people who are blind or have a disability. minnesotacare covers many people whose income is a little too high for medical assistance, for a low monthly premium. apply through mnsure at mnsure.org, or reach dhs health care consumer support at 1-800-657-3672. minnesota 211 (united way) dial 2-1-1 from any minnesota phone, or call 1-800-543-7709, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utility bills, food, healthcare, and more. minnesota 211, run by united way, answers around the clock, every day, in many languages. you can also text your zip code to 898-211 or search at 211unitedway.org. minnesota child tax credit minnesota&#39;s child tax credit is a refundable credit of $1,750 for each qualifying child under age 18, with no limit on the number of children. because it is refundable, it can come back to you as cash even if you owe no tax. the credit begins to phase out once income passes $31,950, or $37,910 for a married couple filing jointly. claim it on schedule m1cwfc with your minnesota return. minnesota energy assistance program (eap) the energy assistance program helps income-qualified minnesota households pay home heating and electric bills and covers emergencies like a broken furnace or a shutoff. both homeowners and renters can apply. the program runs each year from october 1 through may 31 through the local service provider for your county. to find your provider, call the commerce energy information center at 1-800-657-3710. minnesota family investment program (mfip) mfip is minnesota&#39;s cash and food assistance for low-income families with children, and it is the state&#39;s version of tanf. it combines a monthly cash grant with food help and pairs most parents with mfip employment services to move toward work. apply online at mnbenefits.mn.gov or with a paper combined application form (dhs-5223) at your county or tribal human services office. minnesota legal aid (mid-minnesota legal aid / lawhelpmn) minnesota&#39;s legal aid programs provide free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. mid-minnesota legal aid also helps people age 60 and older and people with disabilities. call 1-877-696-6529 to reach intake, and find free legal information and your local office at lawhelpmn.org. minnesota snap (food support) snap, still called food support by many minnesota counties, puts monthly food money on an ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores and many farmers markets. apply online at mnbenefits.mn.gov, the state&#39;s one application that also covers cash and other help, or file a paper combined application form (dhs-5223) at your county or tribal human services office. a snap outreach specialist through mnfoodhelper (mn.gov/dhs/mnfoodhelper) can help you apply for free. minnesota wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. minnesota wic runs through local agencies of the minnesota department of health. apply online at health.state.mn.us/wicapply, or call 1-800-942-4030 to find the wic clinic nearest you. minnesota working family credit the working family credit is minnesota&#39;s version of the earned income tax credit, a refundable credit for workers with low income. it equals 4 percent of your earned income, up to a maximum of $379, and it can pay out even if you owe no tax. you claim it on schedule m1cwfc with your minnesota return, the same form that carries the state child tax credit. unemployment insurance (minnesota) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from minnesota&#39;s unemployment insurance program at deed. apply online at uimn.org, which is the fastest way, or by phone at 1-877-898-9090 for greater minnesota or 651-296-3644 in the twin cities, monday through friday, 8am to 4:30pm. phone service is offered in english, spanish, hmong, and somali. weatherization assistance program (minnesota) free home energy-efficiency work for income-qualified minnesota households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system repairs that permanently lower your energy bills. both homeowners and renters can qualify. the department of commerce runs the program through local service providers, and it shares one joint application with energy assistance. call the commerce energy information center at 1-800-657-3710 to reach the provider serving your area."><h2>Minnesota programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/medical-assistance-medicaid-and-minnesotacare/">Medical Assistance (Medicaid) &amp; MinnesotaCare</a><p>Medical Assistance is Minnesota&#39;s Medicaid program, free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, adults, older adults, and people who are blind or have a disability. MinnesotaCare covers many people whose income is a little too high for Medical Assistance, for a low monthly premium. Apply through MNsure at mnsure.org, or reach DHS Health Care Consumer Support at 1-800-657-3672.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/minnesota-211-united-way/">Minnesota 211 (United Way)</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Minnesota phone, or call 1-800-543-7709, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utility bills, food, healthcare, and more. Minnesota 211, run by United Way, answers around the clock, every day, in many languages. You can also text your zip code to 898-211 or search at 211unitedway.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/minnesota-child-tax-credit/">Minnesota Child Tax Credit</a><p>Minnesota&#39;s Child Tax Credit is a refundable credit of $1,750 for each qualifying child under age 18, with no limit on the number of children. Because it is refundable, it can come back to you as cash even if you owe no tax. The credit begins to phase out once income passes $31,950, or $37,910 for a married couple filing jointly. Claim it on Schedule M1CWFC with your Minnesota return.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/minnesota-energy-assistance-program-eap/">Minnesota Energy Assistance Program (EAP)</a><p>The Energy Assistance Program helps income-qualified Minnesota households pay home heating and electric bills and covers emergencies like a broken furnace or a shutoff. Both homeowners and renters can apply. The program runs each year from October 1 through May 31 through the local service provider for your county. To find your provider, call the Commerce Energy Information Center at 1-800-657-3710.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/minnesota-family-investment-program-mfip/">Minnesota Family Investment Program (MFIP)</a><p>MFIP is Minnesota&#39;s cash and food assistance for low-income families with children, and it is the state&#39;s version of TANF. It combines a monthly cash grant with food help and pairs most parents with MFIP Employment Services to move toward work. Apply online at mnbenefits.mn.gov or with a paper Combined Application Form (DHS-5223) at your county or Tribal human services office.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/minnesota-legal-aid-mid-minnesota-legal-aid-lawhelpmn/">Minnesota Legal Aid (Mid-Minnesota Legal Aid / LawHelpMN)</a><p>Minnesota&#39;s legal aid programs provide free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. Mid-Minnesota Legal Aid also helps people age 60 and older and people with disabilities. Call 1-877-696-6529 to reach intake, and find free legal information and your local office at lawhelpmn.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/minnesota-snap-food-support/">Minnesota SNAP (Food Support)</a><p>SNAP, still called Food Support by many Minnesota counties, puts monthly food money on an EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores and many farmers markets. Apply online at mnbenefits.mn.gov, the state&#39;s one application that also covers cash and other help, or file a paper Combined Application Form (DHS-5223) at your county or Tribal human services office. A SNAP outreach specialist through MNFoodHelper (mn.gov/dhs/mnfoodhelper) can help you apply for free.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/minnesota-wic/">Minnesota WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. Minnesota WIC runs through local agencies of the Minnesota Department of Health. Apply online at health.state.mn.us/wicapply, or call 1-800-942-4030 to find the WIC clinic nearest you.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/minnesota-working-family-credit/">Minnesota Working Family Credit</a><p>The Working Family Credit is Minnesota&#39;s version of the Earned Income Tax Credit, a refundable credit for workers with low income. It equals 4 percent of your earned income, up to a maximum of $379, and it can pay out even if you owe no tax. You claim it on Schedule M1CWFC with your Minnesota return, the same form that carries the state Child Tax Credit.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-minnesota/">Unemployment Insurance (Minnesota)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from Minnesota&#39;s Unemployment Insurance program at DEED. Apply online at uimn.org, which is the fastest way, or by phone at 1-877-898-9090 for Greater Minnesota or 651-296-3644 in the Twin Cities, Monday through Friday, 8am to 4:30pm. Phone service is offered in English, Spanish, Hmong, and Somali.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-minnesota/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Minnesota)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-qualified Minnesota households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system repairs that permanently lower your energy bills. Both homeowners and renters can qualify. The Department of Commerce runs the program through local service providers, and it shares one joint application with Energy Assistance. Call the Commerce Energy Information Center at 1-800-657-3710 to reach the provider serving your area.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="mississippi mississippi 211 dial 2-1-1 for free, confidential help finding local mississippi assistance with housing, utilities, food, and healthcare. if 2-1-1 does not connect from your phone, search your zip code at 211.org to reach the resource center that covers your area. it is available around the clock. mississippi center for legal services (south mississippi legal aid) free civil legal help for low-income residents of southern mississippi on housing, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. call the intake hotline at 1-800-519-2915. you can also find free legal information and your local office at mslegalservices.org. mississippi child care payment program (ccpp) ccpp helps eligible working parents pay for child care through a certificate to a licensed provider, and families pay a monthly co-payment set by family size and income. mdhs fills open slots from a waitlist, so add your email to the waitlist to get a personalized application link. questions go to 1-800-877-7882. mississippi liheap (home energy assistance) liheap helps low-income mississippi households pay heating and cooling bills and handle an energy crisis. apply online through the access ms portal at access.ms.gov and pick community services; your local community action agency then processes the case and schedules an appointment. it runs in all 82 counties as funds last. call the mdhs community services division at 1-800-421-0762. mississippi medicaid &amp; chip mississippi medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents and caretakers of minor children, older adults, and people with disabilities, and chip covers children in families that earn a bit more. mississippi has not expanded medicaid, so most low-income adults without children or a disability do not qualify. check eligibility and apply at medicaid.ms.gov, or call 1-800-421-2408. mississippi snap (food assistance) snap is mississippi&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. apply online through the access ms common web portal at access.ms.gov, or at your local mdhs county office. questions about a snap or tanf case go to the mdhs eae client services line at 1-800-948-3050. mississippi tanf (cash assistance) temporary assistance for needy families, tanf, is mississippi&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children. most adults take part in the tanf work program, which helps you build job skills and find work. apply online through the access ms portal at access.ms.gov or at your local mdhs county office. a 60-month lifetime limit applies. mississippi wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant, breastfeeding, and postpartum women, infants, and children under age 5. mississippi wic runs through county health department clinics of the mississippi state department of health. call 1-800-545-6747, or 601-991-6000 in the jackson area, to find your local clinic and start. north mississippi rural legal services (north mississippi legal aid) free civil legal help for low-income residents of the northern 39 counties of mississippi. north mississippi rural legal services handles housing, elder law, domestic violence, foreclosure, and other noncriminal matters. call the intake line at 1-800-898-8731, monday through thursday, 9:30am to 3:30pm. unemployment insurance (mississippi mdes) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the mississippi department of employment security. file online through the reemployms portal at reemployms.mdes.ms.gov, which is fastest, or call the claimants line at 601-493-9427, monday through friday, 8am to 5pm. weatherization assistance program (mississippi) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible mississippi households: insulation, air sealing, and furnace repair or replacement that lower future bills. apply through the access ms portal at access.ms.gov, pick community services, and your local community action agency handles the rest. homeowners and renters both qualify. call mdhs community services at 1-800-421-0762."><h2>Mississippi programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/mississippi-211/">Mississippi 211</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 for free, confidential help finding local Mississippi assistance with housing, utilities, food, and healthcare. If 2-1-1 does not connect from your phone, search your zip code at 211.org to reach the resource center that covers your area. It is available around the clock.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/mississippi-center-for-legal-services-south-mississippi-legal-aid/">Mississippi Center for Legal Services (South Mississippi Legal Aid)</a><p>Free civil legal help for low-income residents of southern Mississippi on housing, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. Call the intake hotline at 1-800-519-2915. You can also find free legal information and your local office at mslegalservices.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/mississippi-child-care-payment-program-ccpp/">Mississippi Child Care Payment Program (CCPP)</a><p>CCPP helps eligible working parents pay for child care through a certificate to a licensed provider, and families pay a monthly co-payment set by family size and income. MDHS fills open slots from a waitlist, so add your email to the waitlist to get a personalized application link. Questions go to 1-800-877-7882.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/mississippi-liheap-home-energy-assistance/">Mississippi LIHEAP (Home Energy Assistance)</a><p>LIHEAP helps low-income Mississippi households pay heating and cooling bills and handle an energy crisis. Apply online through the Access MS portal at access.ms.gov and pick Community Services; your local Community Action Agency then processes the case and schedules an appointment. It runs in all 82 counties as funds last. Call the MDHS Community Services Division at 1-800-421-0762.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/mississippi-medicaid-and-chip/">Mississippi Medicaid &amp; CHIP</a><p>Mississippi Medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents and caretakers of minor children, older adults, and people with disabilities, and CHIP covers children in families that earn a bit more. Mississippi has not expanded Medicaid, so most low-income adults without children or a disability do not qualify. Check eligibility and apply at medicaid.ms.gov, or call 1-800-421-2408.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/mississippi-snap-food-assistance/">Mississippi SNAP (Food Assistance)</a><p>SNAP is Mississippi&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. Apply online through the Access MS Common Web Portal at access.ms.gov, or at your local MDHS county office. Questions about a SNAP or TANF case go to the MDHS EAE Client Services line at 1-800-948-3050.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/mississippi-tanf-cash-assistance/">Mississippi TANF (Cash Assistance)</a><p>Temporary Assistance for Needy Families, TANF, is Mississippi&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children. Most adults take part in the TANF Work Program, which helps you build job skills and find work. Apply online through the Access MS portal at access.ms.gov or at your local MDHS county office. A 60-month lifetime limit applies.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/mississippi-wic/">Mississippi WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant, breastfeeding, and postpartum women, infants, and children under age 5. Mississippi WIC runs through county health department clinics of the Mississippi State Department of Health. Call 1-800-545-6747, or 601-991-6000 in the Jackson area, to find your local clinic and start.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/north-mississippi-rural-legal-services-north-mississippi-legal-aid/">North Mississippi Rural Legal Services (North Mississippi Legal Aid)</a><p>Free civil legal help for low-income residents of the northern 39 counties of Mississippi. North Mississippi Rural Legal Services handles housing, elder law, domestic violence, foreclosure, and other noncriminal matters. Call the intake line at 1-800-898-8731, Monday through Thursday, 9:30am to 3:30pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-mississippi-mdes/">Unemployment Insurance (Mississippi MDES)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Mississippi Department of Employment Security. File online through the ReEmployMS portal at reemployms.mdes.ms.gov, which is fastest, or call the Claimants Line at 601-493-9427, Monday through Friday, 8am to 5pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-mississippi/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Mississippi)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible Mississippi households: insulation, air sealing, and furnace repair or replacement that lower future bills. Apply through the Access MS portal at access.ms.gov, pick Community Services, and your local Community Action Agency handles the rest. Homeowners and renters both qualify. Call MDHS Community Services at 1-800-421-0762.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="missouri mo healthnet (missouri medicaid) mo healthnet is missouri&#39;s medicaid program: free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. missouri expanded medicaid, so adults ages 19 to 64 can now qualify. apply online at mydss.mo.gov or by phone at 1-855-373-9994. general mo healthnet questions go to 1-800-392-2161. missouri energy assistance (liheap &amp; ecip) missouri&#39;s low income home energy assistance program helps low-income households pay home energy bills. it has two parts: energy assistance, a one-time payment toward heating or cooling from october through may, and the energy crisis intervention program, ecip, for a disconnect notice or an emergency. apply at mydss.mo.gov or call 1-855-373-4636 to have an application mailed. missouri legal aid (free civil legal help) missouri&#39;s legal aid offices provide free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. in eastern missouri, call legal services of eastern missouri at 1-800-444-0514. in western missouri, call legal aid of western missouri at 1-816-474-6750. find the right office and apply at lsmo.org. missouri snap (food stamps) snap is missouri&#39;s food stamp program: monthly food money on an ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. apply online any time through mydss at mydss.mo.gov, run by the family support division, or ask for a paper application. the fsd information center is 1-855-373-4636 (1-855-fsd-info), monday through friday, 8am to 5pm. missouri temporary assistance (cash assistance) temporary assistance, ta, is missouri&#39;s monthly cash benefit for low-income families with children, meant to help cover costs like clothing and utilities. most adults take part in the missouri work assistance program, which helps you move toward a job. apply online at mydss.mo.gov or ask for a paper application through the fsd information center at 1-855-373-4636. missouri wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. missouri wic runs through local agencies of the department of health and senior services. call tel-link at 1-800-835-5465 to find your local wic agency, or reach the state wic office at 1-800-392-8209. missouri working family tax credit missouri&#39;s working family tax credit is a state earned income tax credit equal to 20 percent of your federal earned income tax credit for tax year 2024 and forward. it is nonrefundable, so it lowers the missouri tax you owe but cannot go below zero, and it cannot be carried forward to a later year. claim it on your missouri income tax return. unemployment insurance (missouri) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the missouri division of employment security. file online through uinteract at uinteract.labor.mo.gov, which is the fastest way and open 24 hours a day, or call a regional claims center at 1-800-320-2519, monday through friday, 8am to 5pm central. united way 211 missouri dial 2-1-1 from any missouri phone, or call 1-800-427-4626, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. united way 211 answers around the clock, every day of the year, with multilingual specialists. you can also search the statewide resource directory at 211helps.org. weatherization assistance program (missouri) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible missouri households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system improvements that lower future bills. the missouri department of natural resources runs the program through a statewide network of local weatherization agencies. contact the local agency serving your area to apply, since dnr does not take applications directly."><h2>Missouri programs (10)</h2><article><a href="/programs/mo-healthnet-missouri-medicaid/">MO HealthNet (Missouri Medicaid)</a><p>MO HealthNet is Missouri&#39;s Medicaid program: free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. Missouri expanded Medicaid, so adults ages 19 to 64 can now qualify. Apply online at mydss.mo.gov or by phone at 1-855-373-9994. General MO HealthNet questions go to 1-800-392-2161.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/missouri-energy-assistance-liheap-and-ecip/">Missouri Energy Assistance (LIHEAP &amp; ECIP)</a><p>Missouri&#39;s Low Income Home Energy Assistance Program helps low-income households pay home energy bills. It has two parts: Energy Assistance, a one-time payment toward heating or cooling from October through May, and the Energy Crisis Intervention Program, ECIP, for a disconnect notice or an emergency. Apply at mydss.mo.gov or call 1-855-373-4636 to have an application mailed.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/missouri-legal-aid-free-civil-legal-help/">Missouri Legal Aid (Free Civil Legal Help)</a><p>Missouri&#39;s legal aid offices provide free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. In eastern Missouri, call Legal Services of Eastern Missouri at 1-800-444-0514. In western Missouri, call Legal Aid of Western Missouri at 1-816-474-6750. Find the right office and apply at lsmo.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/missouri-snap-food-stamps/">Missouri SNAP (Food Stamps)</a><p>SNAP is Missouri&#39;s food stamp program: monthly food money on an EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. Apply online any time through myDSS at mydss.mo.gov, run by the Family Support Division, or ask for a paper application. The FSD Information Center is 1-855-373-4636 (1-855-FSD-INFO), Monday through Friday, 8am to 5pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/missouri-temporary-assistance-cash-assistance/">Missouri Temporary Assistance (Cash Assistance)</a><p>Temporary Assistance, TA, is Missouri&#39;s monthly cash benefit for low-income families with children, meant to help cover costs like clothing and utilities. Most adults take part in the Missouri Work Assistance program, which helps you move toward a job. Apply online at mydss.mo.gov or ask for a paper application through the FSD Information Center at 1-855-373-4636.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/missouri-wic/">Missouri WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. Missouri WIC runs through local agencies of the Department of Health and Senior Services. Call TEL-LINK at 1-800-835-5465 to find your local WIC agency, or reach the state WIC office at 1-800-392-8209.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/missouri-working-family-tax-credit/">Missouri Working Family Tax Credit</a><p>Missouri&#39;s Working Family Tax Credit is a state earned income tax credit equal to 20 percent of your federal Earned Income Tax Credit for tax year 2024 and forward. It is nonrefundable, so it lowers the Missouri tax you owe but cannot go below zero, and it cannot be carried forward to a later year. Claim it on your Missouri income tax return.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-missouri/">Unemployment Insurance (Missouri)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Missouri Division of Employment Security. File online through UInteract at uinteract.labor.mo.gov, which is the fastest way and open 24 hours a day, or call a Regional Claims Center at 1-800-320-2519, Monday through Friday, 8am to 5pm Central.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/united-way-211-missouri/">United Way 211 Missouri</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Missouri phone, or call 1-800-427-4626, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. United Way 211 answers around the clock, every day of the year, with multilingual specialists. You can also search the statewide resource directory at 211helps.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-missouri/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Missouri)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible Missouri households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system improvements that lower future bills. The Missouri Department of Natural Resources runs the program through a statewide network of local weatherization agencies. Contact the local agency serving your area to apply, since DNR does not take applications directly.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="montana montana 211 dial 2-1-1 from any montana phone for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. montana 211 is a statewide service run by regional united way partners, and it answers around the clock, every day. you can also search at montana211.org. montana earned income tax credit montana offers a refundable earned income tax credit equal to 10 percent of your federal eitc, so it can pay out even when it is larger than the state tax you owe. if you qualify for the federal eitc, claim the montana credit on your state income tax return using form 2. free tax-preparation sites can file both returns and claim the credit at no cost. montana elderly homeowner/renter credit the montana elderly homeowner/renter credit is a refundable credit of up to 1,150 dollars that helps older montanans with the property taxes or rent they paid on their home. you may qualify if you were 62 or older at the end of the year and had total household income under 45,000 dollars. both homeowners and renters claim it on form 2 with schedule 2ec, even if you do not otherwise have to file. montana lieap (low income energy assistance program) the low income energy assistance program, lieap, helps low-income montana households pay winter heating bills and covers furnace emergencies during the heating season. apply from october 1 through april 30 by delivering or mailing the combined lieap and weatherization application to your local eligibility office. for help, call the human and community services division at 406-444-1788. montana legal services association (montana law help) montana legal services association gives free civil legal help to low-income montanans on housing, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. call the help line at 1-800-666-6899, open tuesday through thursday, 9am to 1pm, or find free legal information, forms, and your local provider at montanalawhelp.org. montana medicaid &amp; healthy montana kids (hmk) plus montana medicaid and healthy montana kids (hmk) plus are free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. montana expanded medicaid, so adults ages 19 to 64 with low income may qualify through the help plan. apply online at apply.mt.gov or at your local office of public assistance at 1-888-706-1535. for questions about current benefits, call the member help line at 1-800-362-8312, monday through friday, 8am to 5pm. montana snap (food assistance) snap is montana&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. apply online at apply.mt.gov, which also covers medicaid and tanf cash on one application, in person at your local office of public assistance, or by phone through the public assistance helpline at 1-888-706-1535. montana tanf (cash assistance) temporary assistance for needy families, tanf, is montana&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children. most adults take part in tanf pathways, the work program run for the state by maximus, which helps you move toward a job. apply online at apply.mt.gov, in person at your local office of public assistance, or by phone at 1-888-706-1535. montana wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. to start, find a clinic and prescreen at signupwic.com, or call 1-844-583-3237. a father, grandparent, foster parent, or guardian raising a child under 5 can apply for that child. unemployment insurance (montana) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the montana department of labor and industry. file online through the claimant portal at uiclaimant.mt.gov, then request payment each week you are unemployed. for help, call the claims processing center at 406-444-2545. weatherization assistance program (montana) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible montana households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system repairs that lower future bills. both homeowners and renters can apply, at any time of year, using the combined lieap and weatherization application filed with your local eligibility office. for help, call the human and community services division at 406-444-1788."><h2>Montana programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/montana-211/">Montana 211</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Montana phone for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. Montana 211 is a statewide service run by regional United Way partners, and it answers around the clock, every day. You can also search at montana211.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/montana-earned-income-tax-credit/">Montana Earned Income Tax Credit</a><p>Montana offers a refundable Earned Income Tax Credit equal to 10 percent of your federal EITC, so it can pay out even when it is larger than the state tax you owe. If you qualify for the federal EITC, claim the Montana credit on your state income tax return using Form 2. Free tax-preparation sites can file both returns and claim the credit at no cost.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/montana-elderly-homeowner-renter-credit/">Montana Elderly Homeowner/Renter Credit</a><p>The Montana Elderly Homeowner/Renter Credit is a refundable credit of up to 1,150 dollars that helps older Montanans with the property taxes or rent they paid on their home. You may qualify if you were 62 or older at the end of the year and had total household income under 45,000 dollars. Both homeowners and renters claim it on Form 2 with Schedule 2EC, even if you do not otherwise have to file.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/montana-lieap-low-income-energy-assistance-program/">Montana LIEAP (Low Income Energy Assistance Program)</a><p>The Low Income Energy Assistance Program, LIEAP, helps low-income Montana households pay winter heating bills and covers furnace emergencies during the heating season. Apply from October 1 through April 30 by delivering or mailing the combined LIEAP and Weatherization application to your local eligibility office. For help, call the Human and Community Services Division at 406-444-1788.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/montana-legal-services-association-montana-law-help/">Montana Legal Services Association (Montana Law Help)</a><p>Montana Legal Services Association gives free civil legal help to low-income Montanans on housing, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. Call the HELP Line at 1-800-666-6899, open Tuesday through Thursday, 9am to 1pm, or find free legal information, forms, and your local provider at montanalawhelp.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/montana-medicaid-and-healthy-montana-kids-hmk-plus/">Montana Medicaid &amp; Healthy Montana Kids (HMK) Plus</a><p>Montana Medicaid and Healthy Montana Kids (HMK) Plus are free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. Montana expanded Medicaid, so adults ages 19 to 64 with low income may qualify through the HELP Plan. Apply online at apply.mt.gov or at your local Office of Public Assistance at 1-888-706-1535. For questions about current benefits, call the Member Help Line at 1-800-362-8312, Monday through Friday, 8am to 5pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/montana-snap-food-assistance/">Montana SNAP (Food Assistance)</a><p>SNAP is Montana&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. Apply online at apply.mt.gov, which also covers Medicaid and TANF cash on one application, in person at your local Office of Public Assistance, or by phone through the Public Assistance Helpline at 1-888-706-1535.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/montana-tanf-cash-assistance/">Montana TANF (Cash Assistance)</a><p>Temporary Assistance for Needy Families, TANF, is Montana&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children. Most adults take part in TANF Pathways, the work program run for the state by Maximus, which helps you move toward a job. Apply online at apply.mt.gov, in person at your local Office of Public Assistance, or by phone at 1-888-706-1535.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/montana-wic/">Montana WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. To start, find a clinic and prescreen at signupwic.com, or call 1-844-583-3237. A father, grandparent, foster parent, or guardian raising a child under 5 can apply for that child.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-montana/">Unemployment Insurance (Montana)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Montana Department of Labor and Industry. File online through the claimant portal at uiclaimant.mt.gov, then request payment each week you are unemployed. For help, call the Claims Processing Center at 406-444-2545.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-montana/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Montana)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible Montana households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system repairs that lower future bills. Both homeowners and renters can apply, at any time of year, using the combined LIEAP and Weatherization application filed with your local eligibility office. For help, call the Human and Community Services Division at 406-444-1788.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="nebraska legal aid of nebraska legal aid of nebraska provides free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing and eviction, family, public benefits, consumer, and debt matters. call the accessline at 1-877-250-2016 to apply. nebraskans 60 and older can use the elderaccessline at 1-800-527-7249, and a disaster line runs at 1-844-268-5627. find more at legalaidofnebraska.org. nebraska 211 (united way of the midlands) dial 2-1-1 from any nebraska phone, or call 1-866-813-1731, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, and more. nebraska 211 is run by united way of the midlands. you can also text your zip code to 898211, or chat and search at ne211.org, with live chat open 7am to 10pm every day. nebraska adc (aid to dependent children / tanf cash) aid to dependent children, adc, is nebraska&#39;s tanf cash assistance for low-income families with children. most working-age adults take part in employment first, which helps you move toward a job through training and work preparation. apply online through iserve nebraska at iserve.nebraska.gov or at your local dhhs office. nebraska child care subsidy the child care subsidy helps low-income families pay for child care while parents work, go to school, or train for a job. apply online through iserve nebraska at iserve.nebraska.gov. for questions about your eligibility, call the child care subsidy program at 402-471-9152. nebraska earned income tax credit nebraska has a refundable earned income tax credit worth 10 percent of your federal eitc. because it is refundable, it can come back to you as cash even when you owe no state income tax. claim it on your nebraska individual income tax return, form 1040n, after you claim the federal credit. nebraska energy assistance (liheap) the low income home energy assistance program, liheap, helps low-income nebraska households with home energy costs. heating help runs october 1 through march 31, cooling help runs june 1 through august 31, and crisis help is available 