ntact the provider serving your area to apply. call 1-877-253-2603 or 402-471-2186 for help finding yours."><h2>Nebraska programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/legal-aid-of-nebraska/">Legal Aid of Nebraska</a><p>Legal Aid of Nebraska provides free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing and eviction, family, public benefits, consumer, and debt matters. Call the AccessLine at 1-877-250-2016 to apply. Nebraskans 60 and older can use the ElderAccessLine at 1-800-527-7249, and a disaster line runs at 1-844-268-5627. Find more at legalaidofnebraska.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/nebraska-211-united-way-of-the-midlands/">Nebraska 211 (United Way of the Midlands)</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Nebraska phone, or call 1-866-813-1731, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, and more. Nebraska 211 is run by United Way of the Midlands. You can also text your zip code to 898211, or chat and search at ne211.org, with live chat open 7am to 10pm every day.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/nebraska-adc-aid-to-dependent-children-tanf-cash/">Nebraska ADC (Aid to Dependent Children / TANF Cash)</a><p>Aid to Dependent Children, ADC, is Nebraska&#39;s TANF cash assistance for low-income families with children. Most working-age adults take part in Employment First, which helps you move toward a job through training and work preparation. Apply online through iServe Nebraska at iserve.nebraska.gov or at your local DHHS office.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/nebraska-child-care-subsidy/">Nebraska Child Care Subsidy</a><p>The Child Care Subsidy helps low-income families pay for child care while parents work, go to school, or train for a job. Apply online through iServe Nebraska at iserve.nebraska.gov. For questions about your eligibility, call the Child Care Subsidy Program at 402-471-9152.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/nebraska-earned-income-tax-credit/">Nebraska Earned Income Tax Credit</a><p>Nebraska has a refundable Earned Income Tax Credit worth 10 percent of your federal EITC. Because it is refundable, it can come back to you as cash even when you owe no state income tax. Claim it on your Nebraska individual income tax return, Form 1040N, after you claim the federal credit.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/nebraska-energy-assistance-liheap/">Nebraska Energy Assistance (LIHEAP)</a><p>The Low Income Home Energy Assistance Program, LIHEAP, helps low-income Nebraska households with home energy costs. Heating help runs October 1 through March 31, cooling help runs June 1 through August 31, and crisis help is available year-round for shutoffs, reconnects, and emergencies. Apply online through iServe Nebraska, or call ACCESSNebraska at 1-800-383-4278.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/nebraska-medicaid-heritage-health/">Nebraska Medicaid (Heritage Health)</a><p>Nebraska Medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. Most members get care through Heritage Health managed care plans. Nebraska expanded Medicaid, and the Heritage Health Adult program now covers low-income adults 19 to 64. Apply online through iServe Nebraska at iserve.nebraska.gov.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/nebraska-snap-food-assistance/">Nebraska SNAP (Food Assistance)</a><p>SNAP is Nebraska&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. Apply online through iServe Nebraska at iserve.nebraska.gov, the state&#39;s one application for SNAP, ADC cash, Medicaid, energy help, and child care. You can also apply at your local DHHS office or call DHHS at 402-471-3121.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/nebraska-wic/">Nebraska WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food at no cost, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. Nebraska WIC runs through local clinics across the state. Find your nearest clinic on the DHHS WIC locator, then call it to book an in-person appointment.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-nebraska/">Unemployment Insurance (Nebraska)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Nebraska Department of Labor. File your claim online through NEworks at neworks.nebraska.gov, which is how Nebraska takes unemployment claims. Set up your NEworks account, file your initial claim, then file a weekly claim each week to keep payments coming.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-nebraska/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Nebraska)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible Nebraska households: insulation, air sealing, heating-system safety checks, and repairs that lower future bills. The Nebraska Department of Water, Energy, and Environment runs the program through eight nonprofit community action agencies. Contact the provider serving your area to apply. Call 1-877-253-2603 or 402-471-2186 for help finding yours.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="nevada nevada 211 dial 2-1-1 from any nevada phone, or call 1-866-535-5654, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. the call center answers monday through friday, 9am to 9pm pacific time, and the online search and app work around the clock. you can also text your zip code to 898211. nevada child care subsidy (child care and development program) nevada&#39;s child care and development program helps low-income working families pay for child care so a parent can work or go to school. the division of welfare and supportive services runs it, and you apply through access nevada at accessnevada.nv.gov. for questions about the program, call 775-684-0625. nevada energy assistance program (liheap) nevada&#39;s energy assistance program, the state&#39;s liheap, helps low-income households pay heating and cooling bills. it runs year-round rather than in seasonal windows, but once you get a benefit you generally cannot reapply until about 11 months later. apply online through access nevada at accessnevada.nv.gov, or submit a paper application by mail, fax, email, or at a welfare office drop box. call 775-684-0800 with questions. nevada legal aid (nevada legal services &amp; legal aid center of southern nevada) nevada&#39;s legal aid offices provide free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing, eviction, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. nevada legal services serves the whole state from offices in las vegas, reno, carson city, and elko; call its toll-free line at 1-866-432-0404. in clark county, the legal aid center of southern nevada also helps at 702-386-1070. nevada medicaid &amp; nevada check up nevada medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. nevada check up covers children through chip. nevada expanded medicaid, so low-income adults 19 to 64 can qualify too. apply online through access nevada at accessnevada.nv.gov, which has a pre-screening tool, or at a local welfare office. call 775-684-0800 with questions. nevada snap (food assistance) snap is nevada&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. the fastest way in is access nevada at accessnevada.nv.gov, the state&#39;s online portal that also handles medicaid, cash assistance, and energy help. you can also apply at a local division of welfare and supportive services office or by phone at 775-684-0800. households with almost no money can get benefits within 7 business days. nevada tanf (cash assistance) temporary assistance for needy families, tanf, is nevada&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children. it comes with work requirements that help you move toward a job, and it carries a 60-month lifetime limit on how long an adult can receive benefits. apply online through access nevada at accessnevada.nv.gov or at a local division of welfare and supportive services office. call 775-684-0800 with questions. nevada wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. nevada wic runs clinics in urban and rural communities statewide. find your nearest clinic and apply at nevadawic.org, or call 1-800-863-8942 for help getting started. unemployment insurance (nevada detr) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the nevada department of employment, training and rehabilitation, detr. file online at ui.nv.gov, which is the fastest way and open around the clock. you file an initial claim, then a claim for each week to keep payments coming, and you must stay able, available, and looking for work while you claim. weatherization assistance program (nevada) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible nevada households: air sealing, insulation, and heating and cooling improvements that lower future bills. the nevada housing division runs the program through local nonprofit agencies. both homeowners and renters qualify, though the owner of a rental may have to cover part of the cost. contact the agency serving your county to apply."><h2>Nevada programs (10)</h2><article><a href="/programs/nevada-211/">Nevada 211</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Nevada phone, or call 1-866-535-5654, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. The call center answers Monday through Friday, 9am to 9pm Pacific time, and the online search and app work around the clock. You can also text your zip code to 898211.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/nevada-child-care-subsidy-child-care-and-development-program/">Nevada Child Care Subsidy (Child Care and Development Program)</a><p>Nevada&#39;s Child Care and Development Program helps low-income working families pay for child care so a parent can work or go to school. The Division of Welfare and Supportive Services runs it, and you apply through Access Nevada at accessnevada.nv.gov. For questions about the program, call 775-684-0625.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/nevada-energy-assistance-program-liheap/">Nevada Energy Assistance Program (LIHEAP)</a><p>Nevada&#39;s Energy Assistance Program, the state&#39;s LIHEAP, helps low-income households pay heating and cooling bills. It runs year-round rather than in seasonal windows, but once you get a benefit you generally cannot reapply until about 11 months later. Apply online through Access Nevada at accessnevada.nv.gov, or submit a paper application by mail, fax, email, or at a welfare office drop box. Call 775-684-0800 with questions.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/nevada-legal-aid-nevada-legal-services-and-legal-aid-center-of-southern-nevada/">Nevada Legal Aid (Nevada Legal Services &amp; Legal Aid Center of Southern Nevada)</a><p>Nevada&#39;s legal aid offices provide free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing, eviction, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. Nevada Legal Services serves the whole state from offices in Las Vegas, Reno, Carson City, and Elko; call its toll-free line at 1-866-432-0404. In Clark County, the Legal Aid Center of Southern Nevada also helps at 702-386-1070.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/nevada-medicaid-and-nevada-check-up/">Nevada Medicaid &amp; Nevada Check Up</a><p>Nevada Medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. Nevada Check Up covers children through CHIP. Nevada expanded Medicaid, so low-income adults 19 to 64 can qualify too. Apply online through Access Nevada at accessnevada.nv.gov, which has a pre-screening tool, or at a local welfare office. Call 775-684-0800 with questions.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/nevada-snap-food-assistance/">Nevada SNAP (Food Assistance)</a><p>SNAP is Nevada&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. The fastest way in is Access Nevada at accessnevada.nv.gov, the state&#39;s online portal that also handles Medicaid, cash assistance, and energy help. You can also apply at a local Division of Welfare and Supportive Services office or by phone at 775-684-0800. Households with almost no money can get benefits within 7 business days.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/nevada-tanf-cash-assistance/">Nevada TANF (Cash Assistance)</a><p>Temporary Assistance for Needy Families, TANF, is Nevada&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children. It comes with work requirements that help you move toward a job, and it carries a 60-month lifetime limit on how long an adult can receive benefits. Apply online through Access Nevada at accessnevada.nv.gov or at a local Division of Welfare and Supportive Services office. Call 775-684-0800 with questions.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/nevada-wic/">Nevada WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. Nevada WIC runs clinics in urban and rural communities statewide. Find your nearest clinic and apply at nevadawic.org, or call 1-800-863-8942 for help getting started.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-nevada-detr/">Unemployment Insurance (Nevada DETR)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Nevada Department of Employment, Training and Rehabilitation, DETR. File online at ui.nv.gov, which is the fastest way and open around the clock. You file an initial claim, then a claim for each week to keep payments coming, and you must stay able, available, and looking for work while you claim.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-nevada/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Nevada)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible Nevada households: air sealing, insulation, and heating and cooling improvements that lower future bills. The Nevada Housing Division runs the program through local nonprofit agencies. Both homeowners and renters qualify, though the owner of a rental may have to cover part of the cost. Contact the agency serving your county to apply.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="new hampshire 211 new hampshire dial 2-1-1 from any new hampshire phone, or call 1-866-444-4211, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, health care, and more. 211 nh is answered by trained specialists 24 hours a day, 365 days a year. you can also reach them by text or email through 211nh.org. 603 legal aid (free civil legal help in new hampshire) 603 legal aid runs new hampshire&#39;s statewide intake for free civil legal help on noncriminal problems: eviction and housing, family, public benefits, consumer, and debt matters for low-income residents. call the intake line at 1-800-639-5290 (or 603-224-3333), open 9am to 12:30pm, monday through wednesday. it works alongside new hampshire legal assistance to serve the whole state. new hampshire emergency assistance (housing &amp; utility crisis) emergency assistance, ea, helps new hampshire families with children facing a housing or utility crisis: homelessness, a utility shutoff, or no heat, hot water, or cooking fuel. it can pay a rental security deposit or first month&#39;s rent, back rent, utility deposits or arrears, or a home heating fuel delivery. families must meet the fanf eligibility rules but do not have to receive the cash benefit. apply through your local dhhs district office, or call 1-844-275-3447. new hampshire fanf / tanf (cash assistance) financial assistance to needy families, fanf, is new hampshire&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with dependent children, also known as tanf. most adults take part in the new hampshire employment program, nhep, which helps you move toward a job. apply online through nh easy at nheasy.nh.gov or at your local dhhs district office. new hampshire fuel assistance program (liheap) the fuel assistance program helps income-eligible new hampshire households pay winter heating costs. the new hampshire department of energy runs it through the state&#39;s community action agencies, which take the applications and pay your heating vendor directly. contact the community action agency serving your area to apply, or dial 2-1-1 to find it. new hampshire medicaid &amp; granite advantage new hampshire medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, seniors, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. the granite advantage health care program is the part that covers low-income adults age 19 through 64. apply online through nh easy at nheasy.nh.gov, or call the dhhs customer service center at 1-844-275-3447, monday through friday, 8am to 4pm. new hampshire snap (food assistance) snap is new hampshire&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. apply online through nh easy at nheasy.nh.gov, the state&#39;s system that also covers cash assistance, medicaid, and child care on one application, or at your local dhhs district office. you can also call the dhhs customer service center at 1-844-275-3447 to have an application mailed to you. new hampshire wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. new hampshire wic runs through local clinics statewide. to apply, find your nearest clinic at signupwic.com, or call the state wic agency at 1-800-942-4321. unemployment insurance (new hampshire) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from new hampshire employment security. file online at nhes.nh.gov, which is the fastest way. if you need help, the nhes claims line is 603-271-7700, open monday through friday, 8am to 4:30pm. weatherization assistance program (new hampshire) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible new hampshire households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system improvements that lower future bills. the new hampshire department of energy runs it through the state&#39;s community action agencies, and both homeowners and renters can qualify. contact the community action agency serving your area to apply, since the state does not take applications directly."><h2>New Hampshire programs (10)</h2><article><a href="/programs/211-new-hampshire/">211 New Hampshire</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any New Hampshire phone, or call 1-866-444-4211, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, health care, and more. 211 NH is answered by trained specialists 24 hours a day, 365 days a year. You can also reach them by text or email through 211nh.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/603-legal-aid-free-civil-legal-help-in-new-hampshire/">603 Legal Aid (Free Civil Legal Help in New Hampshire)</a><p>603 Legal Aid runs New Hampshire&#39;s statewide intake for free civil legal help on noncriminal problems: eviction and housing, family, public benefits, consumer, and debt matters for low-income residents. Call the intake line at 1-800-639-5290 (or 603-224-3333), open 9am to 12:30pm, Monday through Wednesday. It works alongside New Hampshire Legal Assistance to serve the whole state.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/new-hampshire-emergency-assistance-housing-and-utility-crisis/">New Hampshire Emergency Assistance (Housing &amp; Utility Crisis)</a><p>Emergency Assistance, EA, helps New Hampshire families with children facing a housing or utility crisis: homelessness, a utility shutoff, or no heat, hot water, or cooking fuel. It can pay a rental security deposit or first month&#39;s rent, back rent, utility deposits or arrears, or a home heating fuel delivery. Families must meet the FANF eligibility rules but do not have to receive the cash benefit. Apply through your local DHHS District Office, or call 1-844-275-3447.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/new-hampshire-fanf-tanf-cash-assistance/">New Hampshire FANF / TANF (Cash Assistance)</a><p>Financial Assistance to Needy Families, FANF, is New Hampshire&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with dependent children, also known as TANF. Most adults take part in the New Hampshire Employment Program, NHEP, which helps you move toward a job. Apply online through NH EASY at nheasy.nh.gov or at your local DHHS District Office.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/new-hampshire-fuel-assistance-program-liheap/">New Hampshire Fuel Assistance Program (LIHEAP)</a><p>The Fuel Assistance Program helps income-eligible New Hampshire households pay winter heating costs. The New Hampshire Department of Energy runs it through the state&#39;s Community Action Agencies, which take the applications and pay your heating vendor directly. Contact the Community Action Agency serving your area to apply, or dial 2-1-1 to find it.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/new-hampshire-medicaid-and-granite-advantage/">New Hampshire Medicaid &amp; Granite Advantage</a><p>New Hampshire Medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, seniors, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. The Granite Advantage Health Care Program is the part that covers low-income adults age 19 through 64. Apply online through NH EASY at nheasy.nh.gov, or call the DHHS Customer Service Center at 1-844-275-3447, Monday through Friday, 8am to 4pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/new-hampshire-snap-food-assistance/">New Hampshire SNAP (Food Assistance)</a><p>SNAP is New Hampshire&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. Apply online through NH EASY at nheasy.nh.gov, the state&#39;s system that also covers cash assistance, Medicaid, and child care on one application, or at your local DHHS District Office. You can also call the DHHS Customer Service Center at 1-844-275-3447 to have an application mailed to you.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/new-hampshire-wic/">New Hampshire WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. New Hampshire WIC runs through local clinics statewide. To apply, find your nearest clinic at signupwic.com, or call the State WIC Agency at 1-800-942-4321.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-new-hampshire/">Unemployment Insurance (New Hampshire)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from New Hampshire Employment Security. File online at nhes.nh.gov, which is the fastest way. If you need help, the NHES claims line is 603-271-7700, open Monday through Friday, 8am to 4:30pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-new-hampshire/">Weatherization Assistance Program (New Hampshire)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible New Hampshire households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system improvements that lower future bills. The New Hampshire Department of Energy runs it through the state&#39;s Community Action Agencies, and both homeowners and renters can qualify. Contact the Community Action Agency serving your area to apply, since the state does not take applications directly.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="new jersey legal services of new jersey (lsnj-law hotline) legal services of new jersey gives free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing, family, public benefits, consumer, employment, and immigration matters. call the statewide lsnj-law hotline at 1-888-576-5529, open monday through friday, 8am to 5:30pm, or find free legal information and self-help tools at lsnjlaw.org. nj 211 dial 2-1-1 from any new jersey phone for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and legal services. nj 211 is a statewide nonprofit that answers around the clock, every day, in many languages. you can also text your zip code to 898211 or search at nj211.org. nj familycare (medicaid &amp; chip) nj familycare is new jersey&#39;s publicly funded health coverage, covering medicaid and chip. it pays for doctor visits, prescriptions, vision, dental, mental health and substance use care, and hospital stays for qualified residents of any age. apply online or call the hotline at 1-800-701-0710. children under 19 can apply regardless of immigration status. nj home energy assistance (liheap &amp; usf) new jersey runs two energy bill programs off one application through the dcaid portal. the universal service fund, usf, gives a monthly credit on gas and electric bills year round. liheap helps with heating and cooling costs on a seasonal schedule. apply at dcaid.dca.nj.gov, or call the energy assistance hotline at 1-800-510-3102. nj snap (food assistance) nj snap is new jersey&#39;s name for food stamps: monthly food money on a families first ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores and some farmers markets. apply online through the state&#39;s nj one app, which also covers cash assistance and nj familycare, or at your county social service agency. new jersey earned income tax credit the new jersey earned income tax credit, njeitc, is worth 40 percent of your federal eitc and is refundable, so it can put cash back in your pocket even if you owe no state tax. new jersey does not follow the federal age limits: residents at least 18 by year end can claim it, with or without children. file a new jersey resident income tax return, the nj-1040, to claim it. new jersey wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum women, infants, and children up to age 5. new jersey wic is offered through local agencies statewide. start a pre-screening at wic.nj.gov, then a local agency follows up to schedule your first appointment. unemployment insurance (new jersey) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the new jersey department of labor. file online any time at myunemployment.nj.gov, or by phone through a reemployment call center: north jersey 201-601-4100, central jersey 732-761-2020, south jersey 856-507-2340. call centers are open monday through friday. weatherization assistance program (new jersey) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible new jersey households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system improvements that lower future bills. the department of community affairs runs the program through local agencies. apply through the dcaid portal at dcaid.dca.nj.gov, or contact the wap agency that serves your county. work first new jersey (cash assistance) work first new jersey, wfnj, is the state&#39;s cash assistance program. wfnj/tanf serves families with children, and wfnj/general assistance serves single adults and couples without children. it pays monthly cash and adds child care, job help, and short-term housing support. apply at njhelps.gov or your county board of social services."><h2>New Jersey programs (10)</h2><article><a href="/programs/legal-services-of-new-jersey-lsnj-law-hotline/">Legal Services of New Jersey (LSNJ-LAW Hotline)</a><p>Legal Services of New Jersey gives free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing, family, public benefits, consumer, employment, and immigration matters. Call the statewide LSNJ-LAW hotline at 1-888-576-5529, open Monday through Friday, 8am to 5:30pm, or find free legal information and self-help tools at lsnjlaw.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/nj-211/">NJ 211</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any New Jersey phone for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and legal services. NJ 211 is a statewide nonprofit that answers around the clock, every day, in many languages. You can also text your zip code to 898211 or search at nj211.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/nj-familycare-medicaid-and-chip/">NJ FamilyCare (Medicaid &amp; CHIP)</a><p>NJ FamilyCare is New Jersey&#39;s publicly funded health coverage, covering Medicaid and CHIP. It pays for doctor visits, prescriptions, vision, dental, mental health and substance use care, and hospital stays for qualified residents of any age. Apply online or call the hotline at 1-800-701-0710. Children under 19 can apply regardless of immigration status.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/nj-home-energy-assistance-liheap-and-usf/">NJ Home Energy Assistance (LIHEAP &amp; USF)</a><p>New Jersey runs two energy bill programs off one application through the DCAid portal. The Universal Service Fund, USF, gives a monthly credit on gas and electric bills year round. LIHEAP helps with heating and cooling costs on a seasonal schedule. Apply at dcaid.dca.nj.gov, or call the energy assistance hotline at 1-800-510-3102.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/nj-snap-food-assistance/">NJ SNAP (Food Assistance)</a><p>NJ SNAP is New Jersey&#39;s name for food stamps: monthly food money on a Families First EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores and some farmers markets. Apply online through the state&#39;s NJ One App, which also covers cash assistance and NJ FamilyCare, or at your county social service agency.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/new-jersey-earned-income-tax-credit/">New Jersey Earned Income Tax Credit</a><p>The New Jersey Earned Income Tax Credit, NJEITC, is worth 40 percent of your federal EITC and is refundable, so it can put cash back in your pocket even if you owe no state tax. New Jersey does not follow the federal age limits: residents at least 18 by year end can claim it, with or without children. File a New Jersey Resident Income Tax Return, the NJ-1040, to claim it.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/new-jersey-wic/">New Jersey WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum women, infants, and children up to age 5. New Jersey WIC is offered through local agencies statewide. Start a pre-screening at wic.nj.gov, then a local agency follows up to schedule your first appointment.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-new-jersey/">Unemployment Insurance (New Jersey)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the New Jersey Department of Labor. File online any time at myunemployment.nj.gov, or by phone through a Reemployment Call Center: North Jersey 201-601-4100, Central Jersey 732-761-2020, South Jersey 856-507-2340. Call centers are open Monday through Friday.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-new-jersey/">Weatherization Assistance Program (New Jersey)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible New Jersey households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system improvements that lower future bills. The Department of Community Affairs runs the program through local agencies. Apply through the DCAid portal at dcaid.dca.nj.gov, or contact the WAP agency that serves your county.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/work-first-new-jersey-cash-assistance/">Work First New Jersey (Cash Assistance)</a><p>Work First New Jersey, WFNJ, is the state&#39;s cash assistance program. WFNJ/TANF serves families with children, and WFNJ/General Assistance serves single adults and couples without children. It pays monthly cash and adds child care, job help, and short-term housing support. Apply at njhelps.gov or your county board of social services.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="new mexico nm energy$mart weatherization program nm energy$mart makes free energy-saving retrofits and home modifications for income-eligible new mexico households, work that lowers future bills. housing new mexico (the mortgage finance authority) runs it, and both homeowners and renters can qualify. there is no statewide intake line; you apply through one of three regional service providers assigned to the area where you live, listed on the housing new mexico site. new mexico 211 dial 2-1-1 from any new mexico phone for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. the service is run by united way and routes your call by where you live. in the albuquerque and santa fe area, united way of north central new mexico answers direct at 505-245-1735, monday through friday, 8am to 5pm. new mexico child tax credit new mexico has a refundable child tax credit worth up to $637 per qualifying child for the lowest-income families, with the amount stepping down as income rises. because it is refundable, it can come back as cash even if you owe no tax. there is no separate application: you claim it on your new mexico personal income tax return, form pit-1, for each qualifying child. new mexico liheap (energy assistance) liheap, the low income home energy assistance program, helps eligible new mexico households with heating and cooling costs. apply online through yesnm at yes.nm.gov, at your local income support division office, or with a paper form the state offers in english, spanish, vietnamese, and simplified chinese. call 1-800-283-4465 with questions, and include a disconnect notice if a shut-off is coming. new mexico legal aid new mexico legal aid gives free civil legal help to low-income residents on the noncriminal problems that upend a household: eviction and housing, family matters, public benefits, consumer and debt issues, and more. call the statewide intake line at 1-833-545-4357 (1-833-lgl-help) or apply online. free plain-language legal information for everyone is at lawhelpnewmexico.com. new mexico medicaid (turquoise care) new mexico medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. most members get care through turquoise care, the managed care program that replaced centennial care on july 1, 2024. apply online through yesnm at yes.nm.gov, or call the consolidated customer service center at 1-800-283-4465. new mexico snap (food assistance) snap is new mexico&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. apply online through yesnm at yes.nm.gov, the state&#39;s one application that also covers cash assistance, medicaid, and energy help, or at your local income support division (isd) office. you can also call the consolidated customer service center at 1-800-283-4465. new mexico wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. new mexico wic runs through the state department of health. request an appointment or check eligibility at nmwic.org, or call 1-866-867-3124 to learn more and find the clinic nearest you. new mexico working families tax credit the working families tax credit is new mexico&#39;s version of the earned income tax credit, and it is refundable, so it can pay out as cash even if you owe no tax. for tax year 2023 and after, the credit equals 25 percent of your federal earned income tax credit, up from 20 percent in 2022. claim it on your new mexico personal income tax return, form pit-1. new mexico works (tanf cash assistance) new mexico works, the state&#39;s tanf program, is a monthly cash benefit for low-income families with children, loaded onto an ebt card you can use like a debit card. it helps cover basics like housing, utilities, and clothing while adults work toward a job. apply online through yesnm at yes.nm.gov or at your local income support division office. questions go to 1-800-283-4465. unemployment insurance (new mexico) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the new mexico department of workforce solutions. file online through the state&#39;s workforce portal at jobs.dws.nm.gov, which is the fastest way, then file a weekly certification for each week you want to be paid. you must be able to work, available for work, and looking for work while you claim."><h2>New Mexico programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/nm-energy-mart-weatherization-program/">NM Energy$mart Weatherization Program</a><p>NM Energy$mart makes free energy-saving retrofits and home modifications for income-eligible New Mexico households, work that lowers future bills. Housing New Mexico (the Mortgage Finance Authority) runs it, and both homeowners and renters can qualify. There is no statewide intake line; you apply through one of three regional service providers assigned to the area where you live, listed on the Housing New Mexico site.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/new-mexico-211/">New Mexico 211</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any New Mexico phone for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. The service is run by United Way and routes your call by where you live. In the Albuquerque and Santa Fe area, United Way of North Central New Mexico answers direct at 505-245-1735, Monday through Friday, 8am to 5pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/new-mexico-child-tax-credit/">New Mexico Child Tax Credit</a><p>New Mexico has a refundable Child Tax Credit worth up to $637 per qualifying child for the lowest-income families, with the amount stepping down as income rises. Because it is refundable, it can come back as cash even if you owe no tax. There is no separate application: you claim it on your New Mexico personal income tax return, Form PIT-1, for each qualifying child.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/new-mexico-liheap-energy-assistance/">New Mexico LIHEAP (Energy Assistance)</a><p>LIHEAP, the Low Income Home Energy Assistance Program, helps eligible New Mexico households with heating and cooling costs. Apply online through YESNM at yes.nm.gov, at your local Income Support Division office, or with a paper form the state offers in English, Spanish, Vietnamese, and Simplified Chinese. Call 1-800-283-4465 with questions, and include a disconnect notice if a shut-off is coming.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/new-mexico-legal-aid/">New Mexico Legal Aid</a><p>New Mexico Legal Aid gives free civil legal help to low-income residents on the noncriminal problems that upend a household: eviction and housing, family matters, public benefits, consumer and debt issues, and more. Call the statewide intake line at 1-833-545-4357 (1-833-LGL-HELP) or apply online. Free plain-language legal information for everyone is at LawHelpNewMexico.com.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/new-mexico-medicaid-turquoise-care/">New Mexico Medicaid (Turquoise Care)</a><p>New Mexico Medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. Most members get care through Turquoise Care, the managed care program that replaced Centennial Care on July 1, 2024. Apply online through YESNM at yes.nm.gov, or call the Consolidated Customer Service Center at 1-800-283-4465.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/new-mexico-snap-food-assistance/">New Mexico SNAP (Food Assistance)</a><p>SNAP is New Mexico&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. Apply online through YESNM at yes.nm.gov, the state&#39;s one application that also covers cash assistance, Medicaid, and energy help, or at your local Income Support Division (ISD) office. You can also call the Consolidated Customer Service Center at 1-800-283-4465.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/new-mexico-wic/">New Mexico WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. New Mexico WIC runs through the state Department of Health. Request an appointment or check eligibility at nmwic.org, or call 1-866-867-3124 to learn more and find the clinic nearest you.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/new-mexico-working-families-tax-credit/">New Mexico Working Families Tax Credit</a><p>The Working Families Tax Credit is New Mexico&#39;s version of the Earned Income Tax Credit, and it is refundable, so it can pay out as cash even if you owe no tax. For tax year 2023 and after, the credit equals 25 percent of your federal Earned Income Tax Credit, up from 20 percent in 2022. Claim it on your New Mexico personal income tax return, Form PIT-1.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/new-mexico-works-tanf-cash-assistance/">New Mexico Works (TANF Cash Assistance)</a><p>New Mexico Works, the state&#39;s TANF program, is a monthly cash benefit for low-income families with children, loaded onto an EBT card you can use like a debit card. It helps cover basics like housing, utilities, and clothing while adults work toward a job. Apply online through YESNM at yes.nm.gov or at your local Income Support Division office. Questions go to 1-800-283-4465.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-new-mexico/">Unemployment Insurance (New Mexico)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the New Mexico Department of Workforce Solutions. File online through the state&#39;s workforce portal at jobs.dws.nm.gov, which is the fastest way, then file a weekly certification for each week you want to be paid. You must be able to work, available for work, and looking for work while you claim.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="new york 2-1-1 new york dial 2-1-1 from any new york phone, or text your zip code to 898-211, for free, confidential help finding local assistance: food, rent, utility bills, health care, and more. regional 211 centers cover all of new york state around the clock. child health plus new york&#39;s health coverage for children under 19 who don&#39;t qualify for medicaid, with no income cap to enroll. coverage is free for families under about 2.2 times the poverty level; higher-income families pay $15 to $60 per child per month. apply through ny state of health or call 1-800-698-4543. heap (home energy assistance program) new york&#39;s help with heating and cooling bills. the regular benefit pays a seasonal credit toward your heating cost, and separate emergency, heating-equipment repair or replacement, and cooling assistance benefits handle crises. apply at mybenefits.ny.gov or your local heap office; the otda hotline is 1-800-342-3009. lawhelpny.org (eviction &amp; legal help) free, plain-language legal information for new yorkers and a directory of local legal aid, covering eviction, debt, public benefits, family law, and more. search by your county or zip code to find the programs and hotlines that serve your area. new york medicaid free or low-cost health coverage for low-income new yorkers. adults 19 to 64 can qualify with income up to 138 percent of the federal poverty level, about $1,836 a month for one person in 2026. apply through ny state of health, the state&#39;s official marketplace, or call the medicaid helpline at 1-800-541-2831. new york state earned income credit a refundable state tax credit for low- and moderate-income workers, worth 30 percent of your federal earned income tax credit, reduced by any new york household credit. because it&#39;s refundable, you can get money back even if you owe no state tax. claim it by filing a state return with form it-215. new york wic healthy food on an ewic card, nutrition counseling, and breastfeeding support for pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children under 5. apply through your local wic office; chat with wanda, the online wic assistant, or call the nys wic program at 518-402-7093 to get connected. snap food benefits (new york) new york snap puts monthly grocery money on an ebt card. check eligibility and apply at mybenefits.ny.gov, or through your local department of social services. questions go to the otda toll-free hotline at 1-800-342-3009. temporary assistance (family assistance &amp; safety net) new york&#39;s cash assistance for people who can&#39;t meet basic needs. family assistance helps families with children and carries a 60-month lifetime limit on federally funded aid; safety net assistance covers single adults, childless couples, and families who have used up family assistance. apply at mybenefits.ny.gov or your local social services district. unemployment insurance (nys dol) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own. file your first claim online through your ny.gov id at the department of labor, or by phone with the telephone claims center at 1-888-209-8124, monday through friday 8am to 5pm. weatherization assistance program (nys hcr) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible new yorkers: insulation, air sealing, and health-and-safety fixes that lower future bills, at no cost to those who qualify. run by nys homes and community renewal through local agencies in all 62 counties, and combined with the empower+ application."><h2>New York programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/2-1-1-new-york/">2-1-1 New York</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any New York phone, or text your zip code to 898-211, for free, confidential help finding local assistance: food, rent, utility bills, health care, and more. Regional 211 centers cover all of New York State around the clock.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/child-health-plus/">Child Health Plus</a><p>New York&#39;s health coverage for children under 19 who don&#39;t qualify for Medicaid, with no income cap to enroll. Coverage is free for families under about 2.2 times the poverty level; higher-income families pay $15 to $60 per child per month. Apply through NY State of Health or call 1-800-698-4543.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/heap-home-energy-assistance-program/">HEAP (Home Energy Assistance Program)</a><p>New York&#39;s help with heating and cooling bills. The Regular benefit pays a seasonal credit toward your heating cost, and separate Emergency, heating-equipment repair or replacement, and Cooling Assistance benefits handle crises. Apply at myBenefits.ny.gov or your local HEAP office; the OTDA hotline is 1-800-342-3009.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/lawhelpny-org-eviction-and-legal-help/">LawHelpNY.org (Eviction &amp; Legal Help)</a><p>Free, plain-language legal information for New Yorkers and a directory of local legal aid, covering eviction, debt, public benefits, family law, and more. Search by your county or zip code to find the programs and hotlines that serve your area.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/new-york-medicaid/">New York Medicaid</a><p>Free or low-cost health coverage for low-income New Yorkers. Adults 19 to 64 can qualify with income up to 138 percent of the federal poverty level, about $1,836 a month for one person in 2026. Apply through NY State of Health, the state&#39;s official marketplace, or call the Medicaid Helpline at 1-800-541-2831.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/new-york-state-earned-income-credit/">New York State Earned Income Credit</a><p>A refundable state tax credit for low- and moderate-income workers, worth 30 percent of your federal Earned Income Tax Credit, reduced by any New York household credit. Because it&#39;s refundable, you can get money back even if you owe no state tax. Claim it by filing a state return with Form IT-215.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/new-york-wic/">New York WIC</a><p>Healthy food on an eWIC card, nutrition counseling, and breastfeeding support for pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children under 5. Apply through your local WIC office; chat with Wanda, the online WIC assistant, or call the NYS WIC Program at 518-402-7093 to get connected.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/snap-food-benefits-new-york/">SNAP Food Benefits (New York)</a><p>New York SNAP puts monthly grocery money on an EBT card. Check eligibility and apply at myBenefits.ny.gov, or through your local department of social services. Questions go to the OTDA toll-free hotline at 1-800-342-3009.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/temporary-assistance-family-assistance-and-safety-net/">Temporary Assistance (Family Assistance &amp; Safety Net)</a><p>New York&#39;s cash assistance for people who can&#39;t meet basic needs. Family Assistance helps families with children and carries a 60-month lifetime limit on federally funded aid; Safety Net Assistance covers single adults, childless couples, and families who have used up Family Assistance. Apply at myBenefits.ny.gov or your local social services district.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-nys-dol/">Unemployment Insurance (NYS DOL)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own. File your first claim online through your NY.gov ID at the Department of Labor, or by phone with the Telephone Claims Center at 1-888-209-8124, Monday through Friday 8am to 5pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-nys-hcr/">Weatherization Assistance Program (NYS HCR)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible New Yorkers: insulation, air sealing, and health-and-safety fixes that lower future bills, at no cost to those who qualify. Run by NYS Homes and Community Renewal through local agencies in all 62 counties, and combined with the EmPower+ application.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="north carolina crisis intervention program (north carolina cip) the crisis intervention program helps when you are in or about to be in a heating or cooling emergency, such as a disconnect notice or no working heat or air. unlike lieap, it runs year-round and covers both heating and cooling. apply at your county department of social services by mail, email, fax, or drop-off. food and nutrition services (north carolina snap) food and nutrition services is north carolina&#39;s name for snap, monthly food money on an ebt card you use like a debit card at the grocery store. apply online at epass.nc.gov, in person at your county department of social services, or by mailing a paper application to your county dss. legal aid of north carolina legal aid of north carolina is the statewide nonprofit law firm that gives free civil legal help to people with low incomes, covering eviction, unsafe housing, domestic violence, public benefits, and more. apply by phone at 1-866-219-5262 or online through the justicehub portal at legalaidnc.org/apply. low income energy assistance program (north carolina lieap) lieap is a one-time yearly payment sent to your heating vendor to help with winter heating costs. the application window is set: households with someone 60 or older or a disabled person getting services through the division of aging and adult services can apply december 1-31, and all other households apply january 1 through march 31 or until funds run out. apply at your county department of social services or online at epass.nc.gov. nc 211 dial 2-1-1 from any north carolina phone, or call 1-888-892-1162, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. nc 211 is run by united way of north carolina, covers all 100 counties, and answers around the clock in english, spanish, and other languages. nc medicaid nc medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. since december 2023, adults 19 to 64 can qualify with income up to 138 percent of the federal poverty level. apply at epass.nc.gov, at healthcare.gov, or at your county department of social services. north carolina wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum women, infants, and children up to age 5. nc wic is offered statewide through county health departments and community health centers. call 1-844-601-6881 to find your local wic clinic. unemployment benefits (north carolina) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the nc division of employment security. file online through myncuibenefits at des.nc.gov, which is the fastest way. for help, call the ui support center at 888-737-0259. weatherization assistance program (north carolina) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible north carolinians: insulation, air sealing, and heating and cooling repairs that lower future bills. the nc department of environmental quality runs it through local agencies. use the find your agency tool on the deq site to reach the provider serving your county. work first family assistance (north carolina tanf) work first is north carolina&#39;s tanf program, giving short-term cash assistance and work services to low-income families with children. cash is provided primarily for the children, and the program pairs it with help finding a job. apply online at epass.nc.gov or at your county department of social services."><h2>North Carolina programs (10)</h2><article><a href="/programs/crisis-intervention-program-north-carolina-cip/">Crisis Intervention Program (North Carolina CIP)</a><p>The Crisis Intervention Program helps when you are in or about to be in a heating or cooling emergency, such as a disconnect notice or no working heat or air. Unlike LIEAP, it runs year-round and covers both heating and cooling. Apply at your county Department of Social Services by mail, email, fax, or drop-off.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/food-and-nutrition-services-north-carolina-snap/">Food and Nutrition Services (North Carolina SNAP)</a><p>Food and Nutrition Services is North Carolina&#39;s name for SNAP, monthly food money on an EBT card you use like a debit card at the grocery store. Apply online at epass.nc.gov, in person at your county Department of Social Services, or by mailing a paper application to your county DSS.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/legal-aid-of-north-carolina/">Legal Aid of North Carolina</a><p>Legal Aid of North Carolina is the statewide nonprofit law firm that gives free civil legal help to people with low incomes, covering eviction, unsafe housing, domestic violence, public benefits, and more. Apply by phone at 1-866-219-5262 or online through the JusticeHub portal at legalaidnc.org/apply.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/low-income-energy-assistance-program-north-carolina-lieap/">Low Income Energy Assistance Program (North Carolina LIEAP)</a><p>LIEAP is a one-time yearly payment sent to your heating vendor to help with winter heating costs. The application window is set: households with someone 60 or older or a disabled person getting services through the Division of Aging and Adult Services can apply December 1-31, and all other households apply January 1 through March 31 or until funds run out. Apply at your county Department of Social Services or online at epass.nc.gov.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/nc-211/">NC 211</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any North Carolina phone, or call 1-888-892-1162, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. NC 211 is run by United Way of North Carolina, covers all 100 counties, and answers around the clock in English, Spanish, and other languages.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/nc-medicaid/">NC Medicaid</a><p>NC Medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. Since December 2023, adults 19 to 64 can qualify with income up to 138 percent of the federal poverty level. Apply at epass.nc.gov, at HealthCare.gov, or at your county Department of Social Services.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/north-carolina-wic/">North Carolina WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum women, infants, and children up to age 5. NC WIC is offered statewide through county health departments and community health centers. Call 1-844-601-6881 to find your local WIC clinic.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-benefits-north-carolina/">Unemployment Benefits (North Carolina)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the NC Division of Employment Security. File online through MyNCUIBenefits at des.nc.gov, which is the fastest way. For help, call the UI Support Center at 888-737-0259.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-north-carolina/">Weatherization Assistance Program (North Carolina)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible North Carolinians: insulation, air sealing, and heating and cooling repairs that lower future bills. The NC Department of Environmental Quality runs it through local agencies. Use the Find Your Agency tool on the DEQ site to reach the provider serving your county.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/work-first-family-assistance-north-carolina-tanf/">Work First Family Assistance (North Carolina TANF)</a><p>Work First is North Carolina&#39;s TANF program, giving short-term cash assistance and work services to low-income families with children. Cash is provided primarily for the children, and the program pairs it with help finding a job. Apply online at epass.nc.gov or at your county Department of Social Services.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="north dakota legal services of north dakota legal services of north dakota provides free civil legal help to low-income and older north dakotans on housing, family, public benefits, consumer, and veteran matters. call the statewide intake line at 1-800-634-5263, or if you are age 60 or older call the senior line at 1-866-621-9886. intake runs monday through thursday, 9am to 3pm central. north dakota 211 (firstlink) dial 2-1-1 from any north dakota phone, or text your zip code to 898211, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, and more. firstlink runs 211 statewide and answers around the clock, every day. firstlink&#39;s office line is 701-293-6462, and it also answers the 988 suicide and crisis lifeline locally. north dakota child care assistance program (ccap) the child care assistance program, ccap, helps eligible low-income north dakota families pay for child care while parents work, look for work, or attend school or training. apply online through the self service portal at applyforhelp.nd.gov, which also covers snap, medicaid, liheap, and tanf, or by phone through the hhs customer support center at 1-866-614-6005. north dakota liheap (energy assistance) the low income home energy assistance program, liheap, helps income-eligible north dakota households with home heating and energy costs, and it can also help in a heating emergency. apply online through the self service portal at applyforhelp.nd.gov, or by phone through the hhs customer support center at 1-866-614-6005. ask about the current application dates when you call, since the heating-season window matters. north dakota medicaid (expanded) north dakota medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. the state expanded medicaid, so low-income adults ages 19 to 64 can qualify. apply online through the self service portal at applyforhelp.nd.gov, or by phone through the hhs customer support center at 1-866-614-6005. north dakota snap (food assistance) snap is north dakota&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. apply online through the state self service portal at applyforhelp.nd.gov, which handles snap, medicaid, liheap, tanf, and child care on one application, or by phone through the hhs customer support center at 1-866-614-6005. north dakota tanf (cash assistance) temporary assistance for needy families, tanf, is north dakota&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children. most adults take part in the jobs program, job opportunities and basic skills, which helps you move toward work. apply online through the self service portal at applyforhelp.nd.gov or by phone through the hhs customer support center at 1-866-614-6005. north dakota wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition guidance, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. to start, call the statewide toll-free line at 1-800-472-2286, schedule online at signupwic.com, or call your local wic clinic. the first appointment is in person and takes about 30 to 45 minutes. unemployment insurance (job service north dakota) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from job service north dakota. file online through the ui ice claims system at apps.nd.gov, which is the fastest way, or get help from the unemployment insurance customer service line at 701-328-1680. weatherization assistance program (north dakota) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible north dakota households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system improvements that lower future bills and make the home safer. north dakota&#39;s community action agencies have weatherized homes since 1976, and both homeowners and renters can qualify. there is no fee for applicants. call 1-800-726-7960 or contact the community action agency serving your area to apply."><h2>North Dakota programs (10)</h2><article><a href="/programs/legal-services-of-north-dakota/">Legal Services of North Dakota</a><p>Legal Services of North Dakota provides free civil legal help to low-income and older North Dakotans on housing, family, public benefits, consumer, and veteran matters. Call the statewide intake line at 1-800-634-5263, or if you are age 60 or older call the senior line at 1-866-621-9886. Intake runs Monday through Thursday, 9am to 3pm Central.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/north-dakota-211-firstlink/">North Dakota 211 (FirstLink)</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any North Dakota phone, or text your ZIP code to 898211, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, and more. FirstLink runs 211 statewide and answers around the clock, every day. FirstLink&#39;s office line is 701-293-6462, and it also answers the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline locally.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/north-dakota-child-care-assistance-program-ccap/">North Dakota Child Care Assistance Program (CCAP)</a><p>The Child Care Assistance Program, CCAP, helps eligible low-income North Dakota families pay for child care while parents work, look for work, or attend school or training. Apply online through the Self Service Portal at applyforhelp.nd.gov, which also covers SNAP, Medicaid, LIHEAP, and TANF, or by phone through the HHS Customer Support Center at 1-866-614-6005.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/north-dakota-liheap-energy-assistance/">North Dakota LIHEAP (Energy Assistance)</a><p>The Low Income Home Energy Assistance Program, LIHEAP, helps income-eligible North Dakota households with home heating and energy costs, and it can also help in a heating emergency. Apply online through the Self Service Portal at applyforhelp.nd.gov, or by phone through the HHS Customer Support Center at 1-866-614-6005. Ask about the current application dates when you call, since the heating-season window matters.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/north-dakota-medicaid-expanded/">North Dakota Medicaid (Expanded)</a><p>North Dakota Medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. The state expanded Medicaid, so low-income adults ages 19 to 64 can qualify. Apply online through the Self Service Portal at applyforhelp.nd.gov, or by phone through the HHS Customer Support Center at 1-866-614-6005.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/north-dakota-snap-food-assistance/">North Dakota SNAP (Food Assistance)</a><p>SNAP is North Dakota&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. Apply online through the state Self Service Portal at applyforhelp.nd.gov, which handles SNAP, Medicaid, LIHEAP, TANF, and child care on one application, or by phone through the HHS Customer Support Center at 1-866-614-6005.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/north-dakota-tanf-cash-assistance/">North Dakota TANF (Cash Assistance)</a><p>Temporary Assistance for Needy Families, TANF, is North Dakota&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children. Most adults take part in the JOBS program, Job Opportunities and Basic Skills, which helps you move toward work. Apply online through the Self Service Portal at applyforhelp.nd.gov or by phone through the HHS Customer Support Center at 1-866-614-6005.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/north-dakota-wic/">North Dakota WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition guidance, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. To start, call the statewide toll-free line at 1-800-472-2286, schedule online at signupwic.com, or call your local WIC clinic. The first appointment is in person and takes about 30 to 45 minutes.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-job-service-north-dakota/">Unemployment Insurance (Job Service North Dakota)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from Job Service North Dakota. File online through the UI ICE claims system at apps.nd.gov, which is the fastest way, or get help from the Unemployment Insurance Customer Service line at 701-328-1680.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-north-dakota/">Weatherization Assistance Program (North Dakota)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible North Dakota households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system improvements that lower future bills and make the home safer. North Dakota&#39;s Community Action Agencies have weatherized homes since 1976, and both homeowners and renters can qualify. There is no fee for applicants. Call 1-800-726-7960 or contact the Community Action Agency serving your area to apply.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="ohio heap energy assistance (ohio) ohio&#39;s home energy assistance program is a one-time benefit applied to your heating or cooling bill, plus a winter crisis program (november 1 to march 31) and a summer crisis program for emergencies. as of april 2026 it is run by the ohio department of job and family services. apply online, by appointment, or by mail through your local energy assistance provider. home weatherization assistance program (ohio hwap) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible ohioans: insulation, air-leak sealing, and heating-system repairs that lower future bills. hwap runs through local energy assistance providers serving all 88 counties, and as of april 2026 is administered by the ohio department of job and family services. medicaid (ohio) ohio medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, adults, older adults, and people with disabilities. adults 19 to 64 can qualify with income at or below 138 percent of the federal poverty level under the group viii adult category. apply online through ohio benefits at benefits.ohio.gov or by phone at 1-844-640-6446. ohio 211 dial 2-1-1 from any ohio phone for free, confidential help finding local assistance: housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and mental health. ohio 211 runs through regional united way hubs, and a 2026 statewide expansion is extending around-the-clock coverage toward all 88 counties. ohio earned income credit ohio&#39;s state earned income credit is worth 30 percent of your federal earned income tax credit. it is nonrefundable, so it can lower your ohio income tax to zero but does not pay out beyond that. claim it on the ohio schedule of credits with your state return. ohio legal help (eviction &amp; legal aid) free, plain-language legal information for ohioans, guided court-form tools through a myolh account, and a find a lawyer directory to reach local legal aid. ohio legal help covers eviction, housing, debt, family law, and public benefits. ohio wic healthy food, nutrition education, and breastfeeding support for pregnant and postpartum women, infants, and children up to age 5. wic clinics operate in all 88 ohio counties. call the help me grow helpline at 1-800-755-4769 to find your local clinic. ohio works first (cash assistance) ohio works first is the cash-assistance part of ohio&#39;s tanf program, paying monthly cash on the way2go debit card to low-income families with children. ohio sets a 36-month state time limit on benefits. apply online through ohio benefits at benefits.ohio.gov or at your county department of job and family services. pipp plus (percentage of income payment plan) pipp plus lets income-eligible ohioans on a puco-regulated utility pay a set percentage of household income toward gas and electric bills instead of the full amount, with a $10 monthly minimum. as of april 2026 it is run by the ohio department of job and family services. apply online, by appointment, or by mail through your local energy assistance provider. snap food assistance (ohio) ohio snap loads monthly food money on the ohio direction card, which works like a debit card at grocery stores. apply online through ohio benefits at benefits.ohio.gov, by phone at 1-844-640-6446, or at your county department of job and family services. unemployment benefits (ohio) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the ohio department of job and family services. file online at unemployment.ohio.gov, or by phone at 1-877-644-6562. the call center is open 8am to 5pm, monday through friday."><h2>Ohio programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/heap-energy-assistance-ohio/">HEAP Energy Assistance (Ohio)</a><p>Ohio&#39;s Home Energy Assistance Program is a one-time benefit applied to your heating or cooling bill, plus a Winter Crisis Program (November 1 to March 31) and a Summer Crisis Program for emergencies. As of April 2026 it is run by the Ohio Department of Job and Family Services. Apply online, by appointment, or by mail through your local Energy Assistance Provider.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/home-weatherization-assistance-program-ohio-hwap/">Home Weatherization Assistance Program (Ohio HWAP)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible Ohioans: insulation, air-leak sealing, and heating-system repairs that lower future bills. HWAP runs through local energy assistance providers serving all 88 counties, and as of April 2026 is administered by the Ohio Department of Job and Family Services.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/medicaid-ohio/">Medicaid (Ohio)</a><p>Ohio Medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, adults, older adults, and people with disabilities. Adults 19 to 64 can qualify with income at or below 138 percent of the federal poverty level under the Group VIII adult category. Apply online through Ohio Benefits at benefits.ohio.gov or by phone at 1-844-640-6446.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/ohio-211/">Ohio 211</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Ohio phone for free, confidential help finding local assistance: housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and mental health. Ohio 211 runs through regional United Way hubs, and a 2026 statewide expansion is extending around-the-clock coverage toward all 88 counties.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/ohio-earned-income-credit/">Ohio Earned Income Credit</a><p>Ohio&#39;s state Earned Income Credit is worth 30 percent of your federal Earned Income Tax Credit. It is nonrefundable, so it can lower your Ohio income tax to zero but does not pay out beyond that. Claim it on the Ohio Schedule of Credits with your state return.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/ohio-legal-help-eviction-and-legal-aid/">Ohio Legal Help (Eviction &amp; Legal Aid)</a><p>Free, plain-language legal information for Ohioans, guided court-form tools through a MyOLH account, and a Find a Lawyer directory to reach local legal aid. Ohio Legal Help covers eviction, housing, debt, family law, and public benefits.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/ohio-wic/">Ohio WIC</a><p>Healthy food, nutrition education, and breastfeeding support for pregnant and postpartum women, infants, and children up to age 5. WIC clinics operate in all 88 Ohio counties. Call the Help Me Grow Helpline at 1-800-755-4769 to find your local clinic.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/ohio-works-first-cash-assistance/">Ohio Works First (Cash Assistance)</a><p>Ohio Works First is the cash-assistance part of Ohio&#39;s TANF program, paying monthly cash on the Way2Go debit card to low-income families with children. Ohio sets a 36-month state time limit on benefits. Apply online through Ohio Benefits at benefits.ohio.gov or at your County Department of Job and Family Services.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/pipp-plus-percentage-of-income-payment-plan/">PIPP Plus (Percentage of Income Payment Plan)</a><p>PIPP Plus lets income-eligible Ohioans on a PUCO-regulated utility pay a set percentage of household income toward gas and electric bills instead of the full amount, with a $10 monthly minimum. As of April 2026 it is run by the Ohio Department of Job and Family Services. Apply online, by appointment, or by mail through your local Energy Assistance Provider.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/snap-food-assistance-ohio/">SNAP Food Assistance (Ohio)</a><p>Ohio SNAP loads monthly food money on the Ohio Direction Card, which works like a debit card at grocery stores. Apply online through Ohio Benefits at benefits.ohio.gov, by phone at 1-844-640-6446, or at your County Department of Job and Family Services.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-benefits-ohio/">Unemployment Benefits (Ohio)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Ohio Department of Job and Family Services. File online at unemployment.ohio.gov, or by phone at 1-877-644-6562. The call center is open 8am to 5pm, Monday through Friday.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="oklahoma 211 oklahoma dial 2-1-1 from any oklahoma phone for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utility bills, food, healthcare, and more. a resource specialist searches for programs, agencies, and charities near you while you are on the line. you can also find and search your local 2-1-1 online at 211.org. legal aid services of oklahoma legal aid services of oklahoma provides free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. call the statewide line at 1-888-534-5243 to reach the office serving your area, or find free legal information and self-help tools at oklaw.org. oklahoma child care subsidy the oklahoma child care subsidy helps eligible families pay for child care while parents work, train, or go to school. apply online at okdhslive.org, at your local oklahoma human services office, or by phone at 405-522-5050. families may owe a copay based on income, family size, and how many children need care. oklahoma earned income tax credit oklahoma has a state earned income tax credit worth 5 percent of your federal eitc. as of tax year 2022 the oklahoma credit is refundable again, so it can pay out as cash even when it is larger than the state tax you owe. claim it on your oklahoma income tax return. oklahoma liheap (energy assistance) the low income home energy assistance program, liheap, helps low-income oklahoma households with home energy costs in three seasonal parts: winter heating, the energy crisis assistance program for a heating or cooling emergency, and summer cooling. apply online at okdhslive.org during each open enrollment window. for a life-threatening energy crisis, call 405-522-5050 year-round. oklahoma snap (food benefits) snap is oklahoma&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. apply online at okdhslive.org, the state benefits portal that also screens you for soonercare, tanf, and energy help, at your local oklahoma human services office, or by phone at 405-522-5050. most applications include a phone or in-person interview, and a case can take up to 30 days. oklahoma tanf (cash assistance) temporary assistance for needy families, tanf, is oklahoma&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with a minor child in the home. adults sign a personalized tanf work plan aimed at getting or keeping a job, and the cash comes on a time-limited basis. apply online at okdhslive.org, at your local oklahoma human services office, or by phone at 405-522-5050. oklahoma wic the oklahoma women, infants and children program, wic, gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. apply through the online wic enrollment tool or a local wic clinic. for more information, call 1-888-655-2942 or 405-426-8500. soonercare (oklahoma medicaid) soonercare is oklahoma&#39;s medicaid program: free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. oklahoma expanded soonercare in 2021 to cover adults ages 19 to 64. apply online at mysoonercare.org, or call the soonercare helpline at 1-800-987-7767. unemployment insurance (oklahoma oesc) weekly unemployment payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the oklahoma employment security commission, oesc. file online through the oesc claimant portal at claimantportal.oesc.ok.gov, which is the fastest way, or reach the unemployment support line at 405-525-1500 for help. weatherization assistance program (oklahoma) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible oklahoma households: air sealing, insulation, weather-stripping, and heating-system repairs that lower future bills. the oklahoma department of commerce runs the program through local community action agencies. contact the community action agency serving your county to apply, since commerce does not take applications directly."><h2>Oklahoma programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/211-oklahoma/">211 Oklahoma</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Oklahoma phone for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utility bills, food, healthcare, and more. A resource specialist searches for programs, agencies, and charities near you while you are on the line. You can also find and search your local 2-1-1 online at 211.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/legal-aid-services-of-oklahoma/">Legal Aid Services of Oklahoma</a><p>Legal Aid Services of Oklahoma provides free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. Call the statewide line at 1-888-534-5243 to reach the office serving your area, or find free legal information and self-help tools at oklaw.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/oklahoma-child-care-subsidy/">Oklahoma Child Care Subsidy</a><p>The Oklahoma Child Care Subsidy helps eligible families pay for child care while parents work, train, or go to school. Apply online at OKDHSLive.org, at your local Oklahoma Human Services office, or by phone at 405-522-5050. Families may owe a copay based on income, family size, and how many children need care.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/oklahoma-earned-income-tax-credit/">Oklahoma Earned Income Tax Credit</a><p>Oklahoma has a state Earned Income Tax Credit worth 5 percent of your federal EITC. As of tax year 2022 the Oklahoma credit is refundable again, so it can pay out as cash even when it is larger than the state tax you owe. Claim it on your Oklahoma income tax return.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/oklahoma-liheap-energy-assistance/">Oklahoma LIHEAP (Energy Assistance)</a><p>The Low Income Home Energy Assistance Program, LIHEAP, helps low-income Oklahoma households with home energy costs in three seasonal parts: Winter Heating, the Energy Crisis Assistance Program for a heating or cooling emergency, and Summer Cooling. Apply online at OKDHSLive.org during each open enrollment window. For a life-threatening energy crisis, call 405-522-5050 year-round.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/oklahoma-snap-food-benefits/">Oklahoma SNAP (Food Benefits)</a><p>SNAP is Oklahoma&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. Apply online at OKDHSLive.org, the state benefits portal that also screens you for SoonerCare, TANF, and energy help, at your local Oklahoma Human Services office, or by phone at 405-522-5050. Most applications include a phone or in-person interview, and a case can take up to 30 days.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/oklahoma-tanf-cash-assistance/">Oklahoma TANF (Cash Assistance)</a><p>Temporary Assistance for Needy Families, TANF, is Oklahoma&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with a minor child in the home. Adults sign a personalized TANF Work Plan aimed at getting or keeping a job, and the cash comes on a time-limited basis. Apply online at OKDHSLive.org, at your local Oklahoma Human Services office, or by phone at 405-522-5050.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/oklahoma-wic/">Oklahoma WIC</a><p>The Oklahoma Women, Infants and Children program, WIC, gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. Apply through the online WIC enrollment tool or a local WIC clinic. For more information, call 1-888-655-2942 or 405-426-8500.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/soonercare-oklahoma-medicaid/">SoonerCare (Oklahoma Medicaid)</a><p>SoonerCare is Oklahoma&#39;s Medicaid program: free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. Oklahoma expanded SoonerCare in 2021 to cover adults ages 19 to 64. Apply online at mysoonercare.org, or call the SoonerCare Helpline at 1-800-987-7767.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-oklahoma-oesc/">Unemployment Insurance (Oklahoma OESC)</a><p>Weekly unemployment payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Oklahoma Employment Security Commission, OESC. File online through the OESC Claimant Portal at claimantportal.oesc.ok.gov, which is the fastest way, or reach the unemployment support line at 405-525-1500 for help.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-oklahoma/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Oklahoma)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible Oklahoma households: air sealing, insulation, weather-stripping, and heating-system repairs that lower future bills. The Oklahoma Department of Commerce runs the program through local Community Action Agencies. Contact the Community Action Agency serving your county to apply, since Commerce does not take applications directly.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="oregon 211info (oregon 211) dial 2-1-1 from any oregon phone for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. 211info is a statewide service. you can also text your zip code to 898211 to get connected to local resources, or search at 211info.org. legal aid services of oregon legal aid services of oregon provides free civil legal help to low-income oregonians on housing, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. for an eviction, call the statewide eviction defense project at 888-585-9638. for public benefits problems, call the public benefits hotline at 503-241-4111. find your local legal aid office at lasoregon.org, and free self-help information at oregonlawhelp.org. oregon earned income credit the oregon earned income credit is a refundable state credit for working people who qualify for the federal earned income tax credit. it is worth 9 percent of your federal eitc, and 12 percent if you have a dependent who is younger than 3 at the end of the tax year. because it is refundable, it can come back to you as a refund even if you owe no oregon tax. claim it on your oregon return. oregon energy assistance (liheap / oeap) oregon housing and community services helps income-eligible households with home heating and cooling bills through the federal liheap program and the oregon energy assistance program. you do not apply to the state directly; you apply through the local community action agency serving your county. find your agency through oregon housing and community services at 800-453-5511 or by dialing 2-1-1. oregon health plan (medicaid) the oregon health plan, ohp, is free health coverage for oregonians with lower incomes: working families, children, pregnant people, single adults, and seniors. oregon expanded medicaid, so low-income adults 19 to 64 can qualify. apply online through one at one.oregon.gov or by phone at 800-699-9075, monday through friday, 7am to 6pm. members with coverage questions call 800-273-0557. oregon kids credit the oregon kids credit is a refundable state tax credit worth up to $1,050 for each qualifying child age 5 or younger, for up to five children. for the 2025 tax year the full credit goes to families with modified adjusted gross income of $26,550 or less, and it phases out completely at $31,550. because it is refundable, it pays out even if you owe no oregon tax. claim it on your oregon return. oregon snap (food benefits) snap is oregon&#39;s food benefit program: monthly food money loaded onto an oregon trail card (ebt) you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. apply online through one at one.oregon.gov, at a local oregon department of human services office, or by phone at 800-699-9075, monday through friday, 7am to 6pm. one one application also checks you for the oregon health plan, tanf cash, and child care help. oregon tanf (cash assistance) temporary assistance for needy families, tanf, is oregon&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children, pregnant people, and young heads of household. a family of three with no other income can get up to $506 a month. apply online through one at one.oregon.gov, at a local oregon department of human services office, or by phone at 800-699-9075. most adults take part in the jobs employment program. oregon wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. oregon wic runs through local clinics in every county. start with the online interest form at the oregon health authority wic site, call the state wic office at 971-673-0040, or dial 2-1-1 to find your local clinic. you finish enrolling at an appointment with the clinic. unemployment insurance (oregon) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the oregon employment department. file online through frances online at frances.oregon.gov, which is available 24 hours a day and is the easiest way, or apply by phone at 1-877-345-3484 (1-877-file-4-ui). you then file a claim for each week to keep payments coming. weatherization assistance (oregon) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible oregon households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system improvements that lower future bills, including a home oil weatherization option for oil-heated homes. oregon housing and community services runs the program through local community action agencies. contact the agency serving your county, or reach oregon housing and community services at 800-453-5511, to apply."><h2>Oregon programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/211info-oregon-211/">211info (Oregon 211)</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Oregon phone for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. 211info is a statewide service. You can also text your zip code to 898211 to get connected to local resources, or search at 211info.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/legal-aid-services-of-oregon/">Legal Aid Services of Oregon</a><p>Legal Aid Services of Oregon provides free civil legal help to low-income Oregonians on housing, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. For an eviction, call the statewide Eviction Defense Project at 888-585-9638. For public benefits problems, call the Public Benefits Hotline at 503-241-4111. Find your local legal aid office at lasoregon.org, and free self-help information at oregonlawhelp.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/oregon-earned-income-credit/">Oregon Earned Income Credit</a><p>The Oregon Earned Income Credit is a refundable state credit for working people who qualify for the federal Earned Income Tax Credit. It is worth 9 percent of your federal EITC, and 12 percent if you have a dependent who is younger than 3 at the end of the tax year. Because it is refundable, it can come back to you as a refund even if you owe no Oregon tax. Claim it on your Oregon return.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/oregon-energy-assistance-liheap-oeap/">Oregon Energy Assistance (LIHEAP / OEAP)</a><p>Oregon Housing and Community Services helps income-eligible households with home heating and cooling bills through the federal LIHEAP program and the Oregon Energy Assistance Program. You do not apply to the state directly; you apply through the local Community Action Agency serving your county. Find your agency through Oregon Housing and Community Services at 800-453-5511 or by dialing 2-1-1.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/oregon-health-plan-medicaid/">Oregon Health Plan (Medicaid)</a><p>The Oregon Health Plan, OHP, is free health coverage for Oregonians with lower incomes: working families, children, pregnant people, single adults, and seniors. Oregon expanded Medicaid, so low-income adults 19 to 64 can qualify. Apply online through ONE at one.oregon.gov or by phone at 800-699-9075, Monday through Friday, 7am to 6pm. Members with coverage questions call 800-273-0557.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/oregon-kids-credit/">Oregon Kids Credit</a><p>The Oregon Kids Credit is a refundable state tax credit worth up to $1,050 for each qualifying child age 5 or younger, for up to five children. For the 2025 tax year the full credit goes to families with modified adjusted gross income of $26,550 or less, and it phases out completely at $31,550. Because it is refundable, it pays out even if you owe no Oregon tax. Claim it on your Oregon return.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/oregon-snap-food-benefits/">Oregon SNAP (Food Benefits)</a><p>SNAP is Oregon&#39;s food benefit program: monthly food money loaded onto an Oregon Trail Card (EBT) you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. Apply online through ONE at one.oregon.gov, at a local Oregon Department of Human Services office, or by phone at 800-699-9075, Monday through Friday, 7am to 6pm. One ONE application also checks you for the Oregon Health Plan, TANF cash, and child care help.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/oregon-tanf-cash-assistance/">Oregon TANF (Cash Assistance)</a><p>Temporary Assistance for Needy Families, TANF, is Oregon&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children, pregnant people, and young heads of household. A family of three with no other income can get up to $506 a month. Apply online through ONE at one.oregon.gov, at a local Oregon Department of Human Services office, or by phone at 800-699-9075. Most adults take part in the JOBS employment program.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/oregon-wic/">Oregon WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. Oregon WIC runs through local clinics in every county. Start with the online interest form at the Oregon Health Authority WIC site, call the state WIC office at 971-673-0040, or dial 2-1-1 to find your local clinic. You finish enrolling at an appointment with the clinic.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-oregon/">Unemployment Insurance (Oregon)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Oregon Employment Department. File online through Frances Online at frances.oregon.gov, which is available 24 hours a day and is the easiest way, or apply by phone at 1-877-345-3484 (1-877-File-4-UI). You then file a claim for each week to keep payments coming.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-oregon/">Weatherization Assistance (Oregon)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible Oregon households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system improvements that lower future bills, including a Home Oil Weatherization option for oil-heated homes. Oregon Housing and Community Services runs the program through local Community Action Agencies. Contact the agency serving your county, or reach Oregon Housing and Community Services at 800-453-5511, to apply.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="pennsylvania chip (children&#39;s health insurance program) pennsylvania chip covers uninsured children and teens up to age 19 who are not eligible for medical assistance, with no family income limit that blocks enrollment. for most families chip is free; others pay low monthly premiums. apply online or call the chip helpline at 1-800-986-5437. liheap (home energy assistance) pennsylvania&#39;s liheap helps low-income households pay heating bills with a cash grant, plus a separate crisis grant when heat is shut off, about to be, or broken. apply at compass during the season; the liheap helpline is 1-866-857-7095. medical assistance (pennsylvania medicaid) medical assistance is pennsylvania&#39;s medicaid: free or low-cost health coverage delivered through healthchoices managed care plans. adults 19 to 64 can qualify with income at or below 133 percent of the federal poverty guidelines. apply at compass, your county assistance office, or by phone at 1-866-550-4355. pa 211 dial 2-1-1 from any pennsylvania phone, or text your zip code to 898-211, for free, confidential help finding local assistance: housing, utilities, food, childcare, and more. pa 211 covers all 67 counties around the clock through regional united way centers. palawhelp.org (eviction &amp; legal help) free, plain-language legal information for pennsylvanians and a directory of the legal aid programs that serve each county, from the pennsylvania legal aid network. covers eviction, benefits, family law, and more, with a legal help finder to reach a local provider. pennsylvania wic healthy food on an ewic card, nutrition counseling, and breastfeeding support for pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. start a pre-application online at pawic.com, or call 1-800-942-9467 to reach your closest wic office. snap food stamps (pennsylvania) pennsylvania snap loads monthly food money on an ebt access card. apply online at compass (compass.dhs.pa.gov), at your county assistance office, or by mail. the dhs helpline is 1-800-692-7462. tanf cash assistance pennsylvania&#39;s cash help for low-income families with children, paid on the ebt access card. adults are limited to 60 months of tanf in a lifetime, though extended tanf can continue benefits in specific hardship situations. apply at compass or your county assistance office. unemployment compensation (pa l&amp;i) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own. file your initial claim online at the department of labor and industry, or by phone with the uc service center at 1-888-313-7284, monday through friday 8am to 4pm. file weekly certifications through pa teleclaims. weatherization assistance program (pa dced) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible pennsylvanians: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system fixes that lower future bills. funded by the u.s. department of energy and run through community action agencies in every county, overseen by the department of community and economic development. working pennsylvanians tax credit pennsylvania&#39;s new refundable state tax credit for low- and moderate-income workers, worth 10 percent of your federal earned income tax credit, up to $805. it starts with the 2026 tax season, and if you qualify for the federal eitc you automatically qualify. claim it on your pa-40 state return."><h2>Pennsylvania programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/chip-childrens-health-insurance-program/">CHIP (Children&#39;s Health Insurance Program)</a><p>Pennsylvania CHIP covers uninsured children and teens up to age 19 who are not eligible for Medical Assistance, with no family income limit that blocks enrollment. For most families CHIP is free; others pay low monthly premiums. Apply online or call the CHIP helpline at 1-800-986-5437.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/liheap-home-energy-assistance-2/">LIHEAP (Home Energy Assistance)</a><p>Pennsylvania&#39;s LIHEAP helps low-income households pay heating bills with a cash grant, plus a separate Crisis grant when heat is shut off, about to be, or broken. Apply at COMPASS during the season; the LIHEAP Helpline is 1-866-857-7095.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/medical-assistance-pennsylvania-medicaid/">Medical Assistance (Pennsylvania Medicaid)</a><p>Medical Assistance is Pennsylvania&#39;s Medicaid: free or low-cost health coverage delivered through HealthChoices managed care plans. Adults 19 to 64 can qualify with income at or below 133 percent of the federal poverty guidelines. Apply at COMPASS, your county assistance office, or by phone at 1-866-550-4355.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/pa-211/">PA 211</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Pennsylvania phone, or text your zip code to 898-211, for free, confidential help finding local assistance: housing, utilities, food, childcare, and more. PA 211 covers all 67 counties around the clock through regional United Way centers.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/palawhelp-org-eviction-and-legal-help/">PALawHelp.org (Eviction &amp; Legal Help)</a><p>Free, plain-language legal information for Pennsylvanians and a directory of the legal aid programs that serve each county, from the Pennsylvania Legal Aid Network. Covers eviction, benefits, family law, and more, with a Legal Help Finder to reach a local provider.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/pennsylvania-wic/">Pennsylvania WIC</a><p>Healthy food on an eWIC card, nutrition counseling, and breastfeeding support for pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. Start a pre-application online at pawic.com, or call 1-800-942-9467 to reach your closest WIC office.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/snap-food-stamps-pennsylvania/">SNAP Food Stamps (Pennsylvania)</a><p>Pennsylvania SNAP loads monthly food money on an EBT ACCESS card. Apply online at COMPASS (compass.dhs.pa.gov), at your county assistance office, or by mail. The DHS Helpline is 1-800-692-7462.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/tanf-cash-assistance-2/">TANF Cash Assistance</a><p>Pennsylvania&#39;s cash help for low-income families with children, paid on the EBT ACCESS card. Adults are limited to 60 months of TANF in a lifetime, though Extended TANF can continue benefits in specific hardship situations. Apply at COMPASS or your county assistance office.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-compensation-pa-l-and-i/">Unemployment Compensation (PA L&amp;I)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own. File your initial claim online at the Department of Labor and Industry, or by phone with the UC Service Center at 1-888-313-7284, Monday through Friday 8am to 4pm. File weekly certifications through PA Teleclaims.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-pa-dced/">Weatherization Assistance Program (PA DCED)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible Pennsylvanians: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system fixes that lower future bills. Funded by the U.S. Department of Energy and run through Community Action Agencies in every county, overseen by the Department of Community and Economic Development.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/working-pennsylvanians-tax-credit/">Working Pennsylvanians Tax Credit</a><p>Pennsylvania&#39;s new refundable state tax credit for low- and moderate-income workers, worth 10 percent of your federal Earned Income Tax Credit, up to $805. It starts with the 2026 tax season, and if you qualify for the federal EITC you automatically qualify. Claim it on your PA-40 state return.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="rhode island 211 rhode island (united way of rhode island) dial 2-1-1 from any rhode island phone, or call 401-519-0360, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, child care, healthcare, and more. 211 rhode island is run by united way of rhode island and answers around the clock, every day of the year, with translation available in many languages. you can also chat with a resource specialist online. ri child care assistance program (ccap) the child care assistance program helps low- and moderate-income rhode island families pay for child care so parents can work or train for work. it covers children under 13, or up to 18 with special needs, at licensed and approved providers. apply online through the healthyrhode portal at healthyrhode.ri.gov or by phone through the dhs call center at 1-855-697-4347. ri works (cash assistance) ri works is rhode island&#39;s cash assistance and employment program for parents and families with little to no income who have children high school age or younger. it pairs a monthly cash payment with job readiness, training, and child care support, and kinship guardians such as grandparents raising a child can apply too. apply online through the healthyrhode portal at healthyrhode.ri.gov or by phone through the dhs call center at 1-855-697-4347. rhode island earned income credit rhode island&#39;s earned income credit is a refundable credit worth 16 percent of your federal earned income tax credit for tax years 2024 and 2025, up from 15 percent in earlier years. because it is refundable, it can pay out as cash even when it is larger than the tax you owe. claim it on your rhode island income tax return; you generally must qualify for the federal eitc first. rhode island energy assistance (liheap) the low income home energy assistance program, liheap, helps income-eligible rhode island households with home heating and energy costs, and it runs through the ri department of human services. apply online through the healthyrhode portal at healthyrhode.ri.gov, by mail, or by phone through the dhs call center at 1-855-697-4347. weatherization is a separate program that fixes the home itself. rhode island legal services rhode island legal services provides free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing and eviction, family, public benefits, consumer, and elder law matters. call the office at 401-274-2652, or the toll-free line at 1-800-662-5034, to reach intake, or apply for help online. this is civil legal help only, not criminal defense. rhode island medicaid &amp; rite care rhode island medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. rite care is the managed care part that covers most families and children. rhode island expanded medicaid to cover low-income adults ages 19 to 64, so apply even if you were turned down before. apply online through the healthyrhode portal, or call healthsource ri at 1-855-840-4774. rhode island snap (food assistance) snap is rhode island&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. apply online through the healthyrhode portal at healthyrhode.ri.gov, the state&#39;s one-stop site that also handles medicaid, ri works cash, child care, and energy assistance. you can also apply by phone through the dhs call center at 1-855-697-4347 (1-855-my-ridhs) or by mail to the ri department of human services. rhode island wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. rhode island wic runs through the ri department of health and local clinics. to apply, call the ri department of health information line at 401-222-5960 to reach a local clinic and set up an appointment, or start at health.ri.gov/wic. unemployment insurance (rhode island) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the ri department of labor and training. file online through ui online at uionline.dlt.ri.gov, which is the fastest way, or call the call center at 401-415-6772. the call center is open monday, tuesday, and thursday from 8am to 3pm, friday from 9:30am to 3pm, and is closed wednesday. weatherization assistance program (rhode island) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible rhode island households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system improvements that lower future bills. the ri office of energy resources coordinates the program, and applications run through your local community action program agency, the same agencies that take energy assistance applications. call the office of energy resources at 401-574-9117 or contact your local community action program agency to start."><h2>Rhode Island programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/211-rhode-island-united-way-of-rhode-island/">211 Rhode Island (United Way of Rhode Island)</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Rhode Island phone, or call 401-519-0360, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, child care, healthcare, and more. 211 Rhode Island is run by United Way of Rhode Island and answers around the clock, every day of the year, with translation available in many languages. You can also chat with a resource specialist online.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/ri-child-care-assistance-program-ccap/">RI Child Care Assistance Program (CCAP)</a><p>The Child Care Assistance Program helps low- and moderate-income Rhode Island families pay for child care so parents can work or train for work. It covers children under 13, or up to 18 with special needs, at licensed and approved providers. Apply online through the HealthyRhode portal at healthyrhode.ri.gov or by phone through the DHS Call Center at 1-855-697-4347.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/ri-works-cash-assistance/">RI Works (Cash Assistance)</a><p>RI Works is Rhode Island&#39;s cash assistance and employment program for parents and families with little to no income who have children high school age or younger. It pairs a monthly cash payment with job readiness, training, and child care support, and kinship guardians such as grandparents raising a child can apply too. Apply online through the HealthyRhode portal at healthyrhode.ri.gov or by phone through the DHS Call Center at 1-855-697-4347.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/rhode-island-earned-income-credit/">Rhode Island Earned Income Credit</a><p>Rhode Island&#39;s Earned Income Credit is a refundable credit worth 16 percent of your federal Earned Income Tax Credit for tax years 2024 and 2025, up from 15 percent in earlier years. Because it is refundable, it can pay out as cash even when it is larger than the tax you owe. Claim it on your Rhode Island income tax return; you generally must qualify for the federal EITC first.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/rhode-island-energy-assistance-liheap/">Rhode Island Energy Assistance (LIHEAP)</a><p>The Low Income Home Energy Assistance Program, LIHEAP, helps income-eligible Rhode Island households with home heating and energy costs, and it runs through the RI Department of Human Services. Apply online through the HealthyRhode portal at healthyrhode.ri.gov, by mail, or by phone through the DHS Call Center at 1-855-697-4347. Weatherization is a separate program that fixes the home itself.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/rhode-island-legal-services/">Rhode Island Legal Services</a><p>Rhode Island Legal Services provides free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing and eviction, family, public benefits, consumer, and elder law matters. Call the office at 401-274-2652, or the toll-free line at 1-800-662-5034, to reach intake, or apply for help online. This is civil legal help only, not criminal defense.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/rhode-island-medicaid-and-rite-care/">Rhode Island Medicaid &amp; RIte Care</a><p>Rhode Island Medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. RIte Care is the managed care part that covers most families and children. Rhode Island expanded Medicaid to cover low-income adults ages 19 to 64, so apply even if you were turned down before. Apply online through the HealthyRhode portal, or call HealthSource RI at 1-855-840-4774.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/rhode-island-snap-food-assistance/">Rhode Island SNAP (Food Assistance)</a><p>SNAP is Rhode Island&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. Apply online through the HealthyRhode portal at healthyrhode.ri.gov, the state&#39;s one-stop site that also handles Medicaid, RI Works cash, child care, and energy assistance. You can also apply by phone through the DHS Call Center at 1-855-697-4347 (1-855-MY-RIDHS) or by mail to the RI Department of Human Services.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/rhode-island-wic/">Rhode Island WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. Rhode Island WIC runs through the RI Department of Health and local clinics. To apply, call the RI Department of Health Information Line at 401-222-5960 to reach a local clinic and set up an appointment, or start at health.ri.gov/wic.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-rhode-island/">Unemployment Insurance (Rhode Island)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the RI Department of Labor and Training. File online through UI Online at uionline.dlt.ri.gov, which is the fastest way, or call the call center at 401-415-6772. The call center is open Monday, Tuesday, and Thursday from 8am to 3pm, Friday from 9:30am to 3pm, and is closed Wednesday.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-rhode-island/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Rhode Island)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible Rhode Island households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system improvements that lower future bills. The RI Office of Energy Resources coordinates the program, and applications run through your local Community Action Program agency, the same agencies that take energy assistance applications. Call the Office of Energy Resources at 401-574-9117 or contact your local Community Action Program agency to start.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="south carolina healthy connections medicaid (south carolina) healthy connections is south carolina medicaid: free or low-cost health coverage run by the department of health and human services for children, pregnant people, low-income parents, older adults, and people with disabilities. south carolina has not expanded medicaid, so most adults without children do not qualify on low income alone. apply online at apply.scdhhs.gov, or call the member contact center at 1-888-549-0820, monday through friday, 8am to 6pm. liheap energy assistance (south carolina) the low income home energy assistance program, liheap, helps eligible south carolina households pay home heating and cooling bills and covers energy crises. the office of economic opportunity funds it, but you apply through the community action agency serving your county, not through a central office. find your local agency at oeo.sc.gov or dial 2-1-1. application periods are seasonal and funds are limited, so ask your agency for the current open dates. sc 211 dial 2-1-1 from any south carolina phone, or call 1-866-892-9211, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. sc 211 is operated by the united way association of south carolina and answers around the clock, every day. you can also text help or ayuda to 211-211, or search at sc211.org. sc thrive (free help applying for benefits) sc thrive is a nonprofit that helps south carolinians apply for benefits like snap and healthy connections medicaid over the phone, at no cost. call 1-800-726-8774 and choose option 1 to check what you may qualify for and apply with help. staff assist in english and spanish, so this is a good first call if the online forms feel like too much on your own. south carolina earned income tax credit south carolina has a state earned income tax credit worth 125 percent of your federal eitc, fully phased in since the 2023 tax year. it is nonrefundable, so it lowers the state income tax you owe but does not pay out as a refund beyond that. claim it with form tc-60 on your south carolina return. you generally must qualify for the federal eitc first. south carolina family independence (tanf cash assistance) family independence, south carolina&#39;s tanf program, gives monthly cash assistance to low-income families with children through the department of social services. most adults take part in a tanf work program run through sc works. apply online through the dss benefits portal at benefitsportal.dss.sc.gov, at your county dss office, or by mail or fax. call 1-800-616-1309 for a form or a case question. south carolina legal services south carolina legal services provides free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing and eviction, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. call the statewide intake line at 1-888-346-5592, or apply online at lawhelp.org/sc. a lawyer or paralegal screens your income and problem, and after you apply your local office usually contacts you within 5 to 7 business days. south carolina snap (food assistance) snap is south carolina&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. the department of social services runs it. apply online through the dss benefits portal at benefitsportal.dss.sc.gov, in person at your county dss office, or by mail or fax. to check a case or request a form, call the dss connect call center at 1-800-616-1309. south carolina wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. the south carolina department of public health runs it through local health departments. start with the online pre-application on the dph wic page, or call 1-855-472-3432 to make an in-person or phone appointment. unemployment insurance (south carolina dew) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the south carolina department of employment and workforce. file your claim online through the mybenefits portal at dew.sc.gov, which is the fastest way. for help, call 1-866-831-1724, or use relay 711. you must file a weekly claim to keep payments coming. weatherization assistance program (south carolina) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible south carolina households: insulation, air sealing, and heating and cooling improvements that lower future bills and make the home safer. the office of economic opportunity runs it through eight community action agencies that cover all 46 counties. contact the agency serving your county to apply; find yours at oeo.sc.gov. both homeowners and renters can qualify."><h2>South Carolina programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/healthy-connections-medicaid-south-carolina/">Healthy Connections Medicaid (South Carolina)</a><p>Healthy Connections is South Carolina Medicaid: free or low-cost health coverage run by the Department of Health and Human Services for children, pregnant people, low-income parents, older adults, and people with disabilities. South Carolina has not expanded Medicaid, so most adults without children do not qualify on low income alone. Apply online at apply.scdhhs.gov, or call the Member Contact Center at 1-888-549-0820, Monday through Friday, 8am to 6pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/liheap-energy-assistance-south-carolina/">LIHEAP Energy Assistance (South Carolina)</a><p>The Low Income Home Energy Assistance Program, LIHEAP, helps eligible South Carolina households pay home heating and cooling bills and covers energy crises. The Office of Economic Opportunity funds it, but you apply through the Community Action Agency serving your county, not through a central office. Find your local agency at oeo.sc.gov or dial 2-1-1. Application periods are seasonal and funds are limited, so ask your agency for the current open dates.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/sc-211/">SC 211</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any South Carolina phone, or call 1-866-892-9211, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. SC 211 is operated by the United Way Association of South Carolina and answers around the clock, every day. You can also text HELP or AYUDA to 211-211, or search at sc211.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/sc-thrive-free-help-applying-for-benefits/">SC Thrive (Free Help Applying for Benefits)</a><p>SC Thrive is a nonprofit that helps South Carolinians apply for benefits like SNAP and Healthy Connections Medicaid over the phone, at no cost. Call 1-800-726-8774 and choose Option 1 to check what you may qualify for and apply with help. Staff assist in English and Spanish, so this is a good first call if the online forms feel like too much on your own.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/south-carolina-earned-income-tax-credit/">South Carolina Earned Income Tax Credit</a><p>South Carolina has a state Earned Income Tax Credit worth 125 percent of your federal EITC, fully phased in since the 2023 tax year. It is nonrefundable, so it lowers the state income tax you owe but does not pay out as a refund beyond that. Claim it with form TC-60 on your South Carolina return. You generally must qualify for the federal EITC first.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/south-carolina-family-independence-tanf-cash-assistance/">South Carolina Family Independence (TANF Cash Assistance)</a><p>Family Independence, South Carolina&#39;s TANF program, gives monthly cash assistance to low-income families with children through the Department of Social Services. Most adults take part in a TANF work program run through SC Works. Apply online through the DSS Benefits Portal at benefitsportal.dss.sc.gov, at your county DSS office, or by mail or fax. Call 1-800-616-1309 for a form or a case question.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/south-carolina-legal-services/">South Carolina Legal Services</a><p>South Carolina Legal Services provides free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing and eviction, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. Call the statewide intake line at 1-888-346-5592, or apply online at lawhelp.org/sc. A lawyer or paralegal screens your income and problem, and after you apply your local office usually contacts you within 5 to 7 business days.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/south-carolina-snap-food-assistance/">South Carolina SNAP (Food Assistance)</a><p>SNAP is South Carolina&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. The Department of Social Services runs it. Apply online through the DSS Benefits Portal at benefitsportal.dss.sc.gov, in person at your county DSS office, or by mail or fax. To check a case or request a form, call the DSS Connect Call Center at 1-800-616-1309.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/south-carolina-wic/">South Carolina WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. The South Carolina Department of Public Health runs it through local health departments. Start with the online pre-application on the DPH WIC page, or call 1-855-472-3432 to make an in-person or phone appointment.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-south-carolina-dew/">Unemployment Insurance (South Carolina DEW)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the South Carolina Department of Employment and Workforce. File your claim online through the MyBenefits portal at dew.sc.gov, which is the fastest way. For help, call 1-866-831-1724, or use Relay 711. You must file a weekly claim to keep payments coming.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-south-carolina/">Weatherization Assistance Program (South Carolina)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible South Carolina households: insulation, air sealing, and heating and cooling improvements that lower future bills and make the home safer. The Office of Economic Opportunity runs it through eight Community Action Agencies that cover all 46 counties. Contact the agency serving your county to apply; find yours at oeo.sc.gov. Both homeowners and renters can qualify.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="south dakota 211 south dakota (helpline center) dial 2-1-1, or call 605-339-4357, to reach the helpline center for free, confidential help finding local assistance with food, housing, utilities, healthcare, and more across south dakota. the helpline center is a round-the-clock resource, and its specialists search for programs near you while you are on the line. you can also search at helplinecenter.org. reemployment assistance (south dakota unemployment) reemployment assistance is south dakota&#39;s unemployment program: temporary weekly payments if you lost your job through no fault of your own, run by the department of labor and regulation. file your claim online at sd.gov/rabenefits, which is the fastest way, or call the reemployment assistance line at 605-626-2452 for help. south dakota child care assistance the child care assistance program helps low-income working families in south dakota pay for child care. you qualify by meeting income guidelines and a minimum work or school requirement, and you may owe a small co-payment based on your income and family size. apply through your local department of social services office. call 1-800-227-3020 for help. south dakota housing (rental assistance) south dakota housing helps income-eligible renters through the housing choice voucher, also called section 8, which pays part of your monthly rent, plus security deposit assistance and other programs. there is no open pandemic-era emergency rental assistance program right now, so for a rent crisis dial 2-1-1 for local funds. call south dakota housing at 1-800-540-4241 to ask about vouchers and current waitlists. south dakota liheap (low income energy assistance) the low income energy assistance program, liheap, helps income-eligible south dakota households pay winter heating costs and can step in for a heating emergency. apply online through the state portal or with a paper application you email to dssheat@state.sd.us or mail to the office of energy assistance in pierre. call 1-800-233-8503 to apply or check your status, monday through friday, 8am to 5pm central. south dakota legal aid (east river &amp; dakota plains) south dakota&#39;s legal aid nonprofits provide free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing, family, consumer, public benefits, and other noncriminal matters. east river legal services covers eastern south dakota; call 1-800-952-3015. dakota plains legal services covers western south dakota and the tribal nations; call 605-856-4444. all three legal aid programs share one online application at sdlawhelp.org. south dakota medicaid &amp; chip south dakota medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, seniors, people with disabilities, and, since the state expanded medicaid in 2023, low-income adults ages 19 to 64. chip covers children in families that earn a little more. apply online through the dss benefits portal at eaportal.sd.gov, or call the department of social services at 605-773-3165. south dakota snap (food assistance) snap is south dakota&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. apply online through the dss benefits portal at eaportal.sd.gov, at your local department of social services office, or with a paper application you mail or drop off. for help, call the department of social services at 605-773-3165. south dakota tanf (cash assistance) temporary assistance for needy families, tanf, is south dakota&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children. most adults must take part in work activities, generally 30 hours a week, or 20 hours if you care for a child under 6. apply online at eaportal.sd.gov or at your local department of social services office. call 605-773-3165 with questions. south dakota wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. south dakota wic runs through the department of health. apply at sd.gov/wic, or call the department of health at 605-773-3361 to find your local wic clinic. weatherization assistance program (south dakota) the weatherization assistance program makes free energy-efficiency improvements for income-eligible south dakota households: weather-stripping and sealing, adding insulation in attics, walls, and under floors, and repairing or replacing a non-working heating system so future bills drop. it runs through local community action agencies alongside energy assistance. contact the office of energy assistance at 1-800-233-8503 to get started."><h2>South Dakota programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/211-south-dakota-helpline-center/">211 South Dakota (Helpline Center)</a><p>Dial 2-1-1, or call 605-339-4357, to reach the Helpline Center for free, confidential help finding local assistance with food, housing, utilities, healthcare, and more across South Dakota. The Helpline Center is a round-the-clock resource, and its specialists search for programs near you while you are on the line. You can also search at helplinecenter.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/reemployment-assistance-south-dakota-unemployment/">Reemployment Assistance (South Dakota Unemployment)</a><p>Reemployment Assistance is South Dakota&#39;s unemployment program: temporary weekly payments if you lost your job through no fault of your own, run by the Department of Labor and Regulation. File your claim online at sd.gov/rabenefits, which is the fastest way, or call the Reemployment Assistance line at 605-626-2452 for help.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/south-dakota-child-care-assistance/">South Dakota Child Care Assistance</a><p>The Child Care Assistance program helps low-income working families in South Dakota pay for child care. You qualify by meeting income guidelines and a minimum work or school requirement, and you may owe a small co-payment based on your income and family size. Apply through your local Department of Social Services office. Call 1-800-227-3020 for help.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/south-dakota-housing-rental-assistance/">South Dakota Housing (Rental Assistance)</a><p>South Dakota Housing helps income-eligible renters through the Housing Choice Voucher, also called Section 8, which pays part of your monthly rent, plus security deposit assistance and other programs. There is no open pandemic-era emergency rental assistance program right now, so for a rent crisis dial 2-1-1 for local funds. Call South Dakota Housing at 1-800-540-4241 to ask about vouchers and current waitlists.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/south-dakota-liheap-low-income-energy-assistance/">South Dakota LIHEAP (Low Income Energy Assistance)</a><p>The Low Income Energy Assistance Program, LIHEAP, helps income-eligible South Dakota households pay winter heating costs and can step in for a heating emergency. Apply online through the state portal or with a paper application you email to DSSHeat@state.sd.us or mail to the Office of Energy Assistance in Pierre. Call 1-800-233-8503 to apply or check your status, Monday through Friday, 8am to 5pm Central.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/south-dakota-legal-aid-east-river-and-dakota-plains/">South Dakota Legal Aid (East River &amp; Dakota Plains)</a><p>South Dakota&#39;s legal aid nonprofits provide free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing, family, consumer, public benefits, and other noncriminal matters. East River Legal Services covers eastern South Dakota; call 1-800-952-3015. Dakota Plains Legal Services covers western South Dakota and the tribal nations; call 605-856-4444. All three legal aid programs share one online application at sdlawhelp.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/south-dakota-medicaid-and-chip/">South Dakota Medicaid &amp; CHIP</a><p>South Dakota Medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, seniors, people with disabilities, and, since the state expanded Medicaid in 2023, low-income adults ages 19 to 64. CHIP covers children in families that earn a little more. Apply online through the DSS Benefits Portal at eaportal.sd.gov, or call the Department of Social Services at 605-773-3165.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/south-dakota-snap-food-assistance/">South Dakota SNAP (Food Assistance)</a><p>SNAP is South Dakota&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. Apply online through the DSS Benefits Portal at eaportal.sd.gov, at your local Department of Social Services office, or with a paper application you mail or drop off. For help, call the Department of Social Services at 605-773-3165.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/south-dakota-tanf-cash-assistance/">South Dakota TANF (Cash Assistance)</a><p>Temporary Assistance for Needy Families, TANF, is South Dakota&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children. Most adults must take part in work activities, generally 30 hours a week, or 20 hours if you care for a child under 6. Apply online at eaportal.sd.gov or at your local Department of Social Services office. Call 605-773-3165 with questions.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/south-dakota-wic/">South Dakota WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. South Dakota WIC runs through the Department of Health. Apply at sd.gov/wic, or call the Department of Health at 605-773-3361 to find your local WIC clinic.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-south-dakota/">Weatherization Assistance Program (South Dakota)</a><p>The Weatherization Assistance Program makes free energy-efficiency improvements for income-eligible South Dakota households: weather-stripping and sealing, adding insulation in attics, walls, and under floors, and repairing or replacing a non-working heating system so future bills drop. It runs through local community action agencies alongside energy assistance. Contact the Office of Energy Assistance at 1-800-233-8503 to get started.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="tennessee families first (tennessee tanf cash assistance) families first is tennessee&#39;s temporary assistance for needy families, tanf, program: monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children, paired with a personal responsibility plan that can include work, training, education, child care help, and transportation support. apply online through the one dhs customer portal at onedhs.tn.gov/csp or at your local family assistance office. you can also call 1-833-772-8347. liheap energy assistance (tennessee) liheap, the low-income home energy assistance program, helps low-income tennessee households pay home heating and cooling bills. the tennessee housing development agency, thda, runs it through a network of local community action agencies that reach all 95 counties. payments go straight to your utility company. apply online at thda.org/liheap or through the agency serving your county. tn 211 dial 2-1-1 from any tennessee phone for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utility bills, food, health care, and more. tn 211 is a statewide united way service that answers around the clock, every day of the year. you can also text your zip code to 898-211. details at uwtn.org/tn-211. tenncare (medicaid) &amp; coverkids (chip) tenncare is tennessee&#39;s medicaid program: free or low-cost health coverage for eligible children, pregnant women, low-income parents and caretakers, older adults, and people with disabilities. coverkids is tennessee&#39;s chip, covering children and pregnant women who earn a little too much for tenncare medicaid. apply online through tenncare connect at tenncareconnect.tn.gov, or call 1-855-259-0701. tennessee community action agencies (utility &amp; emergency help) tennessee&#39;s community action agencies help low-income households with utility bills, home energy costs, and other emergencies, and they deliver programs like liheap and weatherization at the local level. there are 19 agencies covering all 95 counties. find the one serving your county through the tennessee association of community action at tncommunityaction.org. tennessee legal aid (help4tn) tennessee&#39;s legal aid programs provide free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing and eviction, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. call the free help4tn legal advice line at 1-844-435-7486, which spells 1-844-help4tn, to be connected with a licensed tennessee attorney, or find self-help information and your local legal aid office at help4tn.org. tennessee snap (food assistance) snap is tennessee&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. apply online through the one dhs customer portal at onedhs.tn.gov/csp, the same portal that handles families first cash assistance, or at your local family assistance office. you can also call the one dhs contact center at 1-833-772-8347. tennessee wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum women, infants, and children up to age 5. tennessee wic runs through county health departments and local clinics. call 1-800-342-5942, which spells 1-800-dial-wic, to find your local clinic and start, or visit tn.gov/health/wic. unemployment insurance (tennessee) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the tennessee department of labor and workforce development. file online at jobs4tn.gov, which is the fastest way, or call the employment security line at 1-844-224-5818. weatherization assistance program (tennessee) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible tennessee households: attic and wall insulation, air and duct sealing, and heating and cooling repairs that lower future bills. the tennessee housing development agency runs the weatherization assistance program through local community agencies covering all 95 counties. contact the agency serving your county to apply, since thda does not take applications directly."><h2>Tennessee programs (10)</h2><article><a href="/programs/families-first-tennessee-tanf-cash-assistance/">Families First (Tennessee TANF Cash Assistance)</a><p>Families First is Tennessee&#39;s Temporary Assistance for Needy Families, TANF, program: monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children, paired with a Personal Responsibility Plan that can include work, training, education, child care help, and transportation support. Apply online through the One DHS Customer Portal at onedhs.tn.gov/csp or at your local Family Assistance office. You can also call 1-833-772-8347.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/liheap-energy-assistance-tennessee/">LIHEAP Energy Assistance (Tennessee)</a><p>LIHEAP, the Low-Income Home Energy Assistance Program, helps low-income Tennessee households pay home heating and cooling bills. The Tennessee Housing Development Agency, THDA, runs it through a network of local community action agencies that reach all 95 counties. Payments go straight to your utility company. Apply online at thda.org/liheap or through the agency serving your county.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/tn-211/">TN 211</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Tennessee phone for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utility bills, food, health care, and more. TN 211 is a statewide United Way service that answers around the clock, every day of the year. You can also text your ZIP code to 898-211. Details at uwtn.org/tn-211.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/tenncare-medicaid-and-coverkids-chip/">TennCare (Medicaid) &amp; CoverKids (CHIP)</a><p>TennCare is Tennessee&#39;s Medicaid program: free or low-cost health coverage for eligible children, pregnant women, low-income parents and caretakers, older adults, and people with disabilities. CoverKids is Tennessee&#39;s CHIP, covering children and pregnant women who earn a little too much for TennCare Medicaid. Apply online through TennCare Connect at tenncareconnect.tn.gov, or call 1-855-259-0701.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/tennessee-community-action-agencies-utility-and-emergency-help/">Tennessee Community Action Agencies (Utility &amp; Emergency Help)</a><p>Tennessee&#39;s community action agencies help low-income households with utility bills, home energy costs, and other emergencies, and they deliver programs like LIHEAP and weatherization at the local level. There are 19 agencies covering all 95 counties. Find the one serving your county through the Tennessee Association of Community Action at tncommunityaction.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/tennessee-legal-aid-help4tn/">Tennessee Legal Aid (HELP4TN)</a><p>Tennessee&#39;s legal aid programs provide free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing and eviction, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. Call the free HELP4TN legal advice line at 1-844-435-7486, which spells 1-844-HELP4TN, to be connected with a licensed Tennessee attorney, or find self-help information and your local legal aid office at help4tn.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/tennessee-snap-food-assistance/">Tennessee SNAP (Food Assistance)</a><p>SNAP is Tennessee&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. Apply online through the One DHS Customer Portal at onedhs.tn.gov/csp, the same portal that handles Families First cash assistance, or at your local Family Assistance office. You can also call the One DHS Contact Center at 1-833-772-8347.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/tennessee-wic/">Tennessee WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum women, infants, and children up to age 5. Tennessee WIC runs through county health departments and local clinics. Call 1-800-342-5942, which spells 1-800-DIAL-WIC, to find your local clinic and start, or visit tn.gov/health/wic.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-tennessee/">Unemployment Insurance (Tennessee)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Tennessee Department of Labor and Workforce Development. File online at Jobs4TN.gov, which is the fastest way, or call the Employment Security line at 1-844-224-5818.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-tennessee/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Tennessee)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible Tennessee households: attic and wall insulation, air and duct sealing, and heating and cooling repairs that lower future bills. The Tennessee Housing Development Agency runs the Weatherization Assistance Program through local community agencies covering all 95 counties. Contact the agency serving your county to apply, since THDA does not take applications directly.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="texas 2-1-1 texas texas&#39;s statewide helpline for finding local assistance of every kind: rent, utility bills, food, shelter, and healthcare. run by texas health and human services; dial 2-1-1 or call the toll-free line, with help in english and spanish. ceap (comprehensive energy assistance program) texas&#39;s liheap utility assistance: helps low-income households pay electric, gas, and propane bills, including in a shutoff crisis. local agencies in all 254 counties take applications; find yours through tdhca&#39;s help for texans page. child care services (twc scholarships) child care scholarships that pay most of the cost of care for low-income working texas families, run by the texas workforce commission through local workforce solutions offices. apply through childcare.texas.gov. healthy texas women free women&#39;s health services for uninsured texas women 15 to 44: annual exams, family planning, and screenings and treatment for conditions like diabetes and high blood pressure. use the site&#39;s find a doctor tool to locate a provider. snap food benefits (lone star card) texas snap puts monthly grocery money on a lone star card that works like a debit card at any store that accepts snap. apply online at yourtexasbenefits.com or call 2-1-1 for help. tanf cash help monthly cash payments for very-low-income texas families with children 18 and younger, paid on the lone star card. one-time tanf also gives a single crisis payment, and certain relatives raising a child can get a once-in-a-lifetime $1,000 payment. twc unemployment benefits temporary, partial income replacement if you lost your job or had hours cut through no fault of your own. apply online through unemployment benefit services or by phone at the twc tele-center. texas medicaid &amp; chip free or low-cost health coverage for low-income texas children, pregnant women, seniors, and people with disabilities. chip covers children in families that earn too much for medicaid; one application at yourtexasbenefits.com checks both. texas wic healthy foods, breastfeeding support, and nutrition help for pregnant women, new mothers, and children under 5 in texas. apply online or find a local wic office; benefits start at pregnancy and continue until a child turns 5. texaslawhelp.org (eviction &amp; legal help) free legal information, forms, and guides for texans, run by the nonprofit texas legal services center. the eviction section explains the notices, deadlines, and court steps, and connects you with legal aid. weatherization assistance program (tdhca) free home energy improvements for low-income texans: insulation, caulking, duct work, and repair or replacement of inefficient heating and cooling systems, so future bills shrink. apply through the weatherization agency that serves your county."><h2>Texas programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/2-1-1-texas/">2-1-1 Texas</a><p>Texas&#39;s statewide helpline for finding local assistance of every kind: rent, utility bills, food, shelter, and healthcare. Run by Texas Health and Human Services; dial 2-1-1 or call the toll-free line, with help in English and Spanish.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/ceap-comprehensive-energy-assistance-program/">CEAP (Comprehensive Energy Assistance Program)</a><p>Texas&#39;s LIHEAP utility assistance: helps low-income households pay electric, gas, and propane bills, including in a shutoff crisis. Local agencies in all 254 counties take applications; find yours through TDHCA&#39;s Help for Texans page.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/child-care-services-twc-scholarships/">Child Care Services (TWC Scholarships)</a><p>Child care scholarships that pay most of the cost of care for low-income working Texas families, run by the Texas Workforce Commission through local Workforce Solutions offices. Apply through childcare.texas.gov.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/healthy-texas-women/">Healthy Texas Women</a><p>Free women&#39;s health services for uninsured Texas women 15 to 44: annual exams, family planning, and screenings and treatment for conditions like diabetes and high blood pressure. Use the site&#39;s Find a Doctor tool to locate a provider.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/snap-food-benefits-lone-star-card/">SNAP Food Benefits (Lone Star Card)</a><p>Texas SNAP puts monthly grocery money on a Lone Star Card that works like a debit card at any store that accepts SNAP. Apply online at YourTexasBenefits.com or call 2-1-1 for help.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/tanf-cash-help/">TANF Cash Help</a><p>Monthly cash payments for very-low-income Texas families with children 18 and younger, paid on the Lone Star Card. One-Time TANF also gives a single crisis payment, and certain relatives raising a child can get a once-in-a-lifetime $1,000 payment.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/twc-unemployment-benefits/">TWC Unemployment Benefits</a><p>Temporary, partial income replacement if you lost your job or had hours cut through no fault of your own. Apply online through Unemployment Benefit Services or by phone at the TWC Tele-Center.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/texas-medicaid-and-chip/">Texas Medicaid &amp; CHIP</a><p>Free or low-cost health coverage for low-income Texas children, pregnant women, seniors, and people with disabilities. CHIP covers children in families that earn too much for Medicaid; one application at YourTexasBenefits.com checks both.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/texas-wic/">Texas WIC</a><p>Healthy foods, breastfeeding support, and nutrition help for pregnant women, new mothers, and children under 5 in Texas. Apply online or find a local WIC office; benefits start at pregnancy and continue until a child turns 5.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/texaslawhelp-org-eviction-and-legal-help/">TexasLawHelp.org (Eviction &amp; Legal Help)</a><p>Free legal information, forms, and guides for Texans, run by the nonprofit Texas Legal Services Center. The eviction section explains the notices, deadlines, and court steps, and connects you with legal aid.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-tdhca/">Weatherization Assistance Program (TDHCA)</a><p>Free home energy improvements for low-income Texans: insulation, caulking, duct work, and repair or replacement of inefficient heating and cooling systems, so future bills shrink. Apply through the weatherization agency that serves your county.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="utah 211 utah dial 2-1-1 from any utah phone, or call 1-888-826-9790, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. 211 utah answers by phone seven days a week, 8am to 6pm. you can also text your zip code to 801-845-2211 on weekdays or search at 211utah.org. family employment program (utah cash assistance) the family employment program, fep, is utah&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children, run by the department of workforce services. it pairs the cash with an employment plan and one-on-one help from a case manager to move you toward work. apply online through mycase at jobs.utah.gov/mycase. state and federal time limits apply to how long you can receive cash. unemployment insurance (utah) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the utah department of workforce services. file online at jobs.utah.gov/ui, which is the fastest way, or call the claims center. the statewide line for counties outside the wasatch front is 1-888-848-0688. you must file a weekly claim to keep payments coming. utah chip (children&#39;s health insurance) utah chip is low-cost health coverage for uninsured children and teens whose family earns too much for medicaid but still needs help paying for care. it covers checkups, doctor visits, dental, vision, and prescriptions. apply online through mycase at jobs.utah.gov/mycase, the same application used for medicaid, or call 1-877-543-7669, which spells 1-877-kids-now. utah earned income tax credit utah offers a state earned income tax credit worth 20 percent of your federal eitc. it is nonrefundable, which means it lowers the utah income tax you owe but does not pay out as a refund on its own, and you cannot carry any unused part to another year. claim it on your utah income tax return if you qualified for the federal eitc. utah heat (home energy assistance) heat is utah&#39;s home energy assistance program, the state&#39;s version of liheap, helping low-income households with heating and other home energy costs. priority households, those with someone age 60 or older, a person with a disability, or a child under 6, can apply starting october 1, and the general public can apply starting november 1, running through september 30 or until federal funds run out. contact your local heat office or call 801-526-9240. utah legal services &amp; timpanogos legal center utah legal services provides free civil legal help to low-income utahns on housing and eviction, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. call 801-328-8891 or apply online at utahlegalservices.org. in utah county, timpanogos legal center runs a free legal advice hotline for self-represented people at 801-649-8895, monday through friday, 9am to 2pm. utah medicaid utah medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. utah expanded medicaid in 2020 to cover adults with low income, so it is worth applying even if you were turned down before. apply online through mycase at jobs.utah.gov/mycase, or call the medicaid information line at 1-866-608-9422. utah snap (food assistance) snap is utah&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money loaded onto an ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. the easiest way to apply is online through mycase at jobs.utah.gov/mycase, the state&#39;s one application for food, medical, financial, and child care assistance. you can also apply at a department of workforce services employment center or by calling 1-866-435-7414. utah wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. to apply, call or text your local wic clinic to set up an appointment. for general information call 801-273-2991, or find your clinic and how to apply at wic.utah.gov. weatherization assistance program (utah) utah&#39;s weatherization assistance program makes free energy-efficiency improvements, insulation, air sealing, and heating-system repairs, so your utility bills drop over time. the division of housing and community development runs it through local agencies across the state, and you apply to the agency serving your county. households save an average of about 583 dollars a year, and you can ask for a weatherization referral when you apply for heat."><h2>Utah programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/211-utah/">211 Utah</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Utah phone, or call 1-888-826-9790, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. 211 Utah answers by phone seven days a week, 8am to 6pm. You can also text your zip code to 801-845-2211 on weekdays or search at 211utah.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/family-employment-program-utah-cash-assistance/">Family Employment Program (Utah Cash Assistance)</a><p>The Family Employment Program, FEP, is Utah&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children, run by the Department of Workforce Services. It pairs the cash with an employment plan and one-on-one help from a case manager to move you toward work. Apply online through myCase at jobs.utah.gov/mycase. State and federal time limits apply to how long you can receive cash.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-utah/">Unemployment Insurance (Utah)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Utah Department of Workforce Services. File online at jobs.utah.gov/ui, which is the fastest way, or call the claims center. The statewide line for counties outside the Wasatch Front is 1-888-848-0688. You must file a weekly claim to keep payments coming.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/utah-chip-childrens-health-insurance/">Utah CHIP (Children&#39;s Health Insurance)</a><p>Utah CHIP is low-cost health coverage for uninsured children and teens whose family earns too much for Medicaid but still needs help paying for care. It covers checkups, doctor visits, dental, vision, and prescriptions. Apply online through myCase at jobs.utah.gov/mycase, the same application used for Medicaid, or call 1-877-543-7669, which spells 1-877-KIDS-NOW.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/utah-earned-income-tax-credit/">Utah Earned Income Tax Credit</a><p>Utah offers a state Earned Income Tax Credit worth 20 percent of your federal EITC. It is nonrefundable, which means it lowers the Utah income tax you owe but does not pay out as a refund on its own, and you cannot carry any unused part to another year. Claim it on your Utah income tax return if you qualified for the federal EITC.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/utah-heat-home-energy-assistance/">Utah HEAT (Home Energy Assistance)</a><p>HEAT is Utah&#39;s home energy assistance program, the state&#39;s version of LIHEAP, helping low-income households with heating and other home energy costs. Priority households, those with someone age 60 or older, a person with a disability, or a child under 6, can apply starting October 1, and the general public can apply starting November 1, running through September 30 or until federal funds run out. Contact your local HEAT office or call 801-526-9240.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/utah-legal-services-and-timpanogos-legal-center/">Utah Legal Services &amp; Timpanogos Legal Center</a><p>Utah Legal Services provides free civil legal help to low-income Utahns on housing and eviction, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. Call 801-328-8891 or apply online at utahlegalservices.org. In Utah County, Timpanogos Legal Center runs a free legal advice hotline for self-represented people at 801-649-8895, Monday through Friday, 9am to 2pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/utah-medicaid/">Utah Medicaid</a><p>Utah Medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. Utah expanded Medicaid in 2020 to cover adults with low income, so it is worth applying even if you were turned down before. Apply online through myCase at jobs.utah.gov/mycase, or call the Medicaid information line at 1-866-608-9422.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/utah-snap-food-assistance/">Utah SNAP (Food Assistance)</a><p>SNAP is Utah&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money loaded onto an EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. The easiest way to apply is online through myCase at jobs.utah.gov/mycase, the state&#39;s one application for food, medical, financial, and child care assistance. You can also apply at a Department of Workforce Services employment center or by calling 1-866-435-7414.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/utah-wic/">Utah WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. To apply, call or text your local WIC clinic to set up an appointment. For general information call 801-273-2991, or find your clinic and how to apply at wic.utah.gov.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-utah/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Utah)</a><p>Utah&#39;s Weatherization Assistance Program makes free energy-efficiency improvements, insulation, air sealing, and heating-system repairs, so your utility bills drop over time. The Division of Housing and Community Development runs it through local agencies across the state, and you apply to the agency serving your county. Households save an average of about 583 dollars a year, and you can ask for a weatherization referral when you apply for HEAT.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="vermont 3squaresvt (snap food assistance) 3squaresvt is vermont&#39;s name for snap: monthly money for food, loaded onto a vermont ebt card you use like a debit card at many stores and farmers markets. apply online through the mybenefits portal, or submit a paper form 202 by mail, at a district office, or through the document uploader. call the benefits service center at 1-800-479-6151 to apply by phone or request a form. if everyone in the home is 65 or older or gets ssi, benefits come as cash to a bank account instead of an ebt card. crisis fuel assistance (vermont) crisis fuel assistance helps when you have a winter heating emergency, like running out of fuel with no money to buy more. it can buy your primary heating source, pay for electricity needed to run your heating system, and help set up payment plans or prevent a gas or electric disconnection. apply in person at your local community action agency, and call first so they can tell you what to bring. do not wait until you run out, because the program cannot help once fuel is already delivered or furnace work is done. green mountain care (vermont medicaid &amp; dr. dynasaur) green mountain care is the umbrella name for vermont&#39;s public health coverage, including medicaid and dr. dynasaur, which covers children under 19 and pregnant people. vermont expanded medicaid, so low-income adults under 65 can qualify too, alongside people 65 or older, blind, or disabled. apply for medicaid, dr. dynasaur, and other coverage through vermont health connect online or by calling 1-855-899-9600, monday through friday, 8am to 4:30pm. current members can reach the customer support center at 1-800-250-8427. reach up (vermont tanf cash assistance) reach up is vermont&#39;s monthly cash assistance for families with minor, dependent children, paired with case management to help you reach goals around work, education, housing, health, and more. apply online, in person at your local district office, or by calling 1-800-479-6151 to request a paper application. you take part in an interview by phone or in person. if the child&#39;s other parent lives elsewhere, a child support case usually opens automatically unless you request a safety waiver. seasonal fuel assistance (vermont liheap) seasonal fuel assistance is vermont&#39;s liheap program, and it pays a portion of your winter home heating bills whether you own or rent and whether you pay for heat directly or as part of your rent. apply online through the mybenefits portal, in person at your district office, or by calling 1-800-479-6151 to request a paper application. if you got fuel assistance last season, watch for the annual review form instead. once you are an active seasonal fuel household, you also qualify for free weatherization. unemployment insurance (vermont department of labor) vermont unemployment insurance gives short-term weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the vermont department of labor. file your initial claim online through the claimant e-services portal, which needs a myvermont account, or by phone at 1-800-479-6151. wait, file by calling 1-877-214-3330, monday through thursday 8:30am to 4pm and friday 9am to 4pm. for help on an existing claim, the claimant assistance line is 1-877-214-3332. a claim cannot start until you have actually separated from your job. vermont 211 dial 2-1-1 from any vermont phone, or call 1-866-652-4636 (or 802-652-4636), for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, health care, and more. vermont 211 is run by the united ways of vermont. you can also text your zip code to 898211. if you need emergency housing, call rather than text or email; emergency housing call hours are 4:30pm to 10pm on weekdays and 8am to 10pm on weekends and holidays. vermont earned income tax credit vermont&#39;s earned income tax credit is a refundable credit equal to 38 percent of your federal eitc, so it can pay out as cash even when it is larger than the tax you owe. you claim it on schedule in-112 with your vermont income tax return. in vermont, the credit is open to all qualifying residents regardless of whether you, your spouse, or a qualifying child has a social security number, so filers using an itin can claim it. free vita tax help can file your federal and vermont returns and claim both credits at no cost. vermont legal aid &amp; legal services vermont vermont legal aid and legal services vermont work together to give free civil legal help to low-income vermonters on housing and eviction, family, public benefits, consumer and debt, health care, and other noncriminal problems. legal services vermont screens all requests. the fastest way in is the online legal help request form, or leave a message on the legal helpline at 1-800-889-2047 (monday, wednesday, and friday 8am to 1pm; tuesday and thursday 12:30pm to 7pm). most people hear back within about 5 to 10 days. free self-help legal information is at vtlawhelp.org for everyone. vermont wic wic gives food benefits, nutrition education, breastfeeding support, and counseling to pregnant vermonters, new parents, and caregivers with children under 5. vermont wic runs through local health department offices, and food benefits load onto a card you use with the wicshopper app at approved stores. apply by contacting the vermont wic program at 1-800-464-4343 or 802-863-7200, by email at wic@vermont.gov, or through your local office. in-person and remote appointments are both available. weatherization assistance program (vermont) vermont&#39;s weatherization assistance program makes free energy-efficiency improvements so your home wastes less heat and your bills drop. on average the program makes about 10,000 dollars of improvements per home, adds around 1,500 square feet of insulation, and cuts drafts by about 40 percent. there is no statewide phone line; you apply through the community action agency serving your area, which are broc, capstone, cvoeo, neto, and sevca. download your agency&#39;s application, complete it, and mail the signed forms to the address on the application."><h2>Vermont programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/3squaresvt-snap-food-assistance/">3SquaresVT (SNAP Food Assistance)</a><p>3SquaresVT is Vermont&#39;s name for SNAP: monthly money for food, loaded onto a Vermont EBT card you use like a debit card at many stores and farmers markets. Apply online through the myBenefits portal, or submit a paper Form 202 by mail, at a district office, or through the document uploader. Call the Benefits Service Center at 1-800-479-6151 to apply by phone or request a form. If everyone in the home is 65 or older or gets SSI, benefits come as cash to a bank account instead of an EBT card.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/crisis-fuel-assistance-vermont/">Crisis Fuel Assistance (Vermont)</a><p>Crisis Fuel Assistance helps when you have a winter heating emergency, like running out of fuel with no money to buy more. It can buy your primary heating source, pay for electricity needed to run your heating system, and help set up payment plans or prevent a gas or electric disconnection. Apply in person at your local Community Action Agency, and call first so they can tell you what to bring. Do not wait until you run out, because the program cannot help once fuel is already delivered or furnace work is done.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/green-mountain-care-vermont-medicaid-and-dr-dynasaur/">Green Mountain Care (Vermont Medicaid &amp; Dr. Dynasaur)</a><p>Green Mountain Care is the umbrella name for Vermont&#39;s public health coverage, including Medicaid and Dr. Dynasaur, which covers children under 19 and pregnant people. Vermont expanded Medicaid, so low-income adults under 65 can qualify too, alongside people 65 or older, blind, or disabled. Apply for Medicaid, Dr. Dynasaur, and other coverage through Vermont Health Connect online or by calling 1-855-899-9600, Monday through Friday, 8am to 4:30pm. Current members can reach the Customer Support Center at 1-800-250-8427.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/reach-up-vermont-tanf-cash-assistance/">Reach Up (Vermont TANF Cash Assistance)</a><p>Reach Up is Vermont&#39;s monthly cash assistance for families with minor, dependent children, paired with case management to help you reach goals around work, education, housing, health, and more. Apply online, in person at your local district office, or by calling 1-800-479-6151 to request a paper application. You take part in an interview by phone or in person. If the child&#39;s other parent lives elsewhere, a child support case usually opens automatically unless you request a safety waiver.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/seasonal-fuel-assistance-vermont-liheap/">Seasonal Fuel Assistance (Vermont LIHEAP)</a><p>Seasonal Fuel Assistance is Vermont&#39;s LIHEAP program, and it pays a portion of your winter home heating bills whether you own or rent and whether you pay for heat directly or as part of your rent. Apply online through the myBenefits portal, in person at your district office, or by calling 1-800-479-6151 to request a paper application. If you got Fuel Assistance last season, watch for the annual review form instead. Once you are an active Seasonal Fuel household, you also qualify for free weatherization.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-vermont-department-of-labor/">Unemployment Insurance (Vermont Department of Labor)</a><p>Vermont unemployment insurance gives short-term weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Vermont Department of Labor. File your initial claim online through the Claimant e-Services portal, which needs a myVermont account, or by phone at 1-800-479-6151. Wait, file by calling 1-877-214-3330, Monday through Thursday 8:30am to 4pm and Friday 9am to 4pm. For help on an existing claim, the Claimant Assistance line is 1-877-214-3332. A claim cannot start until you have actually separated from your job.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/vermont-211/">Vermont 211</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Vermont phone, or call 1-866-652-4636 (or 802-652-4636), for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, health care, and more. Vermont 211 is run by the United Ways of Vermont. You can also text your ZIP code to 898211. If you need emergency housing, call rather than text or email; emergency housing call hours are 4:30pm to 10pm on weekdays and 8am to 10pm on weekends and holidays.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/vermont-earned-income-tax-credit/">Vermont Earned Income Tax Credit</a><p>Vermont&#39;s Earned Income Tax Credit is a refundable credit equal to 38 percent of your federal EITC, so it can pay out as cash even when it is larger than the tax you owe. You claim it on Schedule IN-112 with your Vermont income tax return. In Vermont, the credit is open to all qualifying residents regardless of whether you, your spouse, or a qualifying child has a Social Security number, so filers using an ITIN can claim it. Free VITA tax help can file your federal and Vermont returns and claim both credits at no cost.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/vermont-legal-aid-and-legal-services-vermont/">Vermont Legal Aid &amp; Legal Services Vermont</a><p>Vermont Legal Aid and Legal Services Vermont work together to give free civil legal help to low-income Vermonters on housing and eviction, family, public benefits, consumer and debt, health care, and other noncriminal problems. Legal Services Vermont screens all requests. The fastest way in is the online Legal Help Request Form, or leave a message on the legal helpline at 1-800-889-2047 (Monday, Wednesday, and Friday 8am to 1pm; Tuesday and Thursday 12:30pm to 7pm). Most people hear back within about 5 to 10 days. Free self-help legal information is at vtlawhelp.org for everyone.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/vermont-wic/">Vermont WIC</a><p>WIC gives food benefits, nutrition education, breastfeeding support, and counseling to pregnant Vermonters, new parents, and caregivers with children under 5. Vermont WIC runs through local Health Department offices, and food benefits load onto a card you use with the WICShopper app at approved stores. Apply by contacting the Vermont WIC Program at 1-800-464-4343 or 802-863-7200, by email at wic@vermont.gov, or through your local office. In-person and remote appointments are both available.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-vermont/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Vermont)</a><p>Vermont&#39;s Weatherization Assistance Program makes free energy-efficiency improvements so your home wastes less heat and your bills drop. On average the program makes about 10,000 dollars of improvements per home, adds around 1,500 square feet of insulation, and cuts drafts by about 40 percent. There is no statewide phone line; you apply through the Community Action Agency serving your area, which are BROC, Capstone, CVOEO, NETO, and SEVCA. Download your agency&#39;s application, complete it, and mail the signed forms to the address on the application.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="virginia 211 virginia dial 2-1-1 from any virginia phone, or call 1-800-230-6977, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. 211 virginia is a statewide service that answers around the clock, every day. you can also text the keyword connect to 247211 or search at 211virginia.org. unemployment insurance (virginia) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the virginia employment commission. file online through the vec customer self-service portal at vec.virginia.gov, which is the fastest way, or by phone through the customer contact center at 1-866-832-2363, monday through friday, 8am to 4:30pm. virginia earned income tax credit virginia offers two versions of the earned income tax credit, and you pick the one that helps most. one is nonrefundable and equal to 20 percent of your federal eitc. the other is refundable and, for tax years 2025 and 2026, also equal to 20 percent of your federal eitc, so it pays out even if you owe no tax. claim it on your virginia return. virginia energy assistance program (fuel, crisis &amp; cooling) the energy assistance program helps low-income virginia households with home energy costs through three seasonal parts: fuel assistance for heating, crisis assistance for a heating emergency, and cooling assistance for summer. apply online through commonhelp, at your local department of social services, or by phone at 1-855-635-4370, monday through friday, 7am to 6pm. virginia legal aid (valegalaid / virginia law help) virginia&#39;s legal aid offices provide free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. call the statewide line at 1-866-534-5243 (1-866-leglaid) to reach the legal aid office serving your area, or find free legal information and your local office at virginialawhelp.org. virginia medicaid (cardinal care) &amp; famis virginia medicaid, known as cardinal care, is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. famis covers children through chip. apply online through commonhelp, or call the cover virginia call center at 1-855-242-8282, monday through friday, 8am to 7pm. virginia snap (food assistance) snap is virginia&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. apply online through commonhelp at commonhelp.virginia.gov, which handles snap, medicaid, tanf, and energy assistance on one application, or at your local department of social services. you can also apply by phone at 1-855-635-4370. virginia tanf (cash assistance) temporary assistance for needy families, tanf, is virginia&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children. most adults take part in the virginia initiative for education and work, view, which helps you move toward a job. apply online through commonhelp at commonhelp.virginia.gov or at your local department of social services. virginia wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. virginia wic runs through local health districts of the virginia department of health. apply online at myvawic.org, or call 1-877-835-5942 for more information about wic. weatherization assistance program (virginia) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible virginia households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system improvements that lower future bills. the department of housing and community development runs the program through local providers. contact the weatherization provider serving your area to apply, since dhcd does not take applications directly."><h2>Virginia programs (10)</h2><article><a href="/programs/211-virginia/">211 Virginia</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Virginia phone, or call 1-800-230-6977, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. 211 Virginia is a statewide service that answers around the clock, every day. You can also text the keyword CONNECT to 247211 or search at 211virginia.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-virginia/">Unemployment Insurance (Virginia)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Virginia Employment Commission. File online through the VEC Customer Self-Service portal at vec.virginia.gov, which is the fastest way, or by phone through the Customer Contact Center at 1-866-832-2363, Monday through Friday, 8am to 4:30pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/virginia-earned-income-tax-credit/">Virginia Earned Income Tax Credit</a><p>Virginia offers two versions of the Earned Income Tax Credit, and you pick the one that helps most. One is nonrefundable and equal to 20 percent of your federal EITC. The other is refundable and, for tax years 2025 and 2026, also equal to 20 percent of your federal EITC, so it pays out even if you owe no tax. Claim it on your Virginia return.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/virginia-energy-assistance-program-fuel-crisis-and-cooling/">Virginia Energy Assistance Program (Fuel, Crisis &amp; Cooling)</a><p>The Energy Assistance Program helps low-income Virginia households with home energy costs through three seasonal parts: Fuel Assistance for heating, Crisis Assistance for a heating emergency, and Cooling Assistance for summer. Apply online through CommonHelp, at your local department of social services, or by phone at 1-855-635-4370, Monday through Friday, 7am to 6pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/virginia-legal-aid-valegalaid-virginia-law-help/">Virginia Legal Aid (VaLegalAid / Virginia Law Help)</a><p>Virginia&#39;s legal aid offices provide free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. Call the statewide line at 1-866-534-5243 (1-866-LEGLAID) to reach the legal aid office serving your area, or find free legal information and your local office at virginialawhelp.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/virginia-medicaid-cardinal-care-and-famis/">Virginia Medicaid (Cardinal Care) &amp; FAMIS</a><p>Virginia Medicaid, known as Cardinal Care, is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. FAMIS covers children through CHIP. Apply online through CommonHelp, or call the Cover Virginia Call Center at 1-855-242-8282, Monday through Friday, 8am to 7pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/virginia-snap-food-assistance/">Virginia SNAP (Food Assistance)</a><p>SNAP is Virginia&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. Apply online through CommonHelp at commonhelp.virginia.gov, which handles SNAP, Medicaid, TANF, and energy assistance on one application, or at your local department of social services. You can also apply by phone at 1-855-635-4370.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/virginia-tanf-cash-assistance/">Virginia TANF (Cash Assistance)</a><p>Temporary Assistance for Needy Families, TANF, is Virginia&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children. Most adults take part in the Virginia Initiative for Education and Work, VIEW, which helps you move toward a job. Apply online through CommonHelp at commonhelp.virginia.gov or at your local department of social services.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/virginia-wic/">Virginia WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. Virginia WIC runs through local health districts of the Virginia Department of Health. Apply online at myvawic.org, or call 1-877-835-5942 for more information about WIC.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-virginia/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Virginia)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible Virginia households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system improvements that lower future bills. The Department of Housing and Community Development runs the program through local providers. Contact the weatherization provider serving your area to apply, since DHCD does not take applications directly.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="washington aged, blind, or disabled (abd) cash assistance abd is a washington cash program from dshs for low-income adults who are 65 or older, blind, or likely to meet the social security disability standard. it pays a monthly cash grant while helping you apply for federal ssi, and it can connect you to the housing and essential needs (hen) program. apply at washingtonconnection.org or by phone at 1-877-501-2233. unemployment benefits (washington esd) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the washington employment security department. file online through eservices at esd.wa.gov, which is the fastest way and open around the clock, or call the claims center at 1-800-318-6022. after you apply, you must file a weekly claim for each week to be paid. washington 211 dial 2-1-1 from any washington phone, or call 1-877-211-9274, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. washington 211 is a statewide service with a database of thousands of local resources. you can also text 211waod to 898211 or search at wa211.org. washington apple health (medicaid) apple health is washington&#39;s medicaid program: free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. apply online at wahealthplanfinder.org, or call the washington healthplanfinder customer support center at 1-855-923-4633. for coverage questions, the health care authority line is 1-800-562-3022. washington basic food (snap) basic food is washington&#39;s name for snap: monthly food money on an ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. apply online at washingtonconnection.org, which handles basic food, cash assistance, and other help on one application, or by phone at 1-877-501-2233, monday through friday. you can also apply at your local dshs community services office. washington energy assistance (liheap) liheap helps low-income washington households pay home heating and energy bills. the state department of commerce funds the program, but you apply through the local community action agency that serves your county, not through commerce directly. each agency sets its own appointment process, so find your local provider on the commerce liheap page and contact them to apply. washington legal aid (clear / northwest justice project) clear is washington&#39;s toll-free legal aid intake line, run by the northwest justice project, for low-income residents with civil, noncriminal problems like eviction, housing, family, and public benefits. call 1-888-201-1014, monday through friday, 9:15am to 12:15pm. residents 60 and older can call clear*sr at 1-888-387-7111. free self-help guides are at washingtonlawhelp.org. washington tanf / workfirst (cash assistance) temporary assistance for needy families, tanf, is washington&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children. most adults take part in workfirst, the state&#39;s employment program, while the cash steadies the household. apply online at washingtonconnection.org or by phone at 1-877-501-2233, or at your local dshs community services office. washington wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. washington wic runs through the department of health and local clinics. find a clinic or apply by calling the wic cascades support line at 1-800-841-1410, or start at doh.wa.gov. washington working families tax credit the working families tax credit is washington&#39;s refundable cash credit, run by the department of revenue. for the 2025 credit it pays up to $335 with no qualifying children, $660 with one child, $995 with two, and $1,330 with three or more, with a $50 minimum for those who qualify. apply for free at workingfamiliescredit.wa.gov after you file your federal return. weatherization assistance program (washington) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible washington households: insulation, air sealing, ventilation, and related repairs that lower future bills. the department of commerce funds the work through local agencies. both homeowners and renters can qualify. contact the weatherization agency serving your county to apply, since commerce does not take applications directly."><h2>Washington programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/aged-blind-or-disabled-abd-cash-assistance/">Aged, Blind, or Disabled (ABD) Cash Assistance</a><p>ABD is a Washington cash program from DSHS for low-income adults who are 65 or older, blind, or likely to meet the Social Security disability standard. It pays a monthly cash grant while helping you apply for federal SSI, and it can connect you to the Housing and Essential Needs (HEN) program. Apply at washingtonconnection.org or by phone at 1-877-501-2233.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-benefits-washington-esd/">Unemployment Benefits (Washington ESD)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Washington Employment Security Department. File online through eServices at esd.wa.gov, which is the fastest way and open around the clock, or call the claims center at 1-800-318-6022. After you apply, you must file a weekly claim for each week to be paid.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/washington-211/">Washington 211</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Washington phone, or call 1-877-211-9274, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. Washington 211 is a statewide service with a database of thousands of local resources. You can also text 211WAOD to 898211 or search at wa211.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/washington-apple-health-medicaid/">Washington Apple Health (Medicaid)</a><p>Apple Health is Washington&#39;s Medicaid program: free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. Apply online at wahealthplanfinder.org, or call the Washington Healthplanfinder Customer Support Center at 1-855-923-4633. For coverage questions, the Health Care Authority line is 1-800-562-3022.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/washington-basic-food-snap/">Washington Basic Food (SNAP)</a><p>Basic Food is Washington&#39;s name for SNAP: monthly food money on an EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. Apply online at washingtonconnection.org, which handles Basic Food, cash assistance, and other help on one application, or by phone at 1-877-501-2233, Monday through Friday. You can also apply at your local DSHS Community Services Office.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/washington-energy-assistance-liheap/">Washington Energy Assistance (LIHEAP)</a><p>LIHEAP helps low-income Washington households pay home heating and energy bills. The state Department of Commerce funds the program, but you apply through the local community action agency that serves your county, not through Commerce directly. Each agency sets its own appointment process, so find your local provider on the Commerce LIHEAP page and contact them to apply.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/washington-legal-aid-clear-northwest-justice-project/">Washington Legal Aid (CLEAR / Northwest Justice Project)</a><p>CLEAR is Washington&#39;s toll-free legal aid intake line, run by the Northwest Justice Project, for low-income residents with civil, noncriminal problems like eviction, housing, family, and public benefits. Call 1-888-201-1014, Monday through Friday, 9:15am to 12:15pm. Residents 60 and older can call CLEAR*Sr at 1-888-387-7111. Free self-help guides are at washingtonlawhelp.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/washington-tanf-workfirst-cash-assistance/">Washington TANF / WorkFirst (Cash Assistance)</a><p>Temporary Assistance for Needy Families, TANF, is Washington&#39;s monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children. Most adults take part in WorkFirst, the state&#39;s employment program, while the cash steadies the household. Apply online at washingtonconnection.org or by phone at 1-877-501-2233, or at your local DSHS Community Services Office.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/washington-wic/">Washington WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. Washington WIC runs through the Department of Health and local clinics. Find a clinic or apply by calling the WIC Cascades Support Line at 1-800-841-1410, or start at doh.wa.gov.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/washington-working-families-tax-credit/">Washington Working Families Tax Credit</a><p>The Working Families Tax Credit is Washington&#39;s refundable cash credit, run by the Department of Revenue. For the 2025 credit it pays up to $335 with no qualifying children, $660 with one child, $995 with two, and $1,330 with three or more, with a $50 minimum for those who qualify. Apply for free at workingfamiliescredit.wa.gov after you file your federal return.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-washington/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Washington)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible Washington households: insulation, air sealing, ventilation, and related repairs that lower future bills. The Department of Commerce funds the work through local agencies. Both homeowners and renters can qualify. Contact the weatherization agency serving your county to apply, since Commerce does not take applications directly.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="west virginia lieap (low income energy assistance program) lieap helps low-income west virginia households pay winter heating costs, either as a payment to your utility company or a direct payment. an emergency lieap component helps households without resources facing the loss of a heating source. apply online through wvpath at wvpath.wv.gov, at your local department of human services field office, or through a local community action agency or area agency on aging. legal aid of west virginia legal aid of west virginia gives free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing and eviction, family law, public benefits, consumer and debt problems, and more. call the statewide intake line at 1-866-255-4370 to reach the office serving your area, or apply for help online at legalaidwv.org. this is civil, noncriminal help. unemployment insurance (workforce west virginia) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from workforce west virginia. file online through the workforce west virginia claims portal at uc.workforcewv.org, which is the fastest way, or get help by phone at 1-800-252-5627 (1-800-252-jobs). you must file a claim each week to keep payments coming. wv chip (children&#39;s health insurance program) wv chip covers children and pregnant women in working families who have no other health insurance and earn too much for medicaid. it covers checkups, dental, vision, prescriptions, and more with low or no cost to the family. apply through wvpath along with medicaid at wvpath.wv.gov, and call the wvchip helpline at 1-877-982-2447 (1-877-wva-chip) with questions. wv works (tanf cash assistance) wv works is west virginia&#39;s temporary assistance for needy families program, giving monthly cash assistance to eligible families with children. it pairs the cash with work and education activities like job skills training, vocational training, and college. apply online through wvpath at wvpath.wv.gov or at your local department of human services field office. most families on wv works also receive snap and medicaid. weatherization assistance program (west virginia) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible west virginia households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system improvements that lower future bills. the work is delivered by local community action agencies. call the west virginia community action partnership at 304-347-2277 or use its agency locator to reach the weatherization provider serving your county, since you apply through that local agency. west virginia 211 dial 2-1-1 from any west virginia phone, or call 1-833-848-9905, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. west virginia 211 answers 24 hours a day, 7 days a week, every day of the year. you can also text your zip code to 898-211 or search at search.wv211.org. west virginia medicaid west virginia medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. west virginia expanded medicaid, so adults 19 to 64 with low income can qualify on their own. apply online through wvpath at wvpath.wv.gov, at a local department of human services field office, or by calling the dohs customer service center at 1-877-716-1212. west virginia snap (food assistance) snap is west virginia&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. apply online through wvpath at wvpath.wv.gov, the state&#39;s one application for snap, wv works, and medicaid, at your local department of human services field office, or by phone through the dohs customer service center at 1-877-716-1212. west virginia wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. west virginia wic runs through local clinics tied to county health departments. check the wic eligibility screening tool and find your nearest clinic through the state wic clinic search, then call the clinic to book your first appointment."><h2>West Virginia programs (10)</h2><article><a href="/programs/lieap-low-income-energy-assistance-program/">LIEAP (Low Income Energy Assistance Program)</a><p>LIEAP helps low-income West Virginia households pay winter heating costs, either as a payment to your utility company or a direct payment. An Emergency LIEAP component helps households without resources facing the loss of a heating source. Apply online through wvPATH at wvpath.wv.gov, at your local Department of Human Services field office, or through a local Community Action agency or Area Agency on Aging.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/legal-aid-of-west-virginia/">Legal Aid of West Virginia</a><p>Legal Aid of West Virginia gives free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing and eviction, family law, public benefits, consumer and debt problems, and more. Call the statewide intake line at 1-866-255-4370 to reach the office serving your area, or apply for help online at legalaidwv.org. This is civil, noncriminal help.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-workforce-west-virginia/">Unemployment Insurance (WorkForce West Virginia)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from WorkForce West Virginia. File online through the WorkForce West Virginia claims portal at uc.workforcewv.org, which is the fastest way, or get help by phone at 1-800-252-5627 (1-800-252-JOBS). You must file a claim each week to keep payments coming.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/wv-chip-childrens-health-insurance-program/">WV CHIP (Children&#39;s Health Insurance Program)</a><p>WV CHIP covers children and pregnant women in working families who have no other health insurance and earn too much for Medicaid. It covers checkups, dental, vision, prescriptions, and more with low or no cost to the family. Apply through wvPATH along with Medicaid at wvpath.wv.gov, and call the WVCHIP Helpline at 1-877-982-2447 (1-877-WVA-CHIP) with questions.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/wv-works-tanf-cash-assistance/">WV WORKS (TANF Cash Assistance)</a><p>WV WORKS is West Virginia&#39;s Temporary Assistance for Needy Families program, giving monthly cash assistance to eligible families with children. It pairs the cash with work and education activities like job skills training, vocational training, and college. Apply online through wvPATH at wvpath.wv.gov or at your local Department of Human Services field office. Most families on WV WORKS also receive SNAP and Medicaid.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-west-virginia/">Weatherization Assistance Program (West Virginia)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible West Virginia households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system improvements that lower future bills. The work is delivered by local Community Action agencies. Call the West Virginia Community Action Partnership at 304-347-2277 or use its agency locator to reach the weatherization provider serving your county, since you apply through that local agency.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/west-virginia-211/">West Virginia 211</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any West Virginia phone, or call 1-833-848-9905, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. West Virginia 211 answers 24 hours a day, 7 days a week, every day of the year. You can also text your zip code to 898-211 or search at search.wv211.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/west-virginia-medicaid/">West Virginia Medicaid</a><p>West Virginia Medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, older adults, people with disabilities, and low-income adults. West Virginia expanded Medicaid, so adults 19 to 64 with low income can qualify on their own. Apply online through wvPATH at wvpath.wv.gov, at a local Department of Human Services field office, or by calling the DoHS Customer Service Center at 1-877-716-1212.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/west-virginia-snap-food-assistance/">West Virginia SNAP (Food Assistance)</a><p>SNAP is West Virginia&#39;s food assistance program: monthly food money on an EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. Apply online through wvPATH at wvpath.wv.gov, the state&#39;s one application for SNAP, WV WORKS, and Medicaid, at your local Department of Human Services field office, or by phone through the DoHS Customer Service Center at 1-877-716-1212.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/west-virginia-wic/">West Virginia WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. West Virginia WIC runs through local clinics tied to county health departments. Check the WIC eligibility screening tool and find your nearest clinic through the state WIC clinic search, then call the clinic to book your first appointment.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="wisconsin 211 wisconsin dial 2-1-1 from any wisconsin phone, or call 1-877-947-2211, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. 211 wisconsin answers around the clock, every day, and connects you to programs near you. you can also search at 211wisconsin.communityos.org. badgercare plus (wisconsin medicaid) badgercare plus is wisconsin&#39;s largest medicaid program: free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, and other adults with low income, ages 0 to 64. it covers preventive care, doctor visits, prescriptions, and emergencies. apply online through access at access.wisconsin.gov, or through your local county or tribal agency. call forwardhealth member services at 1-800-362-3002, monday through friday, 8am to 6pm. foodshare wisconsin (snap) foodshare is wisconsin&#39;s snap program: monthly food money on a quest ebt card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. apply online through access at access.wisconsin.gov, which handles foodshare, badgercare plus, and other programs on one application, or through your local county or tribal agency. you can also manage your case with the myaccess app. for help, call forwardhealth member services at 1-800-362-3002, monday through friday, 8am to 6pm. legal action of wisconsin (free civil legal aid) legal action of wisconsin provides free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. as of january 1, 2026 it merged with wisconsin judicare into a single statewide firm that also serves northern wisconsin and tribal communities. call the intake line at 1-855-947-2529, weekdays 9am to 4pm, or apply online any time at legalaction.org. unemployment insurance (wisconsin) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the wisconsin department of workforce development. file online through the claimant portal at my.unemployment.wisconsin.gov, which is the fastest way. for help using online services, call 1-844-910-3661 (or 414-435-7069): monday, tuesday, wednesday, and friday, 7:30am to 4:30pm, and thursday, 8am to 3:15pm. weatherization assistance program (wisconsin) free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible wisconsin households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system improvements that lower future bills. the division of energy, housing and community resources runs the program through local agencies. apply online at energybenefit.wi.gov or through your local agency, and call the customer care center at 1-800-506-5596 with questions. wisconsin earned income credit wisconsin&#39;s earned income credit is a refundable credit for working families with at least one qualifying child, worth a percentage of your federal eitc: 4 percent with one child, 11 percent with two children, and 34 percent with three or more children. it is refundable, so it can pay out even if you owe no state tax. claim it on your wisconsin income tax return (form 1 or 1npr). wisconsin home energy assistance program (wheap) wheap is wisconsin&#39;s liheap program: help with heating and electric costs, plus emergency (crisis) assistance and furnace repair or replacement for income-eligible households. apply online at energybenefit.wi.gov, or contact the local energy assistance agency serving your county or tribe. for more information, call 1-866-432-8947. the main heating benefit runs october 1 through may 15 each year. wisconsin homestead credit the homestead credit is a refundable wisconsin credit that helps renters and homeowners with low income offset the property taxes or rent they pay. for 2025, household income must be under $24,680 to qualify (people with income at or above that must be disabled or age 62 or older). you claim it on schedule h or h-ez with your wisconsin return, and it can come back as a refund even if you owe no tax. wisconsin wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. wisconsin wic runs through local wic agencies. start your pre-application online, or call the well badger resource center at 1-800-642-7837 to find your local clinic, monday through friday, 7am to 6pm. wisconsin works (w-2 cash assistance) wisconsin works, called w-2, is the state&#39;s tanf program: monthly cash assistance and employment services for parents of minor children and pregnant women with low income. the department of children and families runs it through local w-2 agencies, and most participants take part in work activities. apply online through access at access.wisconsin.gov or at your local w-2 agency."><h2>Wisconsin programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/211-wisconsin/">211 Wisconsin</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Wisconsin phone, or call 1-877-947-2211, for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. 211 Wisconsin answers around the clock, every day, and connects you to programs near you. You can also search at 211wisconsin.communityos.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/badgercare-plus-wisconsin-medicaid/">BadgerCare Plus (Wisconsin Medicaid)</a><p>BadgerCare Plus is Wisconsin&#39;s largest Medicaid program: free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents, and other adults with low income, ages 0 to 64. It covers preventive care, doctor visits, prescriptions, and emergencies. Apply online through ACCESS at access.wisconsin.gov, or through your local county or tribal agency. Call ForwardHealth Member Services at 1-800-362-3002, Monday through Friday, 8am to 6pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/foodshare-wisconsin-snap/">FoodShare Wisconsin (SNAP)</a><p>FoodShare is Wisconsin&#39;s SNAP program: monthly food money on a QUEST EBT card you use like a debit card at most grocery stores. Apply online through ACCESS at access.wisconsin.gov, which handles FoodShare, BadgerCare Plus, and other programs on one application, or through your local county or tribal agency. You can also manage your case with the MyACCESS app. For help, call ForwardHealth Member Services at 1-800-362-3002, Monday through Friday, 8am to 6pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/legal-action-of-wisconsin-free-civil-legal-aid/">Legal Action of Wisconsin (Free Civil Legal Aid)</a><p>Legal Action of Wisconsin provides free civil legal help to low-income residents on housing, family, public benefits, consumer, and other noncriminal matters. As of January 1, 2026 it merged with Wisconsin Judicare into a single statewide firm that also serves northern Wisconsin and tribal communities. Call the intake line at 1-855-947-2529, weekdays 9am to 4pm, or apply online any time at legalaction.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-wisconsin/">Unemployment Insurance (Wisconsin)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, from the Wisconsin Department of Workforce Development. File online through the Claimant Portal at my.unemployment.wisconsin.gov, which is the fastest way. For help using online services, call 1-844-910-3661 (or 414-435-7069): Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, and Friday, 7:30am to 4:30pm, and Thursday, 8am to 3:15pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-wisconsin/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Wisconsin)</a><p>Free home energy-efficiency work for income-eligible Wisconsin households: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system improvements that lower future bills. The Division of Energy, Housing and Community Resources runs the program through local agencies. Apply online at energybenefit.wi.gov or through your local agency, and call the Customer Care Center at 1-800-506-5596 with questions.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/wisconsin-earned-income-credit/">Wisconsin Earned Income Credit</a><p>Wisconsin&#39;s Earned Income Credit is a refundable credit for working families with at least one qualifying child, worth a percentage of your federal EITC: 4 percent with one child, 11 percent with two children, and 34 percent with three or more children. It is refundable, so it can pay out even if you owe no state tax. Claim it on your Wisconsin income tax return (Form 1 or 1NPR).</p></article><article><a href="/programs/wisconsin-home-energy-assistance-program-wheap/">Wisconsin Home Energy Assistance Program (WHEAP)</a><p>WHEAP is Wisconsin&#39;s LIHEAP program: help with heating and electric costs, plus emergency (crisis) assistance and furnace repair or replacement for income-eligible households. Apply online at energybenefit.wi.gov, or contact the local energy assistance agency serving your county or tribe. For more information, call 1-866-432-8947. The main heating benefit runs October 1 through May 15 each year.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/wisconsin-homestead-credit/">Wisconsin Homestead Credit</a><p>The Homestead Credit is a refundable Wisconsin credit that helps renters and homeowners with low income offset the property taxes or rent they pay. For 2025, household income must be under $24,680 to qualify (people with income at or above that must be disabled or age 62 or older). You claim it on Schedule H or H-EZ with your Wisconsin return, and it can come back as a refund even if you owe no tax.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/wisconsin-wic/">Wisconsin WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children up to age 5. Wisconsin WIC runs through local WIC agencies. Start your pre-application online, or call the Well Badger Resource Center at 1-800-642-7837 to find your local clinic, Monday through Friday, 7am to 6pm.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/wisconsin-works-w-2-cash-assistance/">Wisconsin Works (W-2 Cash Assistance)</a><p>Wisconsin Works, called W-2, is the state&#39;s TANF program: monthly cash assistance and employment services for parents of minor children and pregnant women with low income. The Department of Children and Families runs it through local W-2 agencies, and most participants take part in work activities. Apply online through ACCESS at access.wisconsin.gov or at your local W-2 agency.</p></article></section><section class="index-group" data-search="wyoming legal aid of wyoming legal aid of wyoming provides free civil legal help to low-income residents on noncriminal matters like eviction and housing, family law, public benefits, and consumer problems. call the statewide line at 1-877-432-9955, monday through friday, 9am to 4pm, or apply online at lawyoming.org/apply-online. offices serve the state from cheyenne, casper, lander, and gillette. unemployment insurance (wyoming) temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, run by the wyoming department of workforce services. file online through the wyui portal at wyui.wyo.gov, which is the fastest way, or reach the claims center at 307-473-3789, open 8am to 4pm monday, tuesday, thursday, and friday, and 8am to noon wednesday. weatherization assistance program (wyoming) wyoming&#39;s weatherization assistance program makes free energy-efficiency improvements that lower future heating bills: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system repairs. it shares one application with lieap, and the utility assistance portal takes weatherization applications year-round. call 1-800-246-4221 for help. homeowners and renters both qualify, including people in mobile homes. wyoming 211 dial 2-1-1 from any wyoming phone for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. wyoming 211 is a statewide referral service; give a short version of what you need and where you live, and a specialist searches for programs near you. you can also search at wyoming211.org. wyoming child care assistance child care assistance helps low-income wyoming parents pay for child care while they work or attend an employment-related education program. as of august 2025 you apply through ecares, the state&#39;s child care eligibility system. contact your local department of family services office or call 1-800-457-3659 to get started. if two parents are in the home, both must be in an approved activity during the same hours. wyoming kid care chip kid care chip is low-cost health coverage for children of working parents who earn too much for medicaid. it covers check-ups, immunizations, prescriptions, dental, vision, and mental health care, with preventive and dental care free and other services at a small co-pay. apply through the same wes portal at wesystem.wyo.gov, or call 1-855-294-2127. one application checks a child for both medicaid and chip. wyoming lieap (low income energy assistance program) lieap helps low-income wyoming households pay winter heating bills. wyoming takes applications online only, through the utility assistance portal, generally from october 1 through april 30 each heating season. for the current window and help applying, call 1-800-246-4221 or email lieapinfo@wyo.gov. approved lieap applicants are automatically considered for free weatherization work. wyoming medicaid wyoming medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents and caretaker relatives, and people who are aged, blind, or disabled. wyoming did not expand medicaid, so low-income adults without children generally do not qualify no matter how little they earn. apply online through the wes portal at wesystem.wyo.gov, or call 1-855-294-2127. if you are a child or a working parent over the limit, ask about kid care chip. wyoming power (cash assistance / tanf) power, personal opportunities with employment responsibilities, is wyoming&#39;s version of tanf: monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children, paired with a work program. there is also a caretaker relative track for relatives raising a child whose parents are absent. download the application for assistance and contact your local department of family services office to set up an appointment. for program questions call 307-473-3948. wyoming snap (food assistance) snap loads monthly food money onto an ebt card you use like a debit card at most wyoming grocery stores. wyoming still uses a paper application: download it, then submit it to your local department of family services office in person, by mail, by fax, or by email to snappowerservice@wyo.gov. for general snap questions call 307-473-3948, and for a scheduled interview call 1-307-777-8550. wyoming wic wic gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children under age 5. wyoming wic runs through local clinics of the wyoming department of health. start at health.wyo.gov/publichealth/wic, use the clinic locator to find your nearest office, or call the wic help desk at 1-307-777-6236."><h2>Wyoming programs (11)</h2><article><a href="/programs/legal-aid-of-wyoming/">Legal Aid of Wyoming</a><p>Legal Aid of Wyoming provides free civil legal help to low-income residents on noncriminal matters like eviction and housing, family law, public benefits, and consumer problems. Call the statewide line at 1-877-432-9955, Monday through Friday, 9am to 4pm, or apply online at lawyoming.org/apply-online. Offices serve the state from Cheyenne, Casper, Lander, and Gillette.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/unemployment-insurance-wyoming/">Unemployment Insurance (Wyoming)</a><p>Temporary weekly payments if you lost work through no fault of your own, run by the Wyoming Department of Workforce Services. File online through the WYUI portal at wyui.wyo.gov, which is the fastest way, or reach the Claims Center at 307-473-3789, open 8am to 4pm Monday, Tuesday, Thursday, and Friday, and 8am to noon Wednesday.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/weatherization-assistance-program-wyoming/">Weatherization Assistance Program (Wyoming)</a><p>Wyoming&#39;s Weatherization Assistance Program makes free energy-efficiency improvements that lower future heating bills: insulation, air sealing, and heating-system repairs. It shares one application with LIEAP, and the Utility Assistance portal takes weatherization applications year-round. Call 1-800-246-4221 for help. Homeowners and renters both qualify, including people in mobile homes.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/wyoming-211/">Wyoming 211</a><p>Dial 2-1-1 from any Wyoming phone for free, confidential help finding local assistance with housing, utilities, food, healthcare, and more. Wyoming 211 is a statewide referral service; give a short version of what you need and where you live, and a specialist searches for programs near you. You can also search at wyoming211.org.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/wyoming-child-care-assistance/">Wyoming Child Care Assistance</a><p>Child Care Assistance helps low-income Wyoming parents pay for child care while they work or attend an employment-related education program. As of August 2025 you apply through ECARES, the state&#39;s child care eligibility system. Contact your local Department of Family Services office or call 1-800-457-3659 to get started. If two parents are in the home, both must be in an approved activity during the same hours.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/wyoming-kid-care-chip/">Wyoming Kid Care CHIP</a><p>Kid Care CHIP is low-cost health coverage for children of working parents who earn too much for Medicaid. It covers check-ups, immunizations, prescriptions, dental, vision, and mental health care, with preventive and dental care free and other services at a small co-pay. Apply through the same WES portal at wesystem.wyo.gov, or call 1-855-294-2127. One application checks a child for both Medicaid and CHIP.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/wyoming-lieap-low-income-energy-assistance-program/">Wyoming LIEAP (Low Income Energy Assistance Program)</a><p>LIEAP helps low-income Wyoming households pay winter heating bills. Wyoming takes applications online only, through the Utility Assistance portal, generally from October 1 through April 30 each heating season. For the current window and help applying, call 1-800-246-4221 or email lieapinfo@wyo.gov. Approved LIEAP applicants are automatically considered for free weatherization work.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/wyoming-medicaid/">Wyoming Medicaid</a><p>Wyoming Medicaid is free or low-cost health coverage for children, pregnant people, parents and caretaker relatives, and people who are aged, blind, or disabled. Wyoming did not expand Medicaid, so low-income adults without children generally do not qualify no matter how little they earn. Apply online through the WES portal at wesystem.wyo.gov, or call 1-855-294-2127. If you are a child or a working parent over the limit, ask about Kid Care CHIP.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/wyoming-power-cash-assistance-tanf/">Wyoming POWER (Cash Assistance / TANF)</a><p>POWER, Personal Opportunities With Employment Responsibilities, is Wyoming&#39;s version of TANF: monthly cash assistance for low-income families with children, paired with a work program. There is also a Caretaker Relative track for relatives raising a child whose parents are absent. Download the Application for Assistance and contact your local Department of Family Services office to set up an appointment. For program questions call 307-473-3948.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/wyoming-snap-food-assistance/">Wyoming SNAP (Food Assistance)</a><p>SNAP loads monthly food money onto an EBT card you use like a debit card at most Wyoming grocery stores. Wyoming still uses a paper application: download it, then submit it to your local Department of Family Services office in person, by mail, by fax, or by email to snappowerservice@wyo.gov. For general SNAP questions call 307-473-3948, and for a scheduled interview call 1-307-777-8550.</p></article><article><a href="/programs/wyoming-wic/">Wyoming WIC</a><p>WIC gives healthy food, breastfeeding support, nutrition education, and health referrals to pregnant and postpartum people, infants, and children under age 5. Wyoming WIC runs through local clinics of the Wyoming Department of Health. Start at health.wyo.gov/publichealth/wic, use the clinic locator to find your nearest office, or call the WIC help desk at 1-307-777-6236.</p></article></section></div><div class="search-empty" data-empty="programs" hidden><h2>No matching programs</h2><p>Try a broader term or browse by state.</p></div></div></section></main><footer><div class="wrap footer-grid"><div><a class="brand" href="/" aria-label="American Aid Network home"><span class="brand-mark" aria-hidden="true"><span>★</span><i></i><i></i><i></i></span><span>AMERICAN <b>AID</b><small>NETWORK</small></span></a><p>Clear, reliable guidance to assistance programs across America.</p><div class="verified">✓ Information from trusted sources</div></div><div><h4>FIND SUPPORT</h4><a href="/find/">Find help near me</a><a href="/help/">Browse by state</a><a href="/programs/">Program guides</a><a href="/#how-it-works">How it works</a></div><div><h4>ABOUT</h4><a href="/about/">Our mission</a><a href="/legal/contact/">Contact us</a><a href="/legal/privacy/">Privacy policy</a><a href="/legal/terms/">Terms of use</a></div></div><div class="wrap disclaimer">American Aid Network is an independent informational resource. We are not a government agency and do not administer benefit programs. Program details change; always confirm requirements with the official provider.<span>© 2026 American Aid Network</span></div></footer><script src="/assets/site.js" defer></script></body></html>